			<img alt='PDF - <img src="../scripts/images/MPS05042016.jpg" width="42" />Monetary Policy Report – October 2026'  style='padding: 8px;' src='../Images/pdf.gif' width='16' height='16' alt='PDF document' border=0 align='absmiddle'></a> (<span id='SPDF_01MPR071020267B057FA4E71E4164A8B14EDC40197418' aria-hidden="true">7048 kb</span>)</td></tr><tr><td align="right" class="tableheader"><b> Date : Oct 07, 2026</b></td></tr><tr><td align="center" class="tableheader"><b><img src="../scripts/images/MPS05042016.jpg" width="42" />Monetary Policy Report – October 2026</b></td></tr><tr><td><table width="100%" border="0" align="center" class="td"><tr><td><table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">  <tr>    <td align="center" class="head">Contents</td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C1" class="links">Chapter I: External Environment</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C1_1" class="links">I.1: Global Economic Conditions</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C1_2" class="links">I.2: Commodity Prices and Inflation</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C1_3" class="links">I.3: Monetary Policy Stance</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C1_4" class="links">I.4: Financial Markets</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C1_5" class="links">I.5: Conclusion</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#B1_1" class="links">Box I.1: The Stock&ndash;Bond Correlation in Advanced and Emerging Economies</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C2" class="links">Chapter II: Liquidity Conditions and Financial Markets</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C2_1" class="links">II.1: Liquidity Conditions and the Operating Procedure of Monetary Policy</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C2_2" class="links">II.2: Domestic Financial Markets</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C2_3" class="links">II.3: Transmission to Lending and Deposit Rates</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C2_4" class="links">II.4: Conclusion</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#B2_1" class="links">Box: II.1: Drivers of CD and CP Rate Spreads</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#B2_2" class="links">Box: II.2: Bank Lending Channel of Monetary Policy: A Sectoral Approach</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C3" class="links">Chapter III: Demand and Output</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C3_1" class="links">III.1: Aggregate Demand</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C3_2" class="links">III.2: Aggregate Supply</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C3_3" class="links">III.3: Conclusion</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#B3_1" class="links">Box III.1: Indian Agricultural Sector amidst Weather Shocks</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C4" class="links">Chapter IV: Prices and Costs</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C4_1" class="links">IV.1: Introduction</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C4_2" class="links">IV.2: Developments across Major Components of the CPI</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C4_3" class="links">IV.3: Decoding the Inflation Dynamics</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C4_4" class="links">IV.4: Drivers of Inflation Trajectory</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C4_5" class="links">IV.5: Conclusion</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#B4_1" class="links">Box IV.1: Measuring Inflation Generalisation through the Diffusion Index</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#B4_2" class="links">Box IV.2: What Drives Inflation in India? Disentangling Demand and Supply Factors</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C5" class="links">Chapter V: Macroeconomic Outlook</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C5_1" class="links">V.1: Key Developments since the April 2026 MPR</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C5_2" class="links">V.2: Inflation Outlook</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C5_3" class="links">V.3: Growth Outlook</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C5_4" class="links">V.4: Balance of Risks</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#C5_5" class="links">V.5: Conclusion</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#B5_1" class="links">Box V.1: Crude Oil Price Shocks: Quantifying the Macroeconomic Impact on the Indian Economy</a></td>      </tr>  <tr>    <td><a href="#A" class="links">Abbreviations</a></td>      </tr>  </table><hr><p align="center" class="head">Executive Summary</p><p>Global growth remained resilient in 2026 so far, supported by AI-related investment, and fiscal stimulus measures, despite disruptions due to the ongoing West Asia conflict. Renewed energy-price pressures stoked inflation pressures and prompted a more hawkish stance from leading central bank. Fiscal sustainability concerns, persistent inflation, and geopolitical uncertainty raised bond yields, while equity markets remained volatile amid lingering concern on valuations technology stocks.</p><p>Amidst global uncertainty, domestic financial markets underwent intermittent volatility during H1:2026-27. System liquidity surplus surged in September 2026, mainly on account of the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s swap facilities. Overnight rates in the money market evolved in sync with prevailing liquidity conditions. Transmission to lending and deposit rates plateaued with status quo in policy rate during H1:2026-27. Bank credit growth became broad based and strengthened further.</p><p>The domestic economy showed resilience in H1:2026-27, despite continued global uncertainty amidst the conflict. The supply-side pressures were alleviated by proactive policy measures by the government that ensured adequate supply of key inputs. Real GDP recorded a growth of 7.8 per cent in Q1:2026-27 reflecting strong momentum in underlying economic activity. Aggregate demand remained strong on the back of both private consumption and investment. Net external demand was also robust and contributed significantly to growth. On the supply side, both manufacturing and services sectors recorded strong growth in Q1. High frequency indicators suggest continued buoyancy in domestic economic activity, albeit with some moderation compared to Q1. Stable employment conditions, congenial financial conditions, revival in corporate investment, and a pickup in goods exports &ndash; nurtured by bilateral FTAs and diversification &ndash; are expected to support economic activity going forward. Sustenance of private consumption growth will be essential for broad-based recovery in private capex.</p><p>Headline CPI inflation increased sequentially, rising above the target during June-August, driven by a sustained positive price momentum. Food and fuel groups led to this increase, reflecting adverse weather conditions and the sharp rise in energy prices following the West Asia conflict. Core inflation registered a pick-up with some signs of generalisation in recent months. Input cost pressures have accentuated, but the pass-through has remained limited so far.</p><p>Going forward, India&rsquo;s macroeconomic outlook remains resilient supported by domestic demand and strengthening investment, despite persistent global uncertainties. The outlook remains vulnerable to geopolitical developments, volatility in global energy markets and weather-related uncertainties. Weak monsoons coupled with strong El Ni&ntilde;o, protracted geopolitical tensions, rising international commodity prices, additional frictions in global trade and tightening of global financial conditions could adversely impact the growth outlook. Persistent supply-side pressures in food and volatile global energy prices pose risks to the domestic inflation outlook, particularly through second-round effects amid strong growth impulses. The interplay of these factors will shape the evolving growth&ndash;inflation dynamics, with the balance of risks depending on developments in global growth, crude oil prices, exchange-rate movements, and domestic food supplies.</p><hr><p align="center" class="head"><a id="C1"></a>I. External Environment</p><p><em>Global growth remains resilient, mainly supported by AI-related investment and fiscal measures, but faces renewed supply-side shocks from the West Asia conflict, elevated oil prices and trade related frictions. Inflation has accelerated again, prompting central banks to shift towards a more cautious and hawkish monetary policy stance. Financial markets remain volatile amid higher yields, geopolitical tensions and shifting risk sentiment.</em></p><p>Global growth has remained resilient despite facing persistent headwinds from supply shocks. However, the near-term growth forecasts have been revised downwards by several multilateral agencies, as key supply chains remain disrupted and crude oil prices remain elevated. Investment in artificial intelligence (AI) and related technologies, along with increased international trade in AI-related hardware, has supported global economic activity. Fiscal support across countries has sustained economic activity but also heightened concerns about rising public debt and reduced fiscal buffers. Along with higher inflation and higher-for-longer monetary policy expectations, these concerns have contributed to a sharp rise in government bond yields across major jurisdictions.</p><p>Inflation, which had been moderating towards the end of 2025, began to accelerate following the supply shock triggered by the West Asia conflict. Crude oil prices rose to levels not seen in recent years. At the same time, other commodity prices also increased, feeding into higher goods inflation even as services inflation remained elevated in several major economies. Consequently, central banks that had been moving towards monetary policy normalisation last year adopted a more hawkish stance following the onset of the conflict. Several advanced and emerging market economies (EMEs) have tightened monetary policy or maintained a cautious stance, while only a handful have reduced policy rates to support growth. Of late, the US Fed and the European Central Bank (ECB) have been placing greater emphasis on incoming data and their monetary policy frameworks in decision making, rather than on forward guidance. The Fed&rsquo;s hawkish stance, combined with elevated crude oil prices, contributed to risk-off sentiment and portfolio outflows from EMEs, exerting depreciation pressure on their currencies. Sovereign bond yields have hardened globally, particularly in advanced economies (AEs), on concerns over rising public debt, debt-servicing costs and persistent inflation concerns. Emerging market equities experienced selloffs during Q3 of 2026, with some recovery in August while advanced economy equities gained, driven by technology stocks and healthy corporate profits. The US dollar gained, as higher bond yields, expectations of a hawkish Fed, and safe-haven demand amid geopolitical uncertainty outweighed intermittent weakness.</p><p class="head"><a id="C1_1"></a>I.1 Global Economic Conditions</p><p>In 2026 so far, global growth has remained resilient, even as near-term growth forecasts have been revised downwards amid the fragile geopolitical situation in West Asia. The growth outlook is dependent on the de-escalation of the conflict, as key supply chains continue to face disruptions, and oil prices remain elevated. In its July 2026 World Economic Outlook update, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) lowered its global growth projection for 2026 by 10 bps to 3.0 per cent, reflecting the uncertainty around the outlook. However, it raised its projection by 20 bps for 2027 to 3.4 per cent, as the impact of the conflict remains less than what was anticipated earlier, while AI-related investment and productivity gains are likely to fuel growth. Meanwhile, the OECD, in its September 2026 Interim Economic Outlook, marginally raised its global growth forecast by 10 bps to 2.9 per cent for 2026, while lowering its 2027 projection by 10 bps to 3.0 per cent. While AI-related activity is expected to provide continued support, stronger price pressures and elevated interest rates are likely to weigh on near-term growth momentum.</p><p>Among AEs, real GDP growth in the US remained resilient in 2026, supported by robust consumer spending and investment, including continued private investment in AI and related technologies. Growth, however, moderated in the second quarter, reflecting a downturn in government spending, slower investment and export growth, while stronger consumer spending partly offset these effects (<a href="#T1_1" class="links">Table I.1</a>). Since April 2026, labour market conditions have softened, with non-farm payroll growth slowing down during summer and weakening further in September, while the unemployment rate remained largely stable. The composite PMI continued to expand strongly in August, recording a 52-month high, driven by services while manufacturing remained healthy amid improved business optimism and new orders. Consumer sentiment, as measured by the University of Michigan Survey, declined in September to its lowest level in four months, amid growing concerns about elevated prices. Overall, underlying economic activity in the US remains resilient.</p><p>Among other AEs, growth in Japan in 2026 so far has been largely driven by the external sector. GDP growth strengthened in Q1, with net exports making the largest contribution. Growth moderated in Q2 due to slowdown in domestic demand, particularly private investment, though this moderation was offset largely by a strong contribution from net exports. The composite PMI continued to expand in August, driven by manufacturing while services also improved. On the other hand, GDP growth recovered in the euro area in Q2 after stagnating in Q1, primarily driven by strong growth in Ireland and Spain. Household and government expenditure continued to provide support to economic activity. The composite PMI expanded in August at the same pace for the second consecutive month as the moderation in services was offset by solid expansion in manufacturing amid growth in new orders and factory output. In the UK, GDP growth rebounded in Q1 2026, supported by services and broad-based expansion across the economy, with gross capital formation and household consumption driving growth on the expenditure side. Growth moderated in Q2, reflecting weaker household consumption and lower inventory accumulation amid the adverse effects of the conflict in West Asia. Among high-frequency indicators, the composite PMI expanded in August for the second consecutive month, driven by services sector activity offsetting the moderation in manufacturing.</p><table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">  <tr>    <td colspan="8" align="center" class="head"><a id="T1_1"></a>Table I.1: Real GDP Growth</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="8" align="right">(Per cent)</td>  </tr>  <tr class="head">    <td width="30%" align="center">Country</td>    <td width="10%" align="center">Q3-2025</td>    <td width="10%" align="center">Q4-2025</td>    <td width="10%" align="center">Q1-2026</td>    <td width="10%" align="center">Q2-2026</td>    <td width="10%" align="center">2025</td>    <td width="10%" align="center">2026 (P)</td>    <td width="10%" align="center">2027 (P)</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="8">Quarter-on-quarter, seasonally adjusted, annualised rate (q-o-q, saar)</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Canada</td>    <td align="right">1.9</td>    <td align="right">-1.0</td>    <td align="right">0.3</td>    <td align="right">3.3</td>    <td align="right">1.9</td>    <td align="right">1.1</td>    <td align="right">1.7</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Euro area</td>    <td align="right">1.3</td>    <td align="right">0.9</td>    <td align="right">0.0</td>    <td align="right">2.6</td>    <td align="right">1.4</td>    <td align="right">0.9</td>    <td align="right">1.2</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Japan</td>    <td align="right">-1.5</td>    <td align="right">1.1</td>    