"right">2.8</td>    <td align="right">2.3</td>    <td align="right">4.4</td>    <td align="right">3.9</td>    <td align="right">5.5</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Russia</td>    <td align="right">0.8</td>    <td align="right">1.0</td>    <td align="right">-0.2</td>    <td align="right">1.3</td>    <td align="right">1.0</td>    <td align="right">1.1</td>    <td align="right">1.1</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>South Africa</td>    <td align="right">2.1</td>    <td align="right">0.8</td>    <td align="right">1.9</td>    <td align="right">0.9</td>    <td align="right">1.1</td>    <td align="right">1.1</td>    <td align="right">1.3</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Thailand</td>    <td align="right">1.2</td>    <td align="right">2.5</td>    <td align="right">2.8</td>    <td align="right">1.9</td>    <td align="right">2.4</td>    <td align="right">1.9</td>    <td align="right">2.2</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="8">Memo:</td>  </tr>  <tr class="head">    <td colspan="2" align="center">World</td>    <td colspan="2" align="center">2025</td>    <td colspan="2" align="center">2026 (P)</td>    <td colspan="2" align="center">2027 (P)</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="8">Year-on-year</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="2">Output</td>    <td colspan="2" align="right">3.5</td>    <td colspan="2" align="right">3.0</td>    <td colspan="2" align="right">3.4</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="2">Trade volume</td>    <td colspan="2" align="right">5.0</td>    <td colspan="2" align="right">3.5</td>    <td colspan="2" align="right">4.3</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="8">P: Projection.<br>      Notes: 1. India's data correspond to fiscal year (April-March); e.g., 2026 pertains to April 2026-March 2027.<br>      2. Projectons for 2026 and 2027 are taken from the IMF WEO, July 2026 update.<br>      Sources: Official statistical agencies; Bloomberg; and IMF.</td>  </tr></table><p>Among major EMEs, China&rsquo;s growth is facing challenges from subdued consumption and a weaker external sector in 2026, with GDP growth moderating in Q2 from Q1. The composition of growth has also undergone a shift, with the share of net exports in GDP growth declining significantly relative to 2025, while the share of gross capital formation increased. Investment growth remains uneven, with stronger activity concentrated in AI-related and high-tech sectors. High-frequency indicators also point to subdued activity, with composite PMI slowing in July as both manufacturing and services sector activity moderated. In August, however, PMI rebounded as output and new orders increased in both the sectors but remained below the average for 2026.</p><p>Brazil&rsquo;s GDP growth remained stable at 1.8 per cent (y-o-y) in Q1, marking the third consecutive quarter of growth at this pace. The agriculture and services sectors supported growth while industrial activity declined. In Q2, however, GDP expanded by 2.0 per cent, driven by recovery in industrial sector and sustained growth in agriculture. Industrial sector benefited from higher oil and gas extraction amid higher energy prices. Brazil&rsquo;s composite PMI remained very close to the neutral line in the last few months, reflecting weakness in the economy as new orders continue to fall. Russia&rsquo;s economy contracted in Q1, reflecting weak manufacturing and construction activity, although services remained resilient. Activity recovered in Q2, led by manufacturing, while agriculture remained subdued. The composite PMI also returned to expansion in August, ending five consecutive months of contraction, supported by stronger output and new orders. In South Africa, GDP growth accelerated in Q1, driven by a recovery in agriculture, increased mining activity, and stronger momentum in services, before moderating in Q2 led by a contraction in manufacturing and mining. Composite PMI continued to remain around neutral line in August amidst subdued new orders and weak business confidence.</p><p>The Asian Development Bank (ADB), in its September 2026 outlook, projected ASEAN<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) includes Brunei Darussalam, Cambodia, Indonesia, Lao PDR, Malaysia, Myanmar, Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and Vietnam."><a href="#C1FN1" class="links">1</a></sup> growth at 4.7 per cent in both 2026 and 2027. The outlook remains subject to downside risks from the West Asia conflict, elevated energy and commodity prices, weaker external demand and heightened global uncertainty.</p><p>Turning to high-frequency indicators, the OECD&rsquo;s composite leading indicators (CLIs) showed that most economies remained above their long-term trend in Q2 and Q3 (till August) of 2026 (<a href="#CH1_1" class="links">Chart I.1a</a>). The global composite PMI expanded at its quickest pace in August in more than two years, with broad-based expansion in both output and new orders (<a href="#CH1_1" class="links">Chart I.1b</a>). The services sector continued to expand in August for the fifth straight month, while the manufacturing sector remained solid.</p><a id="CH1_1"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C11.jpg" alt="Chart I.1: Survey Indicators" title="Chart I.1: Survey Indicators"></div>      <p>World merchandise trade expanded for eleven consecutive quarters through Q2:2026, with growth remaining robust and exceeding expectations. Both EMEs and AEs contributed to the expansion, though the impulse came largely from EMEs, with moderation in contribution by the AEs as frontloading of US imports moderated. In July 2026, world merchandise trade volume growth remained strong at 6.0 per cent (y-o-y), with EMEs contributing 3.2 percentage points and AEs 2.8 percentage points (<a href="#CH1_2" class="links">Chart I.2a</a>). The surge in trade of electronic components related to AI offset the negative impact of the conflict in West Asia. The WTO, in its March 2026 update, revised its projection for global merchandise trade volume growth from 0.5 per cent to 1.9 per cent in 2026, reflecting strong AI-related trade and a smaller-than-anticipated impact of tariffs. The Goods Trade Barometer (September 2026, WTO) remained above trend at 102.0, with the electronic components and other goods linked to investment in artificial intelligence (AI) indices indicating continued momentum in merchandise trade. Meanwhile, shipping costs remained elevated, with the Freightos Baltic Global Index staying higher in 2026 - above its 2025 average - reflecting the rerouting of vessels away from disrupted shipping corridors/chokepoints and continued capacity pressures (<a href="#CH1_2" class="links">Chart I.2b</a>).</p>      <p class="head"><a id="C1_2"></a>I.2 Commodity Prices and Inflation</p>      <p>Commodity prices, as captured by the Bloomberg Commodity Index, have exhibited divergent movements, largely attributable to sector-specific fundamentals (<a href="#CH1_3" class="links">Chart I.3a</a>). Agricultural commodity prices have risen due to conflict-related disruptions to energy, fertiliser and shipping markets, adverse weather conditions and tighter crop-supply prospects. Global food prices, as reflected in the Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) food price index, have increased since April 2026, mainly due to vegetable oils, cereals, and sugar (<a href="#CH1_3" class="links">Chart I.3b</a>).</p>      <p>Gold prices declined through Q2 2026 amid expectations of higher interest rates. Gold prices gained overall in Q3 as heightened geopolitical uncertainty, including renewed tensions in West Asia, boosted safe-haven demand. Periods of weakness in the US dollar and lower expectations of further tightening by the Fed also supported prices, outweighing the drag from rising bond yields and the stronger dollar in September. Among base metals, copper and zinc prices have continued to rise, supported by tightening supply conditions. Aluminium prices rose to a four-year high in early June amid concerns over supply disruptions from the West Asia conflict, before moderating as the US dollar strengthened and Chinese exports increased (<a href="#CH1_3" class="links">Chart I.3c</a>).</p>      <a id="CH1_2"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C12.jpg" alt="Chart I.2: World Trade Volume" title="Chart I.2: World Trade Volume"></div>      <br>    <a id="CH1_3"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C13.jpg" alt="Chart I.3: Commodity Prices" title="Chart I.3: Commodity Prices"></div>    <p>Energy prices surged in April, largely reflecting the sharp increase in Brent prices, before moderating as supply disruptions eased. However, the re-escalation of tensions in West Asia led to a renewed increase in energy prices in August and September, amid concerns over disruptions in regional oil production and shipping (<a href="#CH1_4" class="links">Chart I.4a</a>). Against this backdrop, developments in global oil supply buffers and demand conditions remain important. The US Strategic Petroleum Reserve (SPR) is currently placed at roughly 284 million barrels, at its lowest level since October 1982. Strategic releases and inventory drawdowns helped to cushion shocks. Also, weaker import demand by China due to release of stocks and domestic demand destruction helped offset some supply pressures. However, if the disruptions last longer, there is lower flexibility available to deal with supply shortages (<a href="#CH1_4" class="links">Chart I.4 b&c</a>).</p>    <p class="head">Consumer Price Inflation</p>    <p>Inflationary pressures have re-emerged following a temporary moderation, driven primarily by renewed energy and commodity price pressures amid heightened West Asia tensions.</p>    <a id="CH1_4"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C14.jpg" alt="Chart I.4: Crude Prices and Demand - Supply Conditions" title="Chart I.4: Crude Prices and Demand - Supply Conditions"></div>    <p>The US personal consumption expenditures (PCE) price index, the Federal Reserve&rsquo;s preferred inflation metric, saw inflation surge through May, driven by a sharp increase in gasoline prices before falling subsequently as energy prices stabilised. In the UK, headline inflation has been lower in Q2 2026, mainly due to easing food inflation, before edging up in Q3 so far on rising energy and housing prices. In the euro area, energy prices and persistently high service inflation drove the CPI inflation higher, except for a modest correction in June due to a reversal in crude prices. In Japan, inflation rose steadily from April, reflecting a slower decline in energy prices as subsidies were scaled back in Q2, alongside imported inflation from yen depreciation. Inflation rose further in Q3, on elevated food prices and transportation costs, despite lower electricity prices.</p>    <p>In EMEs, inflation has been divergent, reflecting differences in domestic demand conditions and supply-side pressures across economies. In Brazil, the headline CPI rose due to food and beverages, then fell as food, transport, and housing prices moderated. Inflation in South Africa accelerated from April to June, driven predominantly by higher fuel and transport costs, before easing in July as fuel prices declined and food-price inflation moderated. In Russia, CPI inflation eased until May on easing food pressures before edging higher due to fuel and a weaker rouble. China&rsquo;s CPI inflation has remained subdued, supported by persistent food-price deflation (<a href="#CH1_5" class="links">Chart I.5b</a>).</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C1_3"></a>I.3 Monetary Policy Stance</p>        <p>Since April 2026, monetary policy actions have diverged across economies. However, major advanced economy central banks turned more hawkish in Q3 2026, as renewed West Asia tensions raised energy prices and intensified inflationary pressures.</p>        <p>Among major AEs, the US Fed held the target range at 3.50&ndash;3.75 per cent through July. In September, amidst persistent inflationary pressures, robust economic activity and stable labour-market conditions, the Fed unanimously raised the target range by 25 basis points to 3.75&ndash;4.00 per cent. The Bank of England has maintained the policy rate unchanged at 3.75 per cent in the last six meetings, noting little evidence so far of material second-round effects from the energy shocks while reiterating that future policy decisions depend on how the energy shock evolves and its impact on inflation.</p>        <a id="CH1_5"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C15.jpg" alt="Chart I.5: CPI Inflation (y-o-y) &ndash; Select Economies" title="Chart I.5: CPI Inflation (y-o-y) &ndash; Select Economies"></div>        <p>The ECB raised its key policy rates by 25 basis points in June and again in September, taking the deposit facility rate to 2.50 per cent. The decisions reflected persistent energy-driven inflation pressures associated with the conflict; however, indirect and second-round effects have remained largely contained. The Bank of Japan (BoJ) continued policy normalisation, raising its policy rate by 25 basis points each in June and September to 1.25 per cent. The moves reflected persistent inflationary pressures, owing to higher import costs amid yen depreciation, with future policy decisions to be guided by developments in economic activity and prices.</p>        <p>The Reserve Bank of Australia raised rates in May amid elevated inflation and capacity pressures, and again in September as fuel-price pass-through and stronger-than-expected growth intensified inflation risks. The Bank of Canada maintained status quo as longer-term inflation expectations have remained anchored and there was little evidence that oil prices had fed through more broadly to goods and services prices. The Reserve Bank of New Zealand hiked the policy rate by 25 bps for the first time in three years in July and then again in September by the same magnitude to 2.75 per cent. The monetary policy committee considered gradual withdrawal of monetary stimulus as appropriate to steer inflation to the 2 per cent target, while supporting growth and employment (<a href="#CH1_6" class="links">Chart I.6a</a>).</p>        <p>Among key EMEs, Brazil continued its monetary easing cycle, with the central bank reducing the Selic rate in successive meetings amid signs of moderating economic activity and easing inflationary pressures. The Bank of Russia has gradually reduced its key rate as economic growth edged lower. China has held its benchmark rate at 3.0 per cent for more than a year amid ample liquidity. South Africa raised its policy rate by 25 basis points to 7.25 per cent in September, following a similar increase in May, amid rising inflation risks and concerns over potential second-round effects. The Bank of Thailand kept its policy rate unchanged, balancing weaker growth and subdued domestic demand against conflict-related, supply-driven inflation pressures. The Bank of Mexico cut its policy rate by 25 basis points to 6.5 per cent in May, marking the end of its easing cycle. In September, it kept rates unchanged but removed its earlier forward guidance, signalling greater policy flexibility (<a href="#CH1_6" class="links">Chart I.6b</a>).</p>        <a id="CH1_6"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C16.jpg" alt="Chart I.6: Policy Rate Changes &ndash; Select Major Economies" title="Chart I.6: Policy Rate Changes &ndash; Select Major Economies"></div>    <p class="head"><a id="C1_4"></a>I.4 Financial Markets</p>    <p>Global financial markets remained largely resilient yet volatile, guided by the interplay of AI-driven risk appetite, shifting monetary policy expectations, elevated sovereign borrowing needs, and persistent geopolitical tensions in West Asia. The stock-bond correlation in the AEs has shifted for the first time in decades, reflecting uncertainty due to supply-side shocks (<a href="#B1_1" class="links">Box I.1</a>).</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td><p align="center" class="head"><a id="B1_1"></a>Box I.1: Stock&ndash;Bond Correlation in Advanced and Emerging Economies</p>            <p>The equity&ndash;government bond return correlation is central to multi-asset portfolio diversification. Stock&ndash;bond return correlation is influenced by short-term rates, inflation, volatility, and economic growth (Aslanidis and Christiansen, 2012). Theoretical explanations link asset-price co-movement to macroeconomic conditions (Campbell et al., 2020), while changes in stock&ndash;bond correlation have important implications for portfolio diversification (Brixton et al., 2023). More recently, evidence from G7 economies suggests that the macroeconomic drivers of stock&ndash;bond correlation vary over time, with significant implications for portfolio risk and diversification (McMillan, 2026). After two decades of generally positive readings, the correlation in advanced economies turned sharply negative during the 2021&ndash;22 inflation surge and again in 2025 (<a href="#CH1_1ab" class="links">Chart I.1a</a>). Using a DCC-GARCH model, the analysis finds that stock&ndash;bond correlation in advanced economies follows an inflation-regime relationship. Inflation enters with a negative linear and positive quadratic term, implying that correlation is lowest near the inflation target and rises as inflation moves away from it (<a href="#CH1_1ab" class="links">Chart I.1b</a>). The implied turning point is 1.6 per cent, essentially at the 2 per cent target, and the real short rate is also positive and significant. The findings suggest that when inflation is moderate, government bonds tend to hedge equity-market risk.</p>            <a id="CH1_1ab"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C11ab.jpg" alt="Chart I.1 A and Chart I.1 B" title="Chart I.1 A and Chart I.1 B"></div>            <p>This mechanism, however, does not extend to emerging markets. Their correlation averages around +0.6 and never turns negative over the sample, while its relationship with domestic inflation is weak and statistically insignificant. A formal Wald test also rejects equality of the advanced- and emerging-market coefficients. Instead, global equity volatility (VIX) is the only significant explanatory variable. The results suggest that emerging-market government bonds behave more like risk assets than domestic safe havens, selling off alongside equities when global risk appetite deteriorates. Thus, their diversification benefits are not evident in the post-2008 sample, unlike in advanced economies.</p>            <p class="head">References:</p>            <p>1. Aslanidis, N., & Christiansen, C. &ldquo;Smooth Transition Patterns in the Realized Stock&ndash;Bond Correlation&rdquo;. Journal of Empirical Finance, 19(4), 454&ndash;464, 2012.</p>            <p>2. Brixton, A., Brooks, J., Hecht, P., Ilmanen, A., Maloney, T., & McQuinn, N. &ldquo;A Changing Stock&ndash;Bond Correlation: Drivers and Implications&rdquo;. The Journal of Portfolio Management, 49(4), 64&ndash;80, 2023.</p>            <p>3. Campbell, J. Y., Pflueger, C., & Viceira, L. M. &ldquo;Macroeconomic Drivers of Bond and Equity Risks&rdquo;. Journal of Political Economy, 128(8), 3148&ndash;3185, 2020.</p>            <p>4. McMillan, D. G. &ldquo;Stock-Bond Return Correlation: Understanding the Changing Behaviour&rdquo;. Journal of International Financial Markets, Institutions and Money, 106, Article 102242, 2026.</p></td>          </tr>      </table><p>Global equities advanced in Q2 2026, driven by continued momentum in artificial intelligence (AI) and related capital investment cycles (<a href="#CH1_7" class="links">Chart I.7a</a>). Geopolitical tensions in West Asia added sporadic volatility during the quarter. The announcement of a US&ndash;Iran ceasefire framework and a memorandum of understanding (MoU) eased concerns about a broader regional conflict. Energy prices recoiled, and investor sentiment improved as fears of a sustained disruption to global energy supplies diminished. During Q2, US equities advanced, supported by resilient corporate earnings and sustained investor enthusiasm for AI-related companies. In the euro area, equities posted solid gains, with IT outperforming amid strong corporate earnings and diversification by investors from US markets. In the UK, equity prices rose, although declines in energy stocks limited overall gains. Markets largely looked through domestic political developments, with investor sentiment remaining broadly resilient. Japanese equities delivered robust returns in Q2 as easing tensions in West Asia buoyed sentiment, while the yen &ndash; at a four-decade low against the US dollar &ndash; supported exporters. In Q3, major equity markets posted modest gains, although investor sentiment remained cautious amid expectations of further monetary tightening, elevated technology-sector valuations, and persisting tensions in West Asia. Equities gained in the US, supported by resilient corporate earnings and sustained AI optimism, with further support from the Fed&rsquo;s commitment to combating inflation despite its hawkish stance. Euro-area equities declined in Q3 as higher energy prices and persistent inflation lifted bond yields and weighed on risk sentiment, while political uncertainty and fiscal concerns, added to investor caution. Japanese equities were affected by the global correction in AI-related stocks and also concerns surrounding a stronger yen which could weigh on exports. In the UK, equities made modest gains, supported by robust corporate earnings, and elevated commodity prices. Equity markets in most EMEs rose sharply in Q2:2026, outperforming global peers. Taiwan and Korea led market performance, driven by gains in memory and semiconductor stocks amid strong AI demand (<a href="#CH1_7" class="links">Chart I.7b</a>). In Q3, emerging-market equities surrendered the gains recorded in the previous quarter as the AI-led rally showed signs of fatigue. Renewed conflict in West Asia, heightened trade tensions, and expectations of a more hawkish Fed weighed on investor risk appetite.</p><a id="CH1_7"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C17.jpg" alt="Chart I.7: Global Equities" title="Chart I.7: Global Equities"></div>    <p>A global sell-off in government debt led by inflation persistence and high government deficits has raised borrowing costs. These pressures have been exacerbated by the West Asia conflict, which has driven up oil prices, leading investors to seek higher bond yields as the odds of rate cuts have largely diminished. Sovereign bond-market performance diverged in Q2: Japanese and US rates firmed up, while German and UK yields softened. Developments in West Asia continued to be a major driver of the market, with bond yields moving in tandem with oil prices. Yields on government bonds surged to multi-year highs around the middle of the quarter amid fears of further escalation but fell on signs of a possible US&ndash;Iran deal.</p>    <p>In the US, rates were underpinned by a resilient labour market and elevated government debt, reinforcing expectations that monetary policy would remain tight. A combination of global inflation concerns, and domestic politics hit UK gilts. In April, the 10-year gilt yield hit its highest level since 2008, reflecting inflationary pressures from higher oil prices and concerns about budgetary and political vulnerabilities ahead of municipal elections. Higher oil costs and imported-inflation pressures from the yen supported expectations for BoJ policy hikes this year, while concerns about fiscal expansion contributed to upside pressure. In Q3, government bond yields in major AEs have risen amid persistent energy-price pressures and tighter monetary policy, while elevated public debt and renewed fiscal support have added to upward pressure on long-term yields. In the US, higher oil prices, persistent inflation concerns, and higher borrowing requirements pushed treasury yields higher. The Treasury&rsquo;s decision in mid-August to expand buybacks of long-dated securities provided only temporary relief, as underlying inflation and term-premium pressures continued to weigh on the market. Elsewhere, government bond yields in the euro area, Japan and the UK moved up amid renewed inflation concerns from rising energy prices, while rate hikes and firm policy signals, along with country-specific fiscal risks and elevated sovereign borrowing requirements, added to the rise in yields (<a href="#CH1_8" class="links">Chart I.8 a</a>).</p>    <p>Emerging-market 10-year sovereign bonds recorded a mixed performance, displaying divergent country-specific inflation, fiscal, and monetary policy dynamics. While favourable risk sentiment and easing expectations initially supported some markets, the subsequent rise in oil prices, US sovereign bond yields, and geopolitical tensions tightened external financing conditions, increasing borrowing costs and weighing more heavily on sovereigns vulnerable to fiscal and external risks (<a href="#CH1_8" class="links">Chart I.8b</a>).</p>    <p>In currency markets, the US dollar strengthened in Q2 2026, supported by higher oil prices, heightened geopolitical tensions and still elevated inflation, which reinforced safe-haven demand and expectations of relatively tighter monetary policy. Most major currencies weakened against the US dollar, with the yen declining to a four-decade low despite large scale coordinated intervention by the US and the Bank of Japan. The euro also depreciated, while sterling demonstrated greater resilience. In Q3, the US dollar, which had remained broadly weak through much of the quarter, strengthened markedly in September. The rebound was driven by higher US bond yields and a repricing of expectations for further Fed tightening following the release of data suggesting resilient activity and persistent inflation concerns. Renewed geopolitical tensions in West Asia, elevated oil prices, and trade-related uncertainty also supported safe-haven demand for the dollar. Consequently, the dollar index rose by around 2.0 per cent during September to a near two-month high, although it ended Q3 only marginally higher. Against this backdrop, the renewed negative correlation between the S&P 500 and the US dollar since April indicates that the dollar has again provided a partial hedge during periods of equity-market stress, appreciating when risk sentiment deteriorated and weakening as risk appetite recovered (<a href="#CH1_9" class="links">Chart I.9</a>). Shifts in monetary policy expectations, geopolitical developments, and global risk appetite can, however, quickly alter the stock&ndash;dollar correlation.</p>    <a id="CH1_8"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C18.jpg" alt="Chart I.8: 10-Year Sovereign Bond Yields" title="Chart I.8: 10-Year Sovereign Bond Yields"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH1_9"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C19.jpg" alt="Chart I.9: S&P 500 and US Dollar Correlation" title="Chart I.9: S&P 500 and US Dollar Correlation"></div>    <p>The MSCI EM Currency Index rose driven by carry-seeking flows, supportive commodity prices and country-specific inflows. However, gains moderated late in the period amid renewed pressure from rising long-term US bond yields (<a href="#CH1_10" class="links">Chart I.10 a & b</a>).</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C1_5"></a>I.5 Conclusion</p>    <p>The global economic outlook continues to be shaped by supply-side shocks emanating from the West Asia crisis, shifting trade tariffs, and the ongoing AI investment boom. Despite multilateral agencies projecting slower global growth in 2026, high-frequency indicators, including purchasing managers&rsquo; indices, point to resilient economic activity. Inflation has picked up in most economies, prompting central banks to adopt a more hawkish stance. Long-term bond yields have risen amid persistent inflation concerns, while equity markets have advanced, underpinned by the ongoing AI-driven investment boom. Elevated inflation, coupled with heightened risks to growth outlook, warrants a cautious approach from the monetary authorities going ahead.</p>    <a id="CH1_10"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C110.jpg" alt="Chart I.10: Currency Movements and Capital Flows" title="Chart I.10: Currency Movements and Capital Flows"></div>    <p>_________________________________________________________________________________</p>    <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C1FN1"></a>1</sup> The Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) includes Brunei Darussalam, Cambodia, Indonesia, Lao PDR, Malaysia, Myanmar, Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and Vietnam.</p><hr>    <p align="center" class="head"><a id="C2"></a>II. Liquidity Conditions and Financial Markets</p>    <p><em>System liquidity surplus surged during H1:2026-27 mainly on account of the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s swap facilities. Overnight rates in the money market evolved in sync with prevailing liquidity conditions. Domestic financial markets exhibited two-way movements amidst global uncertainty. Transmission to lending rates moderated with status quo on policy rate since February 2026. Bank credit growth accelerated further. Sectoral trends indicate broad-based credit expansion.</em></p>    <p class="head">Introduction</p>    <p>Global financial markets remained volatile amidst shifting expectations, persistent tensions in West Asia and rising fiscal concerns. Against this backdrop, domestic financial markets displayed intermittent volatility during H1:2026-27. System-wide liquidity surplus surged in September 2026 reflecting buoyant capital flows under the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s swap facilities. Money market rates largely moved in line with the evolving liquidity conditions, with rates in Certificates of Deposit (CDs) and Commercial Papers (CPs) easing in Q2. The exchange rate exhibited two-way movements in H1. Monetary policy transmission to lending rate moderated during H1 amidst rate pause and funding pressure. Nonetheless, bank credit continued to grow robustly and financing from non-bank sources also increased.</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C2_1"></a>II.1 Liquidity Conditions and the Operating Procedure of Monetary Policy</p>    <p>The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) Act, 1934 requires the Reserve Bank to place the operating procedure relating to the implementation of monetary policy and changes thereto from time to time, if any, in the public domain.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The revised liquidity management framework was operationalised on September 30, 2025. The salient features of the framework are given in the Monetary Policy Report of October 2025."><a href="#C2FN1" class="links">1</a></sup> During H1:2026-27, the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) kept the policy rate unchanged in three successive meetings while retaining the neutral policy stance.</p>    <p class="head">Drivers and Management of Liquidity</p>    <p>System liquidity, as measured by the net balances under the liquidity adjustment facility (LAF), improved significantly in H1:2026&ndash;27 from a moderate surplus in H2:2025&ndash;26 (<a href="#CH2_1" class="links">Chart II.1</a>). On a net basis, average daily absorption under the LAF increased to &#8377;3.21 lakh crore in H1:2026&ndash;27 from &#8377;1.43 lakh crore in H2:2025&ndash;26. Capital inflows and the build-up of government cash balances were the principal drivers of liquidity conditions in H1:2026&ndash;27.</p>    <p>System liquidity remained in surplus during H1:2026-27 except for end-June when it briefly turned into deficit amidst tax related outflows.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="In H1:2026-27, system liquidity, as measured by balances under the LAF, moved into deficit during June 22 to June 29 on GST and advance tax related outflows."><a href="#C2FN2" class="links">2</a></sup> In Q1, liquidity conditions improved initially supported by faster government spending, but it moderated thereafter due to increase in currency in circulation (CiC) and the RBI&rsquo;s foreign exchange operations. Liquidity conditions improved in Q2, aided by capital inflows under the RBI's swap facilities (<a href="#T2_1" class="links">Table II.1</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_1"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C21.jpg" alt="Chart II.1: Liquidity Operations" title="Chart II.1: Liquidity Operations"></div>    <p>With liquidity conditions remaining largely in surplus, banks&rsquo; recourse to the marginal standing facility (MSF) averaged &#8377;0.01 lakh crore during H1:2026-27, while average daily standing deposit facility (SDF) balances increased marginally<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Daily balances under the SDF averaged ?2.23 lakh crore during H1:2026-27 compared to ?2.20 lakh crore during H2:2025-26."><a href="#C2FN3" class="links">3</a></sup>. The Reserve Bank remained nimble and agile in its liquidity management operations, ensuring sufficient liquidity in the banking system to support transmission to money market. With surge in liquidity surplus mainly due to FCNR(B) swaps<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="To attract foreign capital, the Reserve Bank announced a series of measures on June 5, 2026, including a forex swap facility bearing the full hedging costto Authorised Dealer banks for raising fresh 3–5-year FCNR(B) deposits."><a href="#C2FN4" class="links">4</a></sup>, the RBI conducted variable rate reverse repo (VRRR) operations of varying maturities, although several variable rate repo (VRR) operations were also undertaken mainly until July 2026 to tide over transient liquidity tightness and align overnight rates with the policy rate. Overall, the RBI conducted a total of 36 VRR and 51 VRRR auctions in H1. On a review of current and evolving liquidity conditions, the Reserve Bank also conducted OMO sale auctions of Government of India securities for an aggregate amount of &#8377;1 lakh crore.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="It was conducted in three tranches of ?50,000 crore, ?25,000 crore and ?25,000 crore on September 17, September 21 and September 28, 2026, respectively."><a href="#C2FN5" class="links">5</a></sup></p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="6" align="center" class="head"><a id="T2_1"></a>Table II.1: Liquidity - Key Drivers and Management</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="6" align="right">(&#8377; crore)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="50%" rowspan="2">&nbsp;</td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">2026-27</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">2026-27</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">2026-27</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td align="center"><span class="head">H1</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">H2</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">Q1</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">Q2*</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">H1*</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Drivers</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(i) CiC [withdrawal (-) /return (+)]</td>        <td align="right">-74,052</td>        <td align="right">-3,67,073</td>        <td align="right">-1,29,065</td>        <td align="right">-8,019</td>        <td align="right">-1,37,084</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(ii) Net Forex Purchases (+)/ Sales (-)</td>        <td align="right">-1,41,591</td>        <td align="right">-3,27,863</td>        <td align="right">-1,36,182</td>        <td align="right">9,12,616</td>        <td align="right">7,76,434</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(iii) GoI Cash Balances [build-up (-) / drawdown (+)]</td>        <td align="right">-3,44,103</td>        <td align="right">2,15,110</td>        <td align="right">-1,80,676</td>        <td align="right">-25,018</td>        <td align="right">-2,05,694</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(iv) Excess Reserves [build-up (-) / drawdown (+)]</td>        <td align="right">14,376</td>        <td align="right">-5,849</td>        <td align="right">-8,646</td>        <td align="right">3,345</td>        <td align="right">-5,301</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Management</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>i) Net OMO Purchases (+)/ Sales (-)</td>        <td align="right">2,39,223</td>        <td align="right">6,38,344</td>        <td align="right">10</td>        <td align="right">-</td>        <td align="right">10</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(ii) Required Reserves [including both change in NDTL and CRR]</td>        <td align="right">15,674</td>        <td align="right">1,38,046</td>        <td align="right">-25,807</td>        <td align="right">-20,920</td>        <td align="right">-46,727</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(iii) Term VRR Auctions</td>        <td align="right">25,731</td>        <td align="right">1,36,504</td>        <td align="right">-</td>        <td align="right">-</td>        <td align="right">-</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(iv) Term VRRR Auctions</td>        <td align="right">-</td>        <td align="right">-</td>        <td align="right">-</td>        <td align="right">3,19,725</td>        <td align="right">3,19,725</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Memo Items</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(i) Long term Forex Swaps Buy/Sell (+)/ Sell/Buy (-)^</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">2,26,885</td>        <td align="right">47,800</td>        <td align="right">-</td>        <td align="right">47,800</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(ii) Net Absorption (+)/ Injection (-) under LAF as at end-period</td>        <td align="right">56,274</td>        <td align="right">3,22,439</td>        <td align="right">1,23,964</td>        <td align="right">9,88,822</td>        <td align="right">9,88,822</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="6">Notes: 1. (+) / (-) sign suggests accretion to / depletion of banking system liquidity.<br>          2. Data pertain to the last reporting day of the respective period.<br>          3. *: Data for Q2 and H1:2026-27 are up to September 15, 2026.<br>          4. ^: approximate values.<br>          5. - : Nil.<br>          Source: RBI.</td>          </tr>      </table><p>As on September 15, 2026, reserve money expanded by 8.5 per cent (y-o-y) as against 5.6 per cent a year ago. Adjusted for the first-round impact of CRR change, growth in reserve money stood at 11.9 per cent (9.4 per cent a year ago). The higher growth in reserve money was driven by expansion in currency in circulation. As on September 15, 2026, growth (y-o-y) in money supply (M3) accelerated to 16.6 per cent from 9.2 per cent a year ago owing to faster growth in both aggregate deposits and currency with the public. The money multiplier increased to 6.3 as on September 15, 2026, from 5.8 a year ago, propelled by a declining currency-deposit ratio.</p><p class="head"><a id="C2_2"></a>II.2 Domestic Financial Markets</p><p>Money market rates softened in H1:2026-27 in tandem with evolving liquidity conditions. G-sec yields eased until August before hardening in September, reflecting higher global yields, rising oil prices, and expectations of monetary tightening. Corporate bond yields moved in sync with G-sec yields, with a hardening bias, while risk premia showed mixed trends. Equity markets gained marginally despite heightened volatility in global financial markets and crude oil prices due to West Asia conflict. The foreign exchange market remained volatile amid shifting US policies and lingering conflict in West Asia. In the credit market, both bank and non-bank credit grew at a robust pace.</p><p class="head">II.2.1 Money Market</p><p>During H1:2026-27, money market rates softened and remained mostly range bound amid large surplus liquidity conditioned by robust capital inflows, especially from FCNR(B) deposits under the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s swap facility announced in June 2026. The weighted average call rate (WACR) &ndash; the operating target of monetary policy &ndash; generally remained within the policy rate corridor. However, with the significant rise in surplus liquidity, it moved downward towards the SDF rate, floor of the corridor, in September (<a href="#CH2_2" class="links">Chart II.2a</a>).</p><p>Volatility in the WACR, as measured by the exponential weighted moving average (EWMA)<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="EWMA is an improvement over simple variance as it assigns greater weights to more recent observations. EWMA expresses volatility as a weighted average of past volatility with higher weights assigned to more recent observations."><a href="#C2FN6" class="links">6</a></sup>, declined considerably in the beginning of FY 2026-27, however, it increased marginally in July, and remained moderate till September (<a href="#CH2_2" class="links">Chart II.2b</a>). Reflecting the evolving liquidity conditions, the average spread of WACR over the policy repo rate turned positive during May-July 2026, before turning negative in August and plunging further in September (<a href="#CH2_2" class="links">Chart II.2b</a>). Overnight rates in the collateralised segment, i.e., triparty repo (TREPS) and market repo, broadly tracked the WACR, although both fell below the SDF rate intermittently during H1:2026-27 (<a href="#CH2_3" class="links">Chart II.3</a>).</p><a id="CH2_2"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C22.jpg" alt="Chart II.2: Policy Corridor and WACR" title="Chart II.2: Policy Corridor and WACR"></div><p>Money market activity remained concentrated in the collateralised segments (tri-party and market repo), with their share in overnight money market volume at 97 per cent. During H1:2026-27, the share of the uncollateralised segment, i.e., the call money market, persisted around 3 per cent. Mutual funds remained the major lenders in tri-party repo with their share unchanged at 65 per cent in H1:2026-27, while their share in total lending in market repo segment moderated to 41 per cent in H1 from 48 per cent in H2:2025-26. The share of foreign banks in market repo lending increased to 31 per cent in H1:2026-27 from 29 per cent in H2:2025-26. On the borrowing side, public sector banks (PSBs) were the major player in tri-party repo, with their share increasing to 35 per cent in H1:2026-27 from 32 per cent in H2:2025-26. The share of private banks reduced noticeably to 14 per cent from 24 per cent. The share of PSBs in market repo increased by 5 percentage points to 13 per cent over the same period.</p><p>The rates in term segments of the money market also moved in line with the evolving liquidity conditions and uncertainty (also see <a href="#B2_1" class="links">Box II.1</a>). While 3-month treasury bill (T-Bill) rates moved largely in line with the policy repo rate, the rates on certificates of deposits (CDs) and commercial papers (CPs) edged up in May, but moderated since July notwithstanding intermittent spikes. The average spread of CDs and CPs over the policy repo rate widened to 135 bps and 155 bps in H1:2026-27 from 116 bps and 136 bps, respectively, in H2:2025-26. The average spread of T-Bills over the policy repo rate increased to 5 bps from 1 bp over the same period (<a href="#CH2_3" class="links">Chart II.3</a>).</p><a id="CH2_3"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C23.jpg" alt="Chart II.3: Money Market Rates and Policy Corridor" title="Chart II.3: Money Market Rates and Policy Corridor"></div><br><table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">  <tr>    <td><p align="center" class="head"><a id="B2_1"></a>Box II.1: Drivers of CD and CP Rate Spreads</p>      <p>Rates on short-term instruments like Certificates of Deposit (CDs) and Commercial Papers (CPs) generally move in tandem with the policy repo rate but diverge at times reflecting broader liquidity conditions and funding pressures. Starting from Q3 of 2025-26, CD/CP rates hardened and remained elevated even as the monetary policy easing cycle continued. It resulted in widening of spread of CD/CP rates over the policy repo rate. Besides liquidity conditions and funding pressures, several other factors can cause CP and CD spreads to deviate from their long-run averages, including, policy uncertainty, monetary policy expectations and market-specific factors such as issuance patterns, calendar effects, and episodes of market stress (Priyadarshini et al., 2024, Anshul et al., 2025). To disentangle the respective contribution of various factors which are intertwined and may operate simultaneously, uncorrelated shocks are identified using historical decompositions within a VAR framework using monthly data. The following VAR specification is used.</p>      <div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_F1.jpg"></div><p>Where Y<sub>t</sub> includes the selected endogenous variables, X<sub>t</sub> contains the exogenous dummies and P&epsilon;<sub>t</sub> are the orthogonalised shocks.</p>      <p>The variables are selected to capture the main channels, viz., banking system liquidity, funding conditions and policy uncertainty through which they can affect short-term funding costs, while avoiding overlap across closely related measures.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Variables used for the analysis: India Economic Policy Uncertainty Index, EPU (Baker et al., 2016), system liquidity (average net balances under the liquidity adjustment facility as a per cent of NDTL), the change in the outstanding credit-deposit ratio is used as a measure for funding pressure (for CD model only), percentage change in monthly outstanding issuance as a measure of net new supply, and the spread between the 3 month CD/CP rate minus the policy repo rate as the spread variable."><a href="#C2FN7" class="links">7</a></sup> For CDs, the funding block also captures banks&rsquo; balance-sheet pressures through changes in outstanding credit-deposit ratio and net CD issuance growth. For CPs, only net issuance growth is used to capture funding conditions. The uncorrelated shocks are identified using a recursive identification scheme where the most exogenous factors are placed at the top while the response variable, i.e., CD or CP spreads are placed at the end of the ordering. Historical decomposition for the period January 2025 &ndash; July 2026 show the extent of contribution by each identified factor to the movements in spreads (<a href="#CH2_1_1" class="links">Chart II.1.1</a>).</p>      <a id="CH2_1_1"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C21ab.jpg" alt="Chart II.1.1: Historical Decomposition of CD and CP Spreads over Repo Rate" title="Chart II.1.1: Historical Decomposition of CD and CP Spreads over Repo Rate"></div>      <p>The decomposition results suggest that the drivers of CD and CP spreads have shifted over time. Relatively tight liquidity conditions were the main driver of widening spreads during early 2025, but increased system liquidity along with policy rate easing led spreads to moderate to relatively lower levels by mid-2025. Since late 2025, the spreads hardened again. Funding pressures, owing to stronger credit growth relative to deposit mobilisation, contributed to hardening of CD spread, while economic policy uncertainty was the main driver of widening CP spread. More recently, idiosyncratic market-specific pressures have also contributed to the widening which are unrelated to the identified shocks in the model.</p>      <p class="head">References</p>      <p>Anshul, Priyadarshini, P. and Chaudhari D. R. (2025), &ldquo;Drivers of CD Issuances: An Empirical Assessment&rdquo;. RBI Bulletin. June.</p>      <p>Baker, S. R., Bloom, N., & Davis, S. J. (2016), &ldquo;Measuring Economic Policy Uncertainty&rdquo;, The Quarterly Journal of Economics, 131(4), 1593-1636.</p>      <p>Priyadarshini, P., Anshul, S. S., Chaudhari, D. R., & Das, S. (2024), &ldquo;Drivers of Commercial Paper Rate Spread-An Empirical Assessment&rdquo;, RBI Working Paper Series No. 02.</p></td>  </tr></table><p>Fresh issuances of CDs reduced considerably in H1:2026-27 compared to H2:2025-26, mainly due to mobilisation of deposits under FCNR(B) scheme and the resulting liquidity surplus (<a href="#T2_2" class="links">Table II.2</a>). The share of CD issuances of shorter tenor (up to 91-day) decreased while that of longer tenor increased in Q2 compared to Q1 of 2026-27.</p><p>In contrast to the CD market, CP issuances were robust during H1:2026-27, as rates moderated on the back of large surplus liquidity which incentivised corporates and NBFCs to raise cheaper short-term credit (<a href="#CH2_4" class="links">Chart II.4a</a>; <a href="#T2_3" class="links">Table II.3</a>). The money market risk premia (spread of 3-month CP rate over 91-day T-bill rate) moderated, tracking the reduction in uncertainty in H1:2026-27 (<a href="#CH2_4" class="links">Chart II.4b</a>).</p><a id="CH2_4"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C24.jpg" alt="Chart II.4: Primary Issuances of Commercial Paper" title="Chart II.4: Primary Issuances of Commercial Paper"></div><br>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="6" align="center" class="head"><a id="T2_2"></a>Table II.2: Tenor wise Break up of CD Issuances</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="6" align="right">(&#8377; lakh crore)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="35%" rowspan="2">&nbsp;</td>        <td colspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26</span></td>        <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head">2026-27</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="13%" align="center"><span class="head">H1</span></td>        <td width="13%" align="center"><span class="head">H2</span></td>        <td width="13%" align="center"><span class="head">Q1</span></td>        <td width="13%" align="center"><span class="head">Q2</span></td>        <td width="13%" align="center"><span class="head">H1</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Up to 91 Days</td>        <td align="right">3.55</td>        <td align="right">4.40</td>        <td align="right">3.00</td>        <td align="right">2.15</td>        <td align="right">5.14</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">(73)</td>        <td align="right">(51)</td>        <td align="right">(92)</td>        <td align="right">(81)</td>        <td align="right">(87)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>92-180 Days</td>        <td align="right">0.47</td>        <td align="right">0.61</td>        <td align="right">0.11</td>        <td align="right">0.25</td>        <td align="right">0.36</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">(10)</td>        <td align="right">(7)</td>        <td align="right">(3)</td>        <td align="right">(10)</td>        <td align="right">(6)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>181-365 Days</td>        <td align="right">0.84</td>        <td align="right">3.58</td>        <td align="right">0.17</td>        <td align="right">0.24</td>        <td align="right">0.42</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">(17)</td>        <td align="right">(42)</td>        <td align="right">(5)</td>        <td align="right">(9)</td>        <td align="right">(7)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Total</td>        <td align="right">4.86</td>        <td align="right">8.59</td>        <td align="right">3.28</td>        <td align="right">2.65</td>        <td align="right">5.93</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">(100)</td>        <td align="right">(100)</td>        <td align="right">(100)</td>        <td align="right">(100)</td>        <td align="right">(100)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="6">Notes: 1. Figures in parentheses denote share of each maturity profile.<br>          2. Figures may not add up to total due to rounding off.<br>          Sources: Clearing Corporation of India Limited F-TRAC; and RBI staff estimates.</td>          </tr>      </table><p>Corporates dominated CP issuances with their share increasing to 39 per cent in H1:2026-27 from 32 per cent in H2:2025-26 (<a href="#CH2_5" class="links">Chart II.5</a>). In terms of maturity profile, the 91-180 days segment had the largest share (51 per cent) in fresh issuances followed by the 31-90 days segment (<a href="#T2_3" class="links">Table II.3</a>).</p><p class="head">II.2.2 Government Securities Market</p><p>Government securities (G-sec) yields experienced periodic bouts of volatility during H1:2026-27 due to challenging global environment and rise in global bond yields (<a href="#CH2_6" class="links">Chart II.6</a>). In the wake of the West Asia conflict and a sharp rise in crude oil prices, G-sec yields remained elevated during April-May but softened generally in June-July as tensions in West Asia abated temporarily. Yields softened across the term structure during June-July 2026 as FPI debt flows improved after the announcement of special measures, including the expansion of the universe of Fully Accessible Route (FAR) securities by the Reserve Bank and tax concessions by the Government (<a href="#CH2_7" class="links">Chart II.7a</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_5"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C25.jpg" alt="Chart II.5: Issuer Profile of Commercial Papers" title="Chart II.5: Issuer Profile of Commercial Papers"></div><br>        <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">          <tr>            <td colspan="4" align="center" class="head"><a id="T2_3"></a>Table II.3: Maturity Profile of CP Issuances</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td colspan="4" align="right">(&#8377; lakh crore)</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td width="40%" align="center" class="head">Tenor</td>            <td width="20%" align="center"><span class="head">H1: 2025-26</span></td>            <td width="20%" align="center"><span class="head">H2: 2025-26</span></td>            <td width="20%" align="center"><span class="head">H1: 2026-27</span></td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>7- 30 days</td>            <td align="right">0.43</td>            <td align="right">0.66</td>            <td align="right">1.01</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>&nbsp;</td>            <td align="right">(5)</td>            <td align="right">(8)</td>            <td align="right">(9)</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>31-90 days</td>            <td align="right">3.10</td>            <td align="right">1.87</td>            <td align="right">3.80</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>&nbsp;</td>            <td align="right">(35)</td>            <td align="right">(24)</td>            <td align="right">(34)</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>91-180 days</td>            <td align="right">4.56</td>            <td align="right">4.24</td>            <td align="right">5.67</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>&nbsp;</td>            <td align="right">(51)</td>            <td align="right">(53)</td>            <td align="right">(51)</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>181-365 days</td>            <td align="right">0.86</td>            <td align="right">1.16</td>            <td align="right">0.60</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>&nbsp;</td>            <td align="right">(10)</td>            <td align="right">(15)</td>            <td align="right">(5)</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>Total</td>            <td align="right">8.95</td>            <td align="right">7.93</td>            <td align="right">11.09</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>&nbsp;</td>            <td align="right">(100)</td>            <td align="right">(100)</td>            <td align="right">(100)</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>Outstanding (as at end-period)</td>            <td align="right">4.89</td>            <td align="right">4.60</td>            <td align="right">6.22</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td colspan="4">Notes: 1. Figures in parentheses denote share of each maturity profile.<br>              2. Figures may not add up to total due to rounding off.<br>              Sources: Clearing Corporation of India Limited F-TRAC; RBI staff estimates.</td>          </tr>        </table>        <br><a id="CH2_6"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C26.jpg" alt="Chart II.6: Indian G-sec Yields and Global Crude Oil Prices" title="Chart II.6: Indian G-sec Yields and Global Crude Oil Prices"></div>    <p>After remaining range-bound in early August, the yields hardened again in September, owing to the rise in advanced economy yields, higher domestic inflation print and upward pressure on Brent crude oil prices following renewed West Asia tensions. Overall, the level of yield curve remained elevated during H1 (<a href="#CH2_7" class="links">Chart II.7b</a>).</p>    <p>The average trading volume in G-secs increased in H1:2026-27 relative to H2:2025-26 (<a href="#CH2_8" class="links">Chart II.8</a>). The weighted average yield on traded maturities for G-secs rose by 29 bps during the same period.</p>    <p>As part of active debt consolidation, the Reserve Bank conducted six switch auctions of G-secs amounting to &#8377;1.1 lakh crore during H1:2026-27, on behalf of the Government of India. Furthermore, against the backdrop of an improved cash position, the Reserve Bank accepted offers totalling &#8377;25,704 crore through buyback auctions. The weighted average maturity (WAM) of the outstanding stock of G-secs increased from 13.70 years at end-March 2026 to 13.76 years as at end-September 2026, while its weighted average coupon (WAC) moderated from 7.17 per cent to 7.15 per cent over the same period.</p>    <a id="CH2_7"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C27.jpg" alt="Chart II.7: FPI flows and Yield Curve" title="Chart II.7: FPI flows and Yield Curve"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH2_8"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C28.jpg" alt="Chart II.8: G-sec Trading Volume and Yield" title="Chart II.8: G-sec Trading Volume and Yield"></div>    <p>The weighted average spread of cut-off yields on State Government Securities (SGS) over G-sec yields of comparable maturities was 51 bps in H1:2026-27 as against 57 bps in H2:2025-26 (<a href="#CH2_9" class="links">Chart II.9</a>). The average inter-state spread of cut-off yields on SGS of 10-year tenor (fresh issuances) was 6 bps in H1:2026-27 as against 12 bps in H2:2025-26.</p>    <p class="head">II.2.3 Corporate Bond Market</p>    <p>Corporate bond yields evolved in sync with G-sec yields with a hardening bias during H1:2026-27, while the risk premia showed mixed trends (<a href="#CH2_10" class="links">Chart II.10a</a>). The average bond market risk premium (i.e., the spread of 3-year AAA corporate bond yields over 3-year G-sec yields) hardened from 129 bps to 140 bps for NBFCs, and from 112 bps to 122 bps for corporates, but remained stable at 109 bps for Public Sector Units (PSUs), Financial Institutions (FIs) and banks in H1, even as corporate earnings remained robust in Q1 (<a href="#CH2_10" class="links">Chart II.10b</a> and <a href="#T2_4" class="links">Table II.4</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_9"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C29.jpg" alt="Chart II.9: SGS - Amount Raised and Spread" title="Chart II.9: SGS - Amount Raised and Spread"></div>    <p>Primary issuances of listed corporate bonds in domestic markets declined by 17.9 per cent during H1:2026-27 (up to August 2026) as compared to the corresponding period of the previous year reflecting higher yields (<a href="#CH2_11" class="links">Chart II.11a</a>). Overseas issuances increased to &#8377;1,31,931 crore during H1:2026-27 (up to August 2026) from &#8377;10,303 crore during the same period last year, partly attributed to RBI&rsquo;s swap facility. Most of the resource mobilisation in the corporate bond market (99 per cent) was through the private placement route. Outstanding investments by foreign portfolio investors (FPIs) in corporate bonds stood at &#8377;2.7 lakh crore as at end-September, 2026 with utilisation of investment limits at 29.3 per cent (<a href="#CH2_11" class="links">Chart II.11b</a>). Trading volume in the secondary market moderated to &#8377;9.5 lakh crore during H1 (up to August 2026) from &#8377;10.1 lakh crore during the corresponding period of the previous year.</p>    <a id="CH2_10"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C210.jpg" alt="Chart II.10: AAA-rated 3-Year Corporate Bond Yield and Spread" title="Chart II.10: AAA-rated 3-Year Corporate Bond Yield and Spread"></div>    <p class="head">II.2.4 Equity Market</p>    <p>During H1:2026-27, Indian equity markets gained despite heightened volatility in global financial markets and crude oil prices due to conflict in West Asia. Markets rebounded in April 2026 following the announcement of the US-Iran ceasefire. However, equity market remained subdued in May and early June as persistent tensions in West Asia, elevated crude oil prices and the IMD&rsquo;s forecast of below normal monsoon weighed on investor sentiment. Thereafter, markets gained reflecting resilient corporate earnings results and a rebound in net buying by FPIs, even as the disruption of the US-Iran ceasefire reversed some gains in early July. Equity prices declined towards the end of August and early September amidst lingering geopolitical uncertainties, rising crude oil prices and higher global bond yields.</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="7" align="center"><a id="T2_4"></a><span class="head">Table II.4: Financial Markets &ndash; Rates and Spread</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="28%" align="center">&nbsp;</td>        <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head">Interest Rates<br>            (Per cent)</span></td>        <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head">Spread (bps)<br>            (Over corresponding risk-free rate)</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td align="center"><span class="head">Instruments</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">September 2025</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">March 2026</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">September 2026</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">September 2025</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">March 2026</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">September 2026</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td align="center"><span class="head">1</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">2</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">3</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">4</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">5</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">6</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">7</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Corporate Bonds</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>        <td>&nbsp;</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(i) AAA (1-yr)</td>        <td align="right">6.69</td>        <td align="right">7.40</td>        <td align="right">7.53</td>        <td align="right">100</td>        <td align="right">171</td>        <td align="right">148</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(ii) AAA (3-yr)</td>        <td align="right">7.10</td>        <td align="right">7.36</td>        <td align="right">7.85</td>        <td align="right">105</td>        <td align="right">112</td>        <td align="right">122</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(iii) AAA (5-yr)</td>        <td align="right">7.20</td>        <td align="right">7.52</td>        <td align="right">7.81</td>        <td align="right">86</td>        <td align="right">92</td>        <td align="right">96</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(iv) AA (3-yr)</td>        <td align="right">8.20</td>        <td align="right">8.22</td>        <td align="right">8.63</td>        <td align="right">215</td>        <td align="right">198</td>        <td align="right">200</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>(v) BBB-minus (3-yr)</td>        <td align="right">11.88</td>        <td align="right">11.92</td>        <td align="right">12.23</td>        <td align="right">583</td>        <td align="right">567</td>        <td align="right">561</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="7">Note: Yields and spreads are computed as monthly averages.<br>          Source: FIMMDA.</td>          </tr>      </table>    <br>    <a id="CH2_11"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C211.jpg" alt="Chart II.11: Corporate Bond Market Activity" title="Chart II.11: Corporate Bond Market Activity"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH2_12"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C212.jpg" alt="Chart II.12: Benchmark and Broad Indices Performance" title="Chart II.12: Benchmark and Broad Indices Performance"></div>    <p>Overall, broader market indices outperformed the benchmark during H1:2026-27 (<a href="#CH2_12" class="links">Chart II.12</a>). However, the price-to-earnings (PE) ratio diverged across market segments. The PE ratio for the benchmark BSE Sensex moderated marginally to 19.2 at end-September 2026 from 19.8 at end-March 2026, while the PE ratio for the MidCap index declined to 29.3 from 31.1 during this period. In contrast, the PE ratio for the SmallCap index rose sharply to 34.6 at end-September 2026 from 26.3 at end-March 2026. The India Volatility Index, a measure of short-term expected volatility of Nifty 50, reduced to 13.5 from 27.9 during this period. Most of the sectors gained in H1.</p>    <p>Foreign portfolio investors (FPIs) remained net sellers in H1:2026-27. Domestic institutional investors (DIIs), especially mutual funds, acted as a counterbalancing force with net buying (<a href="#CH2_13" class="links">Chart II.13</a>), which is partly responsible for PE ratios remaining elevated. Resource mobilisation in primary equity markets stood at &#8377;2.6 lakh crore during H1 (up to August 2026), higher than &#8377;1.8 lakh crore during the corresponding period a year ago (<a href="#CH2_14" class="links">Chart II.14</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_13"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C213.jpg" alt="Chart II.13: Net Investment in Indian Equities by Institutional Investors" title="Chart II.13: Net Investment in Indian Equities by Institutional Investors"></div>    <p class="head">II.2.5 Foreign Exchange Market</p>    <p>The foreign exchange market remained volatile during H1:2026-27, driven by shifting US policies on tariff and policy rate, and lingering conflict in West Asia. While the rupee initially depreciated in the aftermath of the West Asia conflict, policy measures announced by the Reserve Bank and the Government to bolster capital flows arrested its decline. The implied volatility cooled down after peaking to a multi-year high in the wake of the West Asia conflict (<a href="#CH2_15" class="links">Chart II.15</a>).</p>    <p>Overall, the rupee depreciated by 1.4 per cent against the US dollar in H1:2026-27, a marked moderation from the sharp depreciation of 6.2 per cent witnessed in H2:2025-26 (<a href="#CH2_16" class="links">Chart II.16</a>). The relative standing of the rupee among EM currencies also improved even as the pressures from volatile global market, elevated crude oil prices and the prolonged West Asia conflict weighed on the currency.</p>    <a id="CH2_14"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C214.jpg" alt="Chart II.14: Resource Mobilisation through Primary Market" title="Chart II.14: Resource Mobilisation through Primary Market"></div>    <p>Forward premia moderated during H1:2026-27 from the peak seen in the first week of April, supported by measures undertaken by the Government and the Reserve Bank to attract foreign capital. Upward pressures persisted, however, reflecting volatility in crude oil prices and flare-up of tensions in West Asia conflict (<a href="#CH2_17" class="links">Chart II.17</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_15"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C215.jpg" alt="Chart II.15: Exchange Rate and Volatility" title="Chart II.15: Exchange Rate and Volatility"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH2_16"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C216.jpg" alt="Chart II.16: Movements in Major EM Currencies (End-September over end-March 2026)" title="Chart II.16: Movements in Major EM Currencies (End-September over end-March 2026)"></div>    <p>In terms of the 40-currency real effective exchange rate (REER), the INR depreciated by 0.5 per cent between March and August 2026, driven by the depreciation of the rupee in nominal terms (<a href="#CH2_18" class="links">Chart II.18a</a>). During the period, the movement in REER index was modest, as compared to major peer emerging economies (<a href="#CH2_18" class="links">Chart II.18b</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_17"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C217.jpg" alt="Chart II.17: Movements in INR-USD Forward Premia" title="Chart II.17: Movements in INR-USD Forward Premia"></div>    <p class="head">II.2.6 Bank and Non-Bank Credit</p>    <p>Bank Credit: Aggregate Trends</p>    <p>Bank credit growth strengthened further during H1: 2026&ndash;27 (up to September 15), on the back of robust loan demand (<a href="#CH2_19" class="links">Chart II.19</a>). As on September 15, 2026, credit growth (y-o-y) for scheduled commercial banks (SCBs) accelerated sharply to 18.1 per cent, from 10.4 per cent during the corresponding period in the previous year (<a href="#CH2_20" class="links">Chart II.20</a>). Across bank groups, foreign banks recorded the highest credit expansion at 25.8 per cent y-o-y, outperforming private banks (PVBs) at 19.1 per cent and public sector banks (PSBs) at 16.9 per cent. While PSBs contributed the largest share to y-o-y incremental credit, their overall market share contracted due to aggressive lending by both PVBs and foreign banks.</p>    <a id="CH2_18"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C218.jpg" alt="Chart II.18: Trends in Real Effective Exchange Rate" title="Chart II.18: Trends in Real Effective Exchange Rate"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH2_19"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C219.jpg" alt="Chart II.19: Credit-Deposit Gap" title="Chart II.19: Credit-Deposit Gap"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH2_20"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C220.jpg" alt="Chart II.20: Credit flow across Bank Groups-Growth" title="Chart II.20: Credit flow across Bank Groups-Growth"></div>    <p>The asset quality of SCBs strengthened further in Q1:2026-27 with the overall gross non-performing assets (NPA) ratio declining to 1.8 per cent in June 2026 from 2.3 per cent a year ago (<a href="#CH2_21" class="links">Chart II.21a</a>). The improvement spanned all major sectors, except for services and retail loans, where asset quality remained unchanged (<a href="#CH2_21" class="links">Chart II.21b</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_21"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C221.jpg" alt="Chart II.21: Stressed Assets and Non-Performing Assets of SCBs" title="Chart II.21: Stressed Assets and Non-Performing Assets of SCBs"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH2_22"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C222.jpg" alt="Chart II.22: Non-SLR Investment and Adjusted Non-food Bank Credit" title="Chart II.22: Non-SLR Investment and Adjusted Non-food Bank Credit"></div>    <p>Banks&rsquo; non statutory liquidity ratio (non-SLR) investments (comprising CPs, bonds, debentures, and shares of public and private corporates) increased by around 2.9 per cent during H1:2026-27 (up to September 15), with an increase in investments in commercial papers due to higher returns (<a href="#CH2_22" class="links">Chart II.22a</a>). Growth in adjusted non-food bank credit (i.e., non-food bank credit plus banks&rsquo; non-SLR investments) accelerated to 17.6 per cent (y-o-y) in Q2:2026-27 (up to September 15) from 10.1 per cent in Q2:2025-26, (<a href="#CH2_22" class="links">Chart II.22b</a>).</p>    <p>As on August 31, 2026, excess holdings of SLR securities by SCBs marginally moderated to 6.3 per cent of their net demand and time liabilities (NDTL) from 6.4 per cent at end-March 2026 as banks brought down their investment portfolio to fund credit demand (<a href="#CH2_23" class="links">Chart II.23</a>).</p>    <p>Bank Credit: A Sectoral Perspective</p>    <p>Sectoral trends<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Bank credit data are based on fortnightly Section-42 return, which covers all scheduled commercial banks (SCBs), while sectoral non-food credit data are based on sector-wise and industry-wise bank credit (SIBC) return, which covers select banks accounting for about 95 per cent of total non-food credit extended by all SCBs, pertaining to the last day of the month. With effect from December 31, 2025, definition of last reporting fortnight has been changed to the last day of the month under the Banking Laws (Amendment) Act 2025. Accordingly, the y-o-y growth rates from December 2025 onwards are based on end-of-month data for the current year and data for the last reporting fortnight (as per old definition) for the corresponding month of the previous year."><a href="#C2FN8" class="links">8</a></sup>,<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="H1:2026-27 data up to August 2026."><a href="#C2FN9" class="links">9</a></sup> indicate strengthening of credit growth across major segments. Sustained robust economic activity continued to underpin credit demand, supporting broad-based expansion in bank credit. Monetary policy was found to have heterogenous impact on sectoral credit growth (<a href="#B2_2" class="links">Box II.2</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_23"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C223.jpg" alt="Chart II.23: Excess SLR of Banks" title="Chart II.23: Excess SLR of Banks"></div>    <br>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>  <td><p align="center" class="head"><a id="B2_2"></a>Box II.2: Bank Lending Channel of Monetary Policy: A Sectoral Approach</p>      <p>Indian financial system continues to be bank-dominated. Bank credit as a proportion to GDP surged from 52.8 per cent in 2011-12 to 59.8 per cent in 2025-26. Therefore, given the importance of the banking sector, it becomes pertinent to understand the effectiveness of the transmission of monetary policy through the lending channel to various sectors of the economy. Under the current policy easing cycle, bank credit has inched up considerably across sectors. Demand conditions for credit have also been strong on the back of persisting buoyancy in domestic economic activity. In this milieu, it would be important to empirically decipher the response of sectoral bank credit to monetary policy shocks.</p>      <p>Against this backdrop, the dynamic response of overall bank credit and credit to major sectors over 24 months following an unexpected policy rate change is examined using panel local projection technique (Jord&agrave;, 2005). Policy surprises are extracted using data on Overnight Index Swap (OIS) rates around RBI policy announcement dates, after controlling for current macroeconomic conditions (Lakdawala and Sengupta, 2025). Using monthly bank-level panel data of 40 banks for the period March 2014 - March 2026, the baseline specification for each horizon h = 0, 1, ..., 24 is given as under:</p>      <div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_F2.jpg"></div>      <p>Where, y<sub>i,t</sub> represents credit growth for bank i in time t, y<sub>i,t+h</sub> &ndash; y<sub>i,t&ndash;1</sub> is the dependent variable representing the cumulative credit growth for bank i from the pre-shock period to horizon h, &alpha;<sub>i,h</sub> are bank fixed effects at horizon h, Shock<sub>t</sub> is the exogenous monetary policy (target factor) shock at time t, and &beta;<sub>h</sub> is the coefficient of interest, capturing the cumulative impulse response of bank credit to a one standard deviation monetary policy shock at horizon h. Plotting &beta;<sub>h</sub> for h = 0, 1, ..., 24 traces the impulse response function.</p>      <p>Shock<sub>t&ndash;j</sub> are lagged shocks controlling for prior monetary policy actions, &Delta;y<sub>i,t&ndash;j</sub> controls for lagged values of the dependent variable, credit growth, X<sub>i,t&ndash;1</sub> is a vector of lagged bank characteristics (size, soundness, profitability, credit to deposits and borrowing ratio and asset quality) that can affect credit growth, MC<sub>t&ndash;p</sub> are lagged macroeconomic controls (nominal GDP growth and inflation) and D<sub>t</sub> and C<sub>t</sub> are dummies for demonetisation and COVID-19 pandemic, respectively. &epsilon;<sub>i,t+h</sub> is the idiosyncratic error term clustered at the bank level. The lag length (p) is set to 12 months following the documented 6-to-18-month transmission lag in the Indian banking sector (Khundrakpam and Jain, 2012; Raj, et al, 2020).</p>      <p>The impulse response of aggregate non-food credit to a one standard deviation expansionary monetary policy shock indicates that credit expands over the short to medium term, increasing by approximately 0.50 percentage points (pp) within the first six months, peaking to 0.8 pp around 13 months after the shock but becomes insignificant by the 15th month. Credit growth increases immediately following expansionary monetary policy shocks, reaches a peak after one year, and gradually reverts as balance sheets adjust (<a href="#CH2_2_1" class="links">Chart II.2.1</a>).</p>      <a id="CH2_2_1"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C22_1.jpg" alt="Chart II.2.1: Impulse Response of Bank Credit to Expansionary Monetary Policy Shocks" title="Chart II.2.1: Impulse Response of Bank Credit to Expansionary Monetary Policy Shocks"></div>      <br>      <a id="CH2_2_2"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C22_2.jpg" alt="Chart II.2.2: Sectoral Credit Response to Expansionary Monetary Policy Shocks" title="Chart II.2.2: Sectoral Credit Response to Expansionary Monetary Policy Shocks"></div>      <p>Across major sectors, the impact of the expansionary monetary policy shock is found to be heterogeneous. Credit to agriculture sector remains steady initially, before expanding from around 4th month onwards, reaching the peak in around 18 months. Industry and services sectors respond immediately to the monetary policy shock, with industry reaching its peak expansion earlier (around 10 months) than services (around 13 months), after which both sectors&rsquo; responses gradually taper off. The response of personal loans is found to be delayed, remaining flat over the first few months, but it follows a steeper expansion and peaks around 10th month, like industry (<a href="#CH2_2_2" class="links">Chart II.2.2</a>).</p>      <p class="head">References</p>      <p>Jord&agrave;, &Ograve;. (2005). Estimation and inference of impulse responses by local projections. American Economic Review, 95(1), 161&ndash;182.</p>      <p>Khundrakpam, J. K., & Jain, R. (2012). Monetary policy transmission in India: A peep inside the black box, RBI Working Paper, WPS (DEPR): 11/2012.</p>      <p>Lakdawala, A., & Sengupta, R. (2025). Measuring monetary policy shocks in emerging economies: Evidence from India. Journal of Money, Credit and Banking, 57(2-3), 407&ndash;437. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/jmcb.13144" target="_blank" class="links">https://doi.org/10.1111/jmcb.13144</a></p>        <p>Raj, J., Rath, D. P., Mitra, P., & John, J. (2020). Asset quality and credit channel of monetary policy transmission in India: Some evidence from bank-level data. RBI Working Paper, WPS (DEPR): 14/2020.</p></td>  </tr>          </table>    <br>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="12" align="center" class="head"><a id="T2_5"></a>Table II.5: Credit Growth</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="12" align="right">(Per cent, y-o-y)</td>          </tr>      <tr align="center" class="head">        <td width="45%">Sectors/Sub-Sectors</td>        <td width="5%">CAGR*</td>        <td width="5%">Post-COVID CAGR**</td>        <td width="5%">Mar-25</td>        <td width="5%">Jun-25</td>        <td width="5%">Aug-25</td>        <td width="5%">Sep-25</td>        <td width="5%">Dec-25</td>        <td width="5%">Mar-26</td>        <td width="5%">Jun-26</td>        <td width="5%">Jul-26</td>        <td width="5%">Aug-26</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Bank Credit</td>        <td align="right">11.9</td>        <td align="right">15.6</td>        <td align="right">11.0</td>        <td align="right">9.5</td>        <td align="right">10.3</td>        <td align="right">10.4</td>        <td align="right">14.5</td>        <td align="right">16.1</td>        <td align="right">18.6</td>        <td align="right">19.3</td>        <td align="right">19.1</td>          </tr>      <tr align="center">        <td colspan="12">Sectoral Deployment of Bank Credit</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Agriculture (12.9)</td>        <td align="right">11.5</td>        <td align="right">14.6</td>        <td align="righ