period between August 2016 to August 2026.<br>          3. **: CAGR for the period between April 2022 to August 2026.<br>          4. Figures in parentheses against each sector and sub-sectors denote share in total outstanding non-food credit as per the latest SIBC data.<br>          Source: RBI.</td>          </tr>      </table>    <p>Industrial credit growth also picked up and it was above its compounded annual growth rate (CAGR) over the last 10-year period in H1:2026-27, supporting overall credit expansion (<a href="#T2_5" class="links">Table II.5</a>). Services and personal loans continued to drive overall credit growth (<a href="#CH2_24" class="links">Chart II.24</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_24"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C224.jpg" alt="Chart II.24: Sectoral Deployment of Bank Credit" title="Chart II.24: Sectoral Deployment of Bank Credit"></div>    <p>Credit to industrial sector registered robust growth during H1:2026-27, largely attributable to a sharp acceleration in credit to large industries. This was supported partly by corporates&rsquo; reduced recourse to market-based funding as its cost was higher on account of heightened geopolitical tensions. Credit to MSMEs<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Pertains to credit to micro, small and medium segments within industry."><a href="#C2FN10" class="links">10</a></sup> continued on its double-digit growth trajectory, reflecting resilient credit demand including increased working capital requirements amidst global uncertainties and continued policy support. The policy support measures comprise the enhanced guarantee limit under the Credit Guarantee Fund Trust for Micro and Small Enterprises (CGTMSE) scheme, Emergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme (ECLGS) 5.0, among others (<a href="#CH2_25" class="links">Chart II.25</a>).</p>    <p>Among the major industrial sub-sectors, credit growth in infrastructure segment made a significant contribution to overall industrial credit expansion. 'Basic metal and metal products', &lsquo;all engineering&rsquo; and 'chemicals and chemical products' also aided industrial growth (<a href="#CH2_25" class="links">Chart II.25b</a>).</p>    <p>Within services sector, 'non-banking financial companies' (NBFCs) remained the largest recipient of bank credit during H1:2026-27, with improved availability following the withdrawal of the additional risk-weights effective April 1, 2025. Credit to services excluding NBFCs also expanded at a steady pace. Segments, such as trade and commercial real estate, recorded robust growth (<a href="#CH2_26" class="links">Chart II.26</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_25"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C225.jpg" alt="Chart II.25: Credit to Industry" title="Chart II.25: Credit to Industry"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH2_26"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C226.jpg" alt="Chart II.26: Credit Growth of Services Sector: Contribution of Major Sub-components" title="Chart II.26: Credit Growth of Services Sector: Contribution of Major Sub-components"></div>    <p>Credit growth in personal loans segment remained buoyant during H1:2026-27, driven primarily by housing, loans against gold jewellery and vehicle loans. Housing loans, which account for nearly half of the personal loans, continued to grow at a steady pace reflecting robust real estate activity. Growth in loans against gold jewellery, albeit moderating from its peak in June 2025, remained elevated, which may be attributed to higher gold prices resulting in increased collateral valuation along with revised regulations effective from April 1, 2026<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="RBI revised its Directions on consumption loan against gold collateral effective April 1, 2026, shifting loan-to-value (LTV) from 75 per cent to a tiered LTV structure [Tiered caps: 85 per cent (loans = ?2.5 lakh), 80 per cent (>?2.5 & =?5 lakh), 75 per cent (>?5 lakh)] that allows higher borrowing limits."><a href="#C2FN11" class="links">11</a></sup>. Vehicle loan growth accelerated sharply, nearly doubling vis-&agrave;-vis the previous year, on the back of persistent demand following the GST cut (<a href="#CH2_27" class="links">Chart II.27</a>).</p>    <p>NBFC Credit: A Sectoral Perspective<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Provisional data as reported on the last day of the month. Sectoral credit data of NBFCs are based on sample of (i) NBFCs in Upper and Middle Layers and (ii) HFCs, accounting for about 87 per cent of total outstanding credit (end-September 2025) with respect to data published in the ‘Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India 2024-25’."><a href="#C2FN12" class="links">12</a></sup></p>    <p>Credit extended by NBFCs exhibited robust expansion since August 2025, driven primarily by strong retail credit. The marked acceleration in credit growth to segments such as housing and gold, coupled with steady credit expansion in vehicle loans, drove the buoyant expansion in retail credit. Industrial credit growth softened, reflecting a moderation in lending to infrastructure, especially power sector, during H1:2026-27 (up to August). Credit growth in services sector remain strong despite some deceleration in recent months vis-&agrave;-vis last year (<a href="#T2_6" class="links">Table II.6</a>). The continued buoyancy in retail lending underscored NBFCs' role in complementing the banking sector by catering to credit requirements of a diverse range of borrowers.</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C2_3"></a>II.3 Transmission to Lending and Deposit Rates</p>    <p>Since the pause in the policy rate in February 2026, the weighted average domestic term deposit rates (WADTDRs) of scheduled commercial banks (SCBs) have moderated for both fresh and outstanding deposits by 40 basis points (bps) and 6 bps, respectively, in H1:2026-27 (till August), led by moderation in bulk deposit rates (<a href="#T2_7" class="links">Table II.7</a>). At the same time, the weighted average lending rate (WALR) of banks have firmed up for fresh rupee loans, owing to strong credit demand. Bank credit growth outpaced deposit growth, leading to increased funding pressure. During H1 (till August), the WALR on fresh rupee loans increased by 21 bps, while it declined modestly by 3 bps for outstanding loans. During H1, the median 1-year marginal cost of funds-based lending rate (MCLR) rose by 21 bps.</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="11" align="center" class="head"><a id="T2_6"></a>Table II.6: Sectoral Credit Growth of NBFCs (including HFCs)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="11" align="right">(Per cent, y-o-y)</td>          </tr>      <tr class="head">        <td width="40%" align="center">Sector</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">CAGR*</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">Mar-25</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">Jun-25</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">Aug-25</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">Sep-25</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">Dec-25</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">Mar-26</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">Jun-26</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">Jul-26</td>        <td width="6%" align="center">Aug-26</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>NBFCs Credit</td>        <td align="right">9.8</td>        <td align="right">11.6</td>        <td align="right">11.2</td>        <td align="right">10.0</td>        <td align="right">13.3</td>        <td align="right">13.8</td>        <td align="right">14.1</td>        <td align="right">14.3</td>        <td align="right">14.9</td>        <td align="right">15.8</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>1. Agriculture and Allied Activities (1.3)</td>        <td align="right">14.1</td>        <td align="right">6.0</td>        <td align="right">5.1</td>        <td align="right">5