<td align="right">0</td>            <td align="right">2</td>            <td align="right">15</td>            <td align="right">1</td>            <td align="right">0</td>            <td align="right">-15</td>            <td align="right">2</td>            <td align="right">6</td>            <td align="right">-1</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>Jul &ndash; 2026</td>            <td align="right">0</td>            <td align="right">3</td>            <td align="right">-10</td>            <td align="right">0</td>            <td align="right">0</td>            <td align="right">10</td>            <td align="right">-1</td>            <td align="right">4</td>            <td align="right">1</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>Aug &ndash; 2026</td>            <td align="right">0</td>            <td align="right">-1</td>            <td align="right">-18</td>            <td align="right">-2</td>            <td align="right">0</td>            <td align="right">10</td>            <td align="right">9</td>            <td align="right">-1</td>            <td align="right">-1</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td colspan="10">Notes: 1. *: Data on WALR and WADTDR pertain up to August 2026.<br>            2. Data on EBLR pertain to 32 domestic banks.<br>            3. #: At constant weight of base period (January 2025).<br>            WALR: Weighted average lending rate; WADTDR: Weighted average domestic term deposit rate; MCLR: Marginal cost of funds-based lending rate;<br>            EBLR: External benchmark-based lending rate.<br>            Sources: RBI; and staff calculations.</td>          </tr>        </table>        <br><a id="CH2_27"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C227.jpg" alt="Chart II.27: Credit Growth of Personal Loans: Contribution of Major Sub-components" title="Chart II.27: Credit Growth of Personal Loans: Contribution of Major Sub-components"></div>    <p>In response to a cumulative reduction of 125 bps in the policy repo rate during February 2025 to August 2026, the WADTDRs for fresh and outstanding deposits have declined by 95 bps and 53 bps, respectively. On the lending side, WALRs for fresh and outstanding loans have declined by 72 bps and 91 bps, respectively.</p>    <p>The share of the external benchmark-based lending rate (EBLR) linked loans in total outstanding floating rate loans of scheduled commercial banks increased from 67.6 per cent as at end-March 2026 to 68.2 per cent at end-June 2026. Consequently, the share of MCLR-linked loans declined from 30.2 per cent to 29.6 per cent. Public sector banks (PSBs) still have a significant proportion of their loans linked to MCLR (<a href="#CH2_28" class="links">Chart II.28a</a>), while private banks (PVBs) extend a large part of their loans at EBLR (<a href="#CH2_28" class="links">Chart II.28b</a>). The MCLR and other legacy rates &ndash; based on internal benchmarks and with longer reset periods &ndash; act as an impediment to faster policy transmission.</p>    <p>PSBs have a high share of MCLR-linked loans in agriculture, infrastructure, other personal loans and large industry (<a href="#CH2_29" class="links">Chart II.29a</a>). The shares of MCLR-linked loans are lower for PVBs than those in PSBs (<a href="#CH2_29" class="links">Chart II.29b</a>).</p>    <p>Bank group-wise, the transmission to WALRs on fresh and outstanding rupee loans of PVBs was higher than that of PSBs (<a href="#CH2_30" class="links">Chart II.30a</a>). The large share of EBLR-linked loans may have facilitated higher transmission in case of PVBs compared to PSBs. However, lending rates of PVBs remained above those of PSBs (<a href="#CH2_30" class="links">Chart II.30b</a>). The maximum pass-through to lending rates was witnessed among foreign banks, reflecting their higher share of EBLR-linked loans<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The proportion of EBLR-linked loans was the highest for foreign banks (94.7 per cent), followed by private banks (90.8 per cent) and public sector banks (53.6 per cent) as of end-June 2026."><a href="#C2FN13" class="links">13</a></sup> and a higher share of low-cost bulk deposits of lower maturity.</p>    <a id="CH2_28"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C228.jpg" alt="Chart II.28: Bank Group-wise Share of Interest Rate Benchmarks" title="Chart II.28: Bank Group-wise Share of Interest Rate Benchmarks"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH2_29"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C229.jpg" alt="Chart II.29: Sector-wise Share of Interest Rate Benchmarks" title="Chart II.29: Sector-wise Share of Interest Rate Benchmarks"></div>    <p>Sectoral analysis shows that the transmission to lending rates has been heterogeneous due to varying proportions of credit portfolios linked to fixed and floating interest rates and differential spreads charged by banks. Furthermore, the shifts in volumes across banks/sectors towards higher/lower interest rate slabs could impact aggregate lending rates. The WALRs showed a broad based decline across sectors since February 2025. The volume effect, however, partially dampened transmission in other personal loans and trade in fresh loans, whereas it complemented in sectors such as agriculture and education, thereby enhancing transmission (<a href="#CH2_31" class="links">Chart II.31</a>).</p>    <a id="CH2_30"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C230.jpg" alt="Chart II.30: Bank Group-wise Lending Rates and Transmission" title="Chart II.30: Bank Group-wise Lending Rates and Transmission"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH2_31"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C231.jpg" alt="Chart II.31: Sector-wise Transmission to Weighted Average Lending Rates (Feb 25-Aug 26)" title="Chart II.31: Sector-wise Transmission to Weighted Average Lending Rates (Feb 25-Aug 26)"></div>    <p>For EBLR-linked loans, PVBs have increased their spreads (charged over the repo rate) for education, housing, MSMEs and vehicle loans, which dampened the extent of transmission (<a href="#T2_8" class="links">Table II.8</a>). On the other hand, PSBs have reduced spread in education, MSME and vehicle loans, and increased it in other personal loans. For domestic banks, spread was higher in August 2026 over January 2025 for all sectors except education and vehicle loans.</p>    <p>Through their extensive outreach and specialised lending activities across sectors, non-banking financial companies (NBFCs) substantially contribute to the transmission of monetary policy via the credit channel. Nevertheless, NBFCs typically charge higher lending rates compared to SCBs, mainly due to differences in the risk profile of borrowers, their funding structures, and cost of liabilities. During February 2025 to August 2026, lending rates of NBFCs moderated across sectors. The highest transmission in fresh lending rates was recorded in advances against gold followed by vehicle and MSME loans (<a href="#CH2_32" class="links">Chart II.32</a>).</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="7" align="center" class="head"><a id="T2_8"></a>Table II.8: Spread of Weighted Average Lending Rates on Fresh Rupee Loans*</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="7" align="right">(Percentage points)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="28%" rowspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Sectors</span></td>        <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head">Jan-25</span></td>        <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head">Aug-26</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">Public sector banks</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">Private banks</span></td>        <td width="12%" ali