d over the repo rate for loans linked to external benchmarks.<br>              Sources: RBI; and staff calculations.</td>          </tr>        </table>    <br><a id="CH2_32"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C232.jpg" alt="Chart II.32: Sector-wise Transmission to Fresh Lending Rates of NBFCs" title="Chart II.32: Sector-wise Transmission to Fresh Lending Rates of NBFCs"></div>    <p>The Government of India reviewed the interest rates on various small savings instruments, which are linked to secondary market yields on G-secs of comparable maturities, and kept them unchanged for Q3:2026-27, marking 11 consecutive quarters with no change in rates. This has led to substantial positive spread of small saving instrument rates over their formula-based rates except for public provident fund (<a href="#T2_9" class="links">Table II.9</a>). The widening gaps may be limiting the transmission of policy rates to banks&rsquo; deposit rates, especially during policy easing, because of the potential substitution effect. A large interest rate differential in favour of small savings could lead to migration of deposits away from banks, thus widening the credit-deposit gap in the banking system.</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C2_4"></a>II.4 Conclusion</p>    <p>Financial markets conditions remained largely congenial during H1:2026-27, with intermittent bouts of volatility arising from geopolitical uncertainties, fluctuations in crude oil prices and capital flows. System liquidity remained in surplus for most of the period, aided by capital inflows under the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s special swap facilities. Money market rates softened and remained largely range bound in line with the evolving liquidity conditions. Government bond yields softened before hardening in September, mainly reflecting global developments. Corporate bond yields moved in tandem with G-sec yields. Domestic equity markets gained in H1, supported by robust corporate earnings, and renewed buying by foreign investors. The Indian rupee experienced two-way movements with a depreciating bias, although the pace of depreciation moderated. Bank credit growth remained robust and broad-based across major sectors. The non-bank sources of finance also expanded. Transmission to deposit rates further strengthened in H1, while it moderated in the case of lending rates. Going ahead, liquidity management operations will be conditional on evolving domestic financial conditions, monetary policy decisions and the stance of monetary policy.</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="7" align="center"><a id="T2_9"></a><span class="head">Table II.9: Interest Rates on Small Savings Instruments &ndash; Q3:2026-27</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="28%" align="center"><span class="head">Small Savings Scheme</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">Maturity<br>            (years)</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">Spread<sup>$</sup><br>            (Percentage point)</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">Average G-sec yield<sup>#</sup><br>            (Per cent)</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">Formula-based rate of Interest<br>            (Per cent)</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">GoI Announced Rate of Interest<br>            (Per cent)</span></td>        <td width="12%" align="center"><span class="head">Difference<br>            (Basis points)</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td align="center"><span class="head">(1)</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">(2)</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">(3)</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">(4)</span></td>        <td align="center"><span class="head">(5) = (3) + (4)</span></td