nly), percentage change in monthly outstanding issuance as a measure of net new supply, and the spread between the 3 month CD/CP rate minus the policy repo rate as the spread variable.</p>        <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C2FN8"></a>8</sup> Bank credit data are based on fortnightly Section-42 return, which covers all scheduled commercial banks (SCBs), while sectoral non-food credit data are based on sector-wise and industry-wise bank credit (SIBC) return, which covers select banks accounting for about 95 per cent of total non-food credit extended by all SCBs, pertaining to the last day of the month. With effect from December 31, 2025, definition of last reporting fortnight has been changed to the last day of the month under the Banking Laws (Amendment) Act 2025. Accordingly, the y-o-y growth rates from December 2025 onwards are based on end-of-month data for the current year and data for the last reporting fortnight (as per old definition) for the corresponding month of the previous year.</p>        <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C2FN9"></a>9</sup> H1:2026-27 data up to August 2026.</p>        <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C2FN10"></a>10</sup> Pertains to credit to micro, small and medium segments within industry.</p>        <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C2FN11"></a>11</sup> RBI revised its Directions on consumption loan against gold collateral effective April 1, 2026, shifting loan-to-value (LTV) from 75 per cent to a tiered LTV structure [Tiered caps: 85 per cent (loans &le; &#8377;2.5 lakh), 80 per cent (>&#8377;2.5 & &le;&#8377;5 lakh), 75 per cent (>&#8377;5 lakh)] that allows higher borrowing limits.</p>        <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C2FN12"></a>12</sup> Provisional data as reported on the last day of the month. Sectoral credit data of NBFCs are based on sample of (i) NBFCs in Upper and Middle Layers and (ii) HFCs, accounting for about 87 per cent of total outstanding credit (end-September 2025) with respect to data published in the &lsquo;Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India 2024-25&rsquo;.</p>        <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C2FN13"></a>13</sup> The proportion of EBLR-linked loans was the highest for foreign banks (94.7 per cent), followed by private banks (90.8 per cent) and public sector banks (53.6 per cent) as of end-June 2026.</p><hr>        <p align="center" class="head"><a id="C3"></a>III. Demand and Output</p>        <p><em>Domestic economic activity exhibited resilience in H1:2026-27, despite continued global uncertainty. Real GDP posted a strong growth of 7.8 per cent in Q1:2026-27. Private final consumption remained healthy, supported by buoyant discretionary consumption. Revival in private capex and government&rsquo;s continued focus on infrastructure spending strengthened investment activity. Manufacturing expanded at a robust pace, while services sector maintained its buoyancy. Looking ahead, weak monsoons, the prolonged West-Asia conflict, additional frictions in global trade and tighter global financial conditions may have an adverse bearing on both the demand and supply side of the economy.</em></p>        <p>Domestic economic activity exhibited resilience in H1:2026-27, despite continued global uncertainty amidst the prolonged West-Asia conflict. The supply-side pressures were effectively dealt with proactive policy measures that ensured supply of key inputs for productive sectors. As per the National Statistics Office (NSO)&rsquo;s estimates, real GDP recorded a growth of 7.8 per cent in Q1:2026-27 reflecting strong momentum in underlying economic activity. Aggregate demand remained strong on the back of both private consumption and investment. Net external demand also contributed positively to growth with rebound in merchandise exports and sustained buoyancy in services exports. On the supply side, both manufacturing and services sectors recorded strong growth in Q1. Going ahead, weak monsoon and El Ni&ntilde;o may weigh on agricultural activity. Industry and services sectors are expected to continue benefitting from the Emergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme 5.0 and the Transition Facilitation (Quality Control) Order 2026. Similarly, India&rsquo;s growing network of bilateral free trade agreements (FTAs) augurs well for external demand, though uncertainties about additional tariffs on US imports, including those under Section 301, pose some risk. The risks from protracted West Asia conflict and financial market repricing due to changes in US monetary policy also require close monitoring.</p>        <p class="head"><a id="C3_1"></a>III.1 Aggregate Demand</p>        <p>Aggregate demand conditions in the economy held up well in Q1:2026-27. As per the statistics released by the National Statistics Office, real GDP grew by 7.8 per cent, up from 6.9 per cent a year ago. Despite modest rise in domestic prices of select commodities, private final consumption expenditure (PFCE) remained healthy, while double-digit growth in gross fixed capital formation (GFCF) suggests a strong recovery in the capex cycle (<a href="#T3_1" class="links">Table III.1</a>).</p>        <p>Private final consumption contributed almost half of GDP growth in Q1:2026-27, though lower than 54.6 per cent a year ago. Strong growth in gross fixed capital formation (GFCF) also led to a sharp rise in its contribution to growth. By contrast, the contribution of government final consumption expenditure moderated in Q1. As real imports contracted and real exports rose sharply, net external demand was a positive contributor to GDP growth for the second successive quarter in Q1 (<a href="#T3_1" class="links">Table III.1</a> and <a href="#CH3_1" class="links">Chart III.1a</a>). The momentum of GDP &ndash; quarter-on-quarter (q-o-q) seasonally adjusted annualised growth rate (SAAR) &ndash; was placed at 6.5 per cent in Q1 (<a href="#CH3_1" class="links">Chart III.1b</a>).</p>        <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">          <tr>            <td colspan="10" align="center" class="head"><a id="T3_1"></a>Table III.1: Real GDP Growth</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td colspan="10" align="right">(Y-o-y, per cent)^</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td width="28%" rowspan="3" align="center"><span class="head">Item</span></td>            <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">Annual</span></td>            <td colspan="5" align="center"><span class="head">Quarterly</span></td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td colspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Growth</span></td>            <td colspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Weighted Contribution*</span></td>            <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26</span></td>            <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">2026-27</span></td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">2024-25</span></td>            <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26</span></td>            <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">2024-25</span></td>            <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26</span></td>            <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">Q1</span></td>            <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">Q2</span></td>            <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">Q3</span></td>            <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">Q4</span></td>            <td align="center"><span class="head">Q1</span></td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>Private final consumption expenditure</td>            <td align="right">6.4</td>            <td align="right">7.2</td>            <td align="right">3.6</td>            <td align="right">4.0</td>            <td align="right">6.8</td>            <td align="right">6.4</td>            <td align="right">8.0</td>            <td align="right">7.5</td>            <td align="rig