           <td align="right">8.6</td>            <td align="right">7.8</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td colspan="10">Note: *: Component-wise contributions to growth do not add up to GDP growth because change in stocks, valuables and statistical discrepancies are not included.<br>              ^: Unless specified otherwise, all discussions on growth rates in this chapter are on year-on-year (y-o-y) basis.<br>              Sources: National Statistics Office (NSO); and RBI staff estimates.</td>          </tr>        </table><p>The realised growth for Q1:2026-27 turned out to be higher than that projected in the Monetary Policy Report (MPR) of April 2026 (<a href="#CH3_2" class="links">Chart III.2</a>). This was mainly on account of a stronger than anticipated performance of gross fixed capital formation and net exports.</p><p class="head">III.1.1 Private Final Consumption Expenditure</p><p>Private consumption growth in Q1:2026-27 remained strong at 7.1 per cent compared to 6.8 per cent during the same quarter last year, supported by buoyant discretionary spending. The rising purchasing power of households, buoyed by a prolonged phase of low inflation, stable employment conditions, tax rationalisation, and robust rabi production in previous quarters bolstered households' discretionary spending. Among major high frequency indicators, passenger vehicle sales recorded double-digit growth in April-July 2026. Demand for consumer durables increased at a healthy pace in Q1 and July-August, while growth in consumer non-durables remained subdued. The non-housing retail loans, which generally support discretionary spending, also increased at a robust pace. Slowdown in domestic air passenger traffic since February 2026 reflected price pressures amid the West Asia conflict. Tractor sales expanded at a robust pace during April-July, before remaining flat in August, which coupled with buoyant growth in two-wheeler sales during April-August, imply strong rural demand conditions. However, the increase in demand for work under the Viksit Bharat&ndash;Guarantee for Rozgar and Ajeevika Mission (Gramin) [earlier MGNREGA] in August- September 2026 due to weakening of agricultural activity reflects a shift of rural households towards low-paying jobs (<a href="#T3_2" class="links">Table III.2</a>).</p>        <a id="CH3_1"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C31.jpg" alt="Chart III.1: GDP Growth and its Constituent" title="Chart III.1: GDP Growth and its Constituent"></div>        <br>    <a id="CH3_2"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C32.jpg" alt="Chart III.2: GDP Growth - Projection versus Actual" title="Chart III.2: GDP Growth - Projection versus Actual"></div>    <p>The extent of pass-through of input costs, impact of weak monsoon on rural income, and continued momentum in manufacturing and service sector activities will remain pivotal to the sustenance of overall PFCE growth. Government&rsquo;s policy measures to support agriculture, resilient agriculture allied sectors, and a wider fiscal safety net through direct benefit transfers may help protect rural income and consumption.</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="12" align="center" class="head"><a id="T3_2"></a>Table III.2: Indicators of Consumption</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="12" align="right">(Y-o-y growth, per cent)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="34%" rowspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Indicators</span></td>        <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">2024-25</span></td>        <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26</span></td>        <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head">2026-27</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="6%" align="center"><span class="head">Q1</span></td>      