ederation of Automobile Dealers Associations (FADA); NSO; RBI; Tractor and Mechanisation Association (TMA); and Ministry of Chemicals and Fertilisers (MoC&F); Ministry of Rural Development; NielsenIQ&rsquo;s Retail Audit Service; and RBI staff estimates.</td>          </tr>        </table><p>The July 2026 round of the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s urban and rural consumer confidence surveys found moderation in households&rsquo; confidence for the current period. However, households are optimistic about their spending outlook for the year ahead amidst some concerns on the economic situation and employment (<a href="#T3_3" class="links">Table III.3</a>). Labour market conditions continued to present a mixed picture in April-August 2026 (<a href="#CH3_3" class="links">Chart III.3a</a>). The worker population ratio and the labour force participation rate improved since June 2026 after three months of successive decline. The unemployment rate dropped in July and August 2026, after edging up in March-June. The workforce composition shifted towards regular salaried jobs, particularly in the secondary and tertiary sectors, with a decline in the share of self-employed in Q1:2026-27 over the previous quarters (<a href="#CH3_3" class="links">Chart III.3b</a>).<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Share of regular salaried employees increased from 25.5 per cent in Q4:2025-26 to 26.2 per cent in Q1:2026-27. The share of secondary sector in totalemployment increased from 25.2 per cent to 26.7 per cent, while that of tertiary sector increased from 33.7 per cent to 34.6 per cent during the same period."><a href="#C3FN1" class="links">1</a></sup></p><p class="head">III.1.2 Gross Fixed Capital Formation</p><p>Gross fixed capital formation (GFCF) expanded by 11.9 per cent in Q1:2026-27 &ndash; the fastest pace in over three years. Investment activity was aided by both private and government capex. The share of GFCF in GDP improved to 34.3 per cent in Q1 from 33.1 per cent in the previous quarter. Buoyancy in investment activity was borne out by rising seasonally adjusted capacity utilisation in the corporate sector. A sharp surge in production and import of capital goods, robust credit demand and government&rsquo;s capital outlay during July-August indicate continued buoyancy in investment activity during Q2.</p><table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">  <tr>    <td colspan="5" align="center"><span class="head"><a id="T3_3"></a>Table III.3: Summary of Urban Consumer Confidence based on Net Responses</span></td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td width="40%" rowspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Main variables</span></td>    <td colspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Current Perceptions</span></td>    <td colspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">One year ahead Expectations</span></td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td width="15%" align="center"><span class="head">May-26</span></td>    <td width="15%" align="center"><span class="head">Jul-26</span></td>    <td width="15%" align="center"><span class="head">May-26</span></td>    <td width="15%" align="center"><span class="head">Jul-26</span></td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Economic Situation</td>    <td width="15%" align="right">-16.5</td>    <td width="15%" align="right">-23.4</td>    <td width="15%" align="right">19.5</td>    <td width="15%" align="right">11.1</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Employment</td>    <td align="right">-14.4</td>    <td align="right">-21.9</td>    <td align="right">21.8</td>    <td align="right">15.1</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Price Level</td>    <td align="right">-90.7</td>    <td align="right">-92.7</td>    <td align="right">-71.5</td>    <td align="right">-74.9</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Income</td>    <td align="right">0.9</td>    <td align="right">1.9</td>    <td align="right">47.1</td>    <td align="right">45.8</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Spending</td>    <td align="right">74.0</td>    <td align="right">77.3</td>    <td align="right">76.6</td>    <td align="right">79.7</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Consumer Confidence Index</td>    <td align="right">90.7</td>    <td align="right">88.3</td>    <td align="right">118.7</td>    <td align="right">115.3</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="5">Note: &lsquo;Net response&rsquo; is the difference between the percentage of respondents reporting optimism and those reporting pessimism. It ranges between -100 and 100. Any value greater than zero indicates expansion/ optimism and values less than zero indicate contraction/ pessimism. Consumer Confidence Index = 100 + Average of Net Responses on the economic situation, income, spending, employment and the price level.<br>      Source: Urban Consumer Confidence Survey, RBI.</td>    </tr></table><br><a id="CH3_3"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C33.jpg" alt="Chart III.3: Employment Situation" title="Chart III.3: Employment Situation"></div>    <p>Revival in private-sector investment is gathering pace gradually as borne out by related indicators. Credit disbursal to large scale industry picked up sharply. Among major industries, credit to infrastructure; basic metal and metal products; engineering sector; chemical and chemical products; petroleum, coal products and nuclear fuels; and textiles recorded higher growth. Among major coincident indicators of investment in the construction sector, both steel consumption and cement production exhibited healthy growth in Q2:2026-27 so far (<a href="#T3_4" class="links">Table III.4</a>). As per the CMIE data, private sector constituted 86.3 per cent of total new projects announced in H1:2026-27 vis-&agrave;-vis 68.7 per cent in H2:2025-26. The flow of resources to the commercial sector through various channels (banks/financial institutions, external commercial borrowings, initial public offerings, etc.) increased significantly in April-August 2026 from its level a year ago.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Total flow of resources to the commercial sector increased to ? 17.46 lakh crore in April-August 2026 from ? 11.24 lakh crore a year ago."><a href="#C3FN2" class="links">2</a></sup> The continued focus of public spending on transport, power, logistics and urban infrastructure is expected to crowd in private capex.</p>    <p>Rise in capacity utilisation (CU) in previous few quarters augured well for new capacity additions that are required to meet domestic demand. Although CU in the manufacturing sector decreased sequentially in Q1, it was above its long-period average as well as the corresponding period&rsquo;s level. The seasonally adjusted CU increased by 110 basis points from its level in the previous quarter and stood at 76.3 per cent in Q1:2026-27 (<a href="#CH3_4" class="links">Chart III.4</a>).</p>    <a id="CH3_4"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C34.jpg" alt="Chart III.4: Capacity Utilisation in Manufacturing" title="Chart III.4: Capacity Utilisation in Manufacturing"></div>    <p class="head">III.1.3 Government Consumption</p>    <p>Government final consumption expenditure (GFCE) in real terms grew by 4.3 per cent (y-o-y) in Q1:2026-27 compared to 7.7 per cent in the previous quarter (<a href="#T3_1" class="links">Table III.1</a>). The gross fiscal deficit of the central government stood at 41.9 per cent of the fiscal year's budget estimates (BE) during April-August 2026, higher than 38.1 per cent, recorded during the corresponding period of the previous year. On the expenditure front, revenue expenditure of the central government (excluding interest payments and major subsidies) registered a growth of 12.5 per cent during April-August 2026 on a y-o-y basis, as compared to marginal contraction of 0.3 per cent during the same period of the last fiscal year (<a href="#CH3_5" class="links">Chart III.5a</a>). Expenditure on major subsidies (comprising food, fertiliser, and petroleum) of the Centre registered a double-digit growth of 24.4 per cent during April-August 2026<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="As compared to a contraction of 15.8 per cent during April-August 2025-26."><a href="#C3FN3" class="links">3</a></sup> led by expansion in fertiliser and food subsidy. Interest payments by the Centre, however, contracted during April-August 2026, over the corresponding period of 2025-26. Capital expenditure of the Centre registered a y-o-y growth of 18.2 per cent during April-August 2026 (<a href="#CH3_5" class="links">Chart III.5a</a>). Accordingly, the quality of government expenditure improved as the ratio of revenue expenditure to capital outlay (RECO) declined in April-August 2026, boding well for infrastructure-led development<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="A lower RECO ratio indicates an improvement in the quality of expenditure of the central government."><a href="#C3FN4" class="links">4</a></sup> (<a href="#CH3_5" class="links">Chart III.5b</a>).</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="8" align="center" class="head"><a id="T3_4"></a>Table III.4: Indicators of Investment Demand</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="8" align="right">(Y-o-y, per cent)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="30%" rowspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Indicators</span></td>        <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26</span></td>        <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head">2026-27</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">Q1</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">Q2</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">Q3</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">Q4</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">Q1</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">Jul</span></td>        <td width="10%" align="center"><span class="head">Aug</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Import of capital goods</td>        <td align="right">14.3</td>        <td align="right">8.5</td>        <td align="right">14.7</td>        <td align="right">12.9</td>        <td align="right">25.6</td>        <td align="right">25.4</td>        <td align="right">23.6</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>IIP- Capital goods</td>        <td align="right">8.8</td>        <td align="right">8.5</td>        <td align="right">11.7</td>        <td align="right">12.1</td>        <td align="right">15.2</td>        <td align="right">19.0</td>        <td align="right">16.9</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Finished steel consumption</td>        <td align="right">8.0</td>        <td align="right">8.6</td>        <td align="right">4.6</td>        <td align="right">10.4</td>        <td align="right">8.3</td>        <td align="right">6.7</td>        <td align="right">5.7</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Cement production</td>        <td align="right">7.3</td>        <td align="right">7.2</td>        <td align="right">11.5</td>        <td align="right">8.2</td>        <td align="right">8.9</td>        <td align="right">12.7</td>        <td align="right">12.5</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="8">Sources: Directorate General of Commercial Intelligence and Statistics (DGCI&S); NSO; Joint Plant Committee; and Office of Economic Adviser.</td>          </tr>      </table>        <p>On the receipts side, Centre&rsquo;s gross tax revenue registered a growth of 6.5 per cent (y-o-y) during April-August 2026, attributable to growth in direct taxes. Direct tax collections registered a robust growth of 14.6 per cent (y-o-y) during April-August 2026, as compared to a contraction of 0.8 per cent during the corresponding period of last year. Robust growth in direct taxes is on account of double-digit growth in both corporation and income tax collections. Centre&rsquo;s collections from indirect taxes contracted during April-August 2026, due to decline in goods and services tax (GST) revenue<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="This GST data released by the Controller General of Accounts is exclusive of States’ GST (SGST) collections. This figure includes GST compensation cess."><a href="#C3FN5" class="links">5</a></sup> and revenue collected from union excise duties (<a href="#T3_5" class="links">Table III.5</a>). It reflected GST rationalisation across key sectors and cut in excise duty on petrol and diesel. However, the GST collections by Centre plus States<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The data on gross GST collections (Centre plus States) is released monthly on the official website of GST. The collections reported in this section exclude GST compensation cess."><a href="#C3FN6" class="links">6</a></sup> registered robust growth during April-September 2026 (<a href="#CH3_6" class="links">Chart III.6</a>). Net tax revenue, which largely represents Centre&rsquo;s gross tax revenue adjusted for assignment to States, increased during April-August 2026 as assignment to States registered a strong growth during the period (<a href="#T3_5" class="links">Table III.5</a>).</p>        <a id="CH3_5"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C35.jpg" alt="Chart III.5: Central Government Expenditure &ndash; April-August" title="Chart III.5: Central Government Expenditure &ndash; April-August"></div>        <br>        <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">          <tr>            <td colspan="9" align="center" class="head"><a id="T3_5"></a>Table III.5: Central Government's Tax Collections</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td width="28%" rowspan="3" align="center"><span class="head">Item</span></td>            <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">&#8377; thousand crore</span></td>            <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">P