   <td align="right">39.3</td>            <td align="right">-11.9</td>            <td align="right">28.2</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>3. Union excise duties</td>            <td align="right">317</td>            <td align="right">389</td>            <td align="right">112</td>            <td align="right">87</td>            <td align="right">35.3</td>            <td align="right">22.3</td>            <td align="right">7.6</td>            <td align="right">-22.6</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>C. Gross tax revenue</td>            <td align="right">4,270</td>            <td align="right">4,404</td>            <td align="right">1,344</td>            <td align="right">1,432</td>            <td align="right">31.5</td>            <td align="right">32.5</td>            <td align="right">0.8</td>            <td align="right">6.5</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>D. Assignment to States</td>            <td align="right">1,422</td>            <td align="right">1,526</td>            <td align="right">530</td>            <td align="right">590</td>            <td align="right">37.3</td>            <td align="right">38.7</td>            <td align="right">16.3</td>            <td align="right">11.4</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td>E. Net tax revenue</td>            <td align="right">2,837</td>            <td align="right">2,867</td>            <td align="right">810</td>            <td align="right">838</td>            <td align="right">28.6</td>            <td align="right">29.2</td>            <td align="right">-7.3</td>            <td align="right">3.4</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td colspan="9">Note: BE: Budget Estimates.<br>              Sources: Union Budget Documents; and Controller General of Accounts.</td>          </tr>        </table>        <p>The central government&rsquo;s non-tax revenue recorded a growth of 3.2 per cent during April-August 2026, primarily attributable to the surplus transfer of &#8377;2.87 lakh crore by the Reserve Bank in May 2026 (<a href="#CH3_7" class="links">Chart III.7</a>).</p>        <p>The consolidated gross fiscal deficit of state governments and union territories<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Data pertain to 31 States/UTs."><a href="#C3FN7" class="links">7</a></sup> is budgeted at 3.2 per cent of GDP for 2026-27, lower than the provisional estimates of 2025-26 (<a href="#T3_6" class="links">Table III.6</a>). State governments continue to prioritise capital expenditure, as reflected in the decline in RECO ratio<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="A lower value signifies an improvement in the RECO ratio."><a href="#C3FN8" class="links">8</a></sup> (<a href="#CH3_8" class="links">Chart III.8</a>). States&rsquo; capital expenditure is also supported by the central government through the &lsquo;Scheme for Special Assistance to States for Capital Investment&rsquo;, under which &#8377;2 lakh crore has been allocated for 2026-27.</p>        <a id="CH3_6"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C36.jpg" alt="Chart III.6: GST Collections (Centre plus States)" title="Chart III.6: GST Collections (Centre plus States)"></div>        <br>    <a id="CH3_7"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C37.jpg" alt="Chart III.7: Centre's Non-tax Revenue in April-August" title="Chart III.7: Centre's Non-tax Revenue in April-August"></div>    <br>    <table width="80%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="4" align="center" class="head"><a id="T3_6"></a>Table III.6: State Government Finances - Key Fiscal Indicators</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="4" align="right">(Per cent of GDP)</td>          </tr>      <tr class="head">        <td width="34%" align="center">Item</td>        <td width="22%" align="center">2024-25 (A)</td>        <td width="22%" align="center">2025-26 (PA)</td>        <td width="22%" align="center">2026-27 (BE)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Revenue deficit</td>        <td align="right">0.6</td>        <td align="right">0.7</td>        <td align="right">0.2</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Gross fiscal deficit</td>        <td align="right">3.2</td>        <td align="right">3.4</td>        <td align="right">3.2</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Primary deficit</td>        <td align="right">1.5</td>        <td align="right">1.8</td>        <td align="right">1.5</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="4">Note: Data pertain to 31 States/UTs.<br>          A: Actuals; PA: Provisional Accounts; BE: Budget Estimates.<br>          Sources: Budget documents of States/UTs and Comptroller and Auditor General (CAG) of India.</td>          </tr>      </table>        <p>Data available for April-August 2026 indicate that the key deficit indicators of states, measured as a proportion of their respective budget estimates for 2026-27, were lower than in the corresponding period a year ago (<a href="#CH3_9" class="links">Chart III.9a</a>). This improvement is largely due to a lower growth in expenditure alongside a significant expansion in revenue receipts (<a href="#CH3_9" class="links">Chart III.9b</a>). Among the major components of revenue receipts, State Goods and Services Tax (SGST) collections registered strong growth at 15.2 per cent, while receipts from state excise duties and sales tax also remained buoyant.</p>        <a id="CH3_8"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C38.jpg" alt="Chart III.8: States&rsquo; Capital Outlay" title="Chart III.8: States&rsquo; Capital Outlay"></div>        <br>    <a id="CH3_9"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C39.jpg" alt="Chart III.9: States&rsquo; Key Fiscal Performance Indicators (April-August)" title="Chart III.9: States&rsquo; Key Fiscal Performance Indicators (April-August)"></div>    <br>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="7" align="center" class="head"><a id="T3_7"></a>Table III.7: Government Market Borrowings</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="7" align="right">(&#8377; crore)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="28%">&nbsp;</td>        <td colspan="3" align="center" class="head">H1:2025-26</td>        <td colspan="3" align="center" class="head">H1: 2026-27</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="28%">&nbsp;</td>        <td width="12%" align="center" class="head">Centre</td>        <td width="12%" align="center" class="head">States</td>        <td width="12%" align="center" class="head">Total</td>        <td width="12%" align="center" class="head">Centre</td>        <td width="12%" align="center" class="head">States</td>        <td width="12%" align="center" class="head">Total</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Net Borrowings</td>        <td align="right">5,88,299</td>        <td align="right">3,56,092</td>        <td align="right">9,44,391</td>        <td align="right">5,41,250</td>        <td align="right">3,39,249</td>        <td align="right">8,80,499</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Gross Borrowings</td>        <td align="right">7,95,000</td>        <td align="right">5,00,792</td>        <td align="right">12,95,792</td>        <td align="right">8,13,506</td>        <td align="right">4,97,939</td>        <td align="right">13,11,445</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="7">Source: RBI.</td>          </tr>      </table><p>In the Union Budget for 2026-27, gross and net market borrowings of the Central Government through dated securities were pegged at &#8377;17.2 lakh crore and &#8377;11.7 lakh crore, respectively. Since the Budget presentation, switches of G-sec were conducted in March 2026, thereby reducing gross market borrowings of the Central Government to &#8377;16.0 lakh crore. During H1:2026-27, gross market borrowings raised by the Centre stood at &#8377;8.1 lakh crore, constituting 50.9 per cent of the revised gross market borrowings (<a href="#T3_7" class="links">Table III.7</a>). The weighted average yield of issuances at 7.0 per cent in H1:2026-27 was higher than 6.6 per cent in H1:2025-26. The weighted average maturity of the issuances declined to 16.7 years in H1:2026-27 from 19.6 years in H1:2025-26. During H2:2026-27, the Government of India, in consultation with the Reserve Bank, has decided to borrow &#8377;7.9 lakh crore through dated securities. State Governments/Union Territories mobilised &#8377;5.0 lakh crore during H1:2026-27, as against the indicative calendar amount of &#8377;5.7 lakh crore. In order to bridge temporary mismatches between receipts and expenditures, the limit for Ways and Means Advances (WMA) for the Central Government was fixed at &#8377;2.5 lakh crore for H1:2026-27 and &#8377;50,000 crore for H2:2026-27. Currently, the aggregate WMA limit for States/UTs stands at &#8377;61,008 crore.</p><p class="head">III.1.4 External Demand</p><p>Amidst the ongoing West Asia crisis and lingering trade policy uncertainty, global demand conditions continue to remain fragile. Strong demand for AI-related goods and the frontloading of consumer goods in anticipation of additional US tariffs after the expiration of temporary tariffs in July 2026<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The US imposed additional tariffs ranging from 10 per cent to 12.5 per cent on 60 economies effective from July 24, 2026, under section 301 measures for forced labour. India is placed in the lower tariff tier of 10 per cent (above Most-Favoured-Nation)."><a href="#C3FN9" class="links">9</a></sup>, however, supported international trade. India's merchandise exports also rebounded and grew by 17.8 per cent in H1:2026-27 (April-August). With merchandise imports growing at a higher pace than exports, the merchandise trade deficit expanded to US$147.1 billion in H1:2026-27 (April-August) from US$ 123.9 billion in H1:2025-26 (April-August) (<a href="#CH3_10" class="links">Chart III.10</a>).</p><p>Improvement in export performance was broad-based as the share of commodity groups with double-digit growth in H1:2026-27 (April-August) was 73.4 per cent vis-&agrave;-vis 16.2 per cent in H1:2025-26 (April-August). Petroleum products; engineering goods; electronic goods; organic and inorganic chemicals; and meat, dairy and poultry products were the main drivers of merchandise exports growth. Exports to the US remained resilient, primarily driven by electronic goods and engineering goods. Sectors that were a drag on exports included readymade garments of all textiles; ceramic products and glassware; fruits and vegetables; tobacco; and spices (<a href="#CH3_11" class="links">Chart III.11a and b</a>).</p>    <a id="CH3_10"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C310.jpg" alt="Chart III.10: Merchandise Trade" title="Chart III.10: Merchandise Trade"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH3_11"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C311.jpg" alt="Chart III.11: Merchandise Exports" title="Chart III.11: Merchandise Exports"></div>    <p>The growth in merchandise imports during H1:2026-27 (April-August) was driven by higher imports of electronic goods; petroleum, crude and products; machinery, electrical and non-electrical; fertilisers, crude and manufactured; and non-ferrous metals. By contrast, chemical material and products; pearls, precious and semi-precious stones; iron and steel; silver; and pulp and waste paper contributed negatively to import growth (<a href="#CH3_12" class="links">Chart III.12</a>). Gold imports at US$ 17.5 billion grew by 3.4 per cent during this period, while petroleum, crude and products imports grew by 22.4 per cent to US$ 95.6 billion, both reflecting the value impact due to steep rise in prices.</p>    <a id="CH3_12"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C312.jpg" alt="Chart III.12: Merchandise Imports" title="Chart III.12: Merchandise Imports"></div>    <p>Despite elevated trade uncertainty, both net terms of trade and income terms of trade improved in Q1:2026-27 vis-&agrave;-vis Q4: 2025-26, implying greater purchasing power of exports in terms of imports. As prices of key imports softened in June, net terms of trade in Q1 recouped from losses in the previous quarter (<a href="#CH3_13" class="links">Chart III.13</a>).</p>    <p>India&rsquo;s services exports growth remained robust at 10.9 per cent (y-o-y) in April-August 2026, led by software and business services (<a href="#CH3_14" class="links">Chart III.14</a>). India remains one of the top five service-exporting countries worldwide in terms of export growth in Q1:2026-27. Services imports also registered a strong growth in April-August 2026 on the back of higher transport and business services. Net services surplus increased by 7.3 per cent to US$ 85.8 billion in April-August 2026.</p>    <p>Despite significant widening of the goods trade deficit, the current account deficit (CAD) at US$ 4.2 billion (0.5 per cent of GDP) in Q1:2026-27 was marginally higher than its level of US$ 3.4 billion (0.4 per cent of GDP) in Q1:2025-26 (<a href="#CH3_15" class="links">Chart III.15</a>). Services and remittance receipts continued to support the current account. There were net capital outflows of US$ 3.9 billion in Q1, as against net inflows of US$ 7.9 billion last year.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Including errors and omissions."><a href="#C3FN10" class="links">10</a></sup> Consequently, India&rsquo;s foreign exchange reserves depleted by US$ 8.1 billion (BoP basis) during the quarter. As per preliminary estimates, CAD widened to US$ 7.0 billion in July 2026 from US$ 3.2 billion a year ago, driven by higher merchandise trade deficit and net income outgoes.</p>    <a id="CH3_13"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C313.jpg" alt="Chart III.13: Terms of Trade (Merchandise)" title="Chart III.13: Terms of Trade (Merchandise)"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH3_14"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C314.jpg" alt="Chart III.14: Services Trade" title="Chart III.14: Services Trade"></div>    <br>    <a id="CH3_15"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C315.jpg" alt="Chart III.15: Components of Current Account Balance " title="Chart III.15: Components of Current Account Balance "></div>    <p>India&rsquo;s gross inward foreign direct investment (FDI) and net FDI flows were higher in April-August 2026, both on y-o-y as well as sequential basis. A slowdown in the pace of outward FDI along with sustained higher gross FDI inflows supported net FDI flows. Financial services, communication services, computer services and manufacturing accounted for around 73 per cent of total FDI equity inflows during April-August 2026. In terms of origin, Singapore, Japan, Mauritius, The United Arab Emirates and the US were major sources of inward FDI to India, accounting for around 81 per cent of total inflows.</p>    <p>Foreign portfolio investment (FPI) registered net outflows of US$ 8.9 billion during 2026-27 (up to September). After recording sustained outflows during the first quarter following geopolitical tensions in Middle East, FPI turned positive during June-August 2026 supported by measures taken by the RBI and the Government (<a href="#T3_8" class="links">Table III.8</a>). FPI flows reversed again in September 2026 amidst rising global risk-off sentiment on re-escalation of Middle East tensions and rising US bond yields.</p>    <p>External commercial borrowing (ECB) agreements during April-August 2026 (P) increased significantly by 93.4 per cent (y-o-y) to US$ 30.4 billion, partly attributed to the RBI&rsquo;s concessional US dollar-rupee swap facility introduced in June 2026<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="On June 8, 2026, the Reserve Bank of India introduced a US Dollar-Rupee Forex Swap Facility at a fixed rate of 1.5 per cent per annum (compounded semi-annually) till December 31, 2026 for (i) ECB mobilisation by PSUs (other than banks) with an average maturity of three years and a maximum period of five years and (ii) overseas foreign currency borrowings (OFCBs) raised by Authorised Dealer Category I banks with a minimum maturity of three years and a maximum period of five years. Consequently, under this swap facility, gross inflows under OFCBs stood at US$ 5.3 billion till September 18, 2026."><a href="#C3FN11" class="links">11</a></sup>. Consequently, net ECB inflows to India<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Adjusted for intercompany borrowings and repayments."><a href="#C3FN12" class="links">12</a></sup> grew by 55.9 per cent to US$ 7.3 billion during April-August 2026 from US$ 4.7 billion a year ago. In terms of utilisation, on-lending/ sub-lending accounted for the highest share (28.0 per cent), followed by general corporate purpose (13.8 per cent) and refinancing of rupee loans (11.9 per cent) during this period. As at end-August 2026, about 73.0 per cent of outstanding ECBs (excluding rupee and inter-company borrowings) were hedged by the Indian companies.</p>    <p>Net inflows under non-resident deposits stood at US$ 119.2 billion during April-August 2026 (P), significantly higher than US$ 5.6 billion a year ago due to a surge in FCNR(B) deposits on account of RBI&rsquo;s forex swap facility. During June 8 - August 31, 2026, gross inflows under the FCNR(B) deposit scheme stood at US$ 133.0 billion<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="On August 14, 2026, RBI announced that the swap facility for the FCNR(B) deposits will be available for deposits mobilisation till August 31, 2026."><a href="#C3FN13" class="links">13</a></sup>. As of September 25, 2026, India&rsquo;s foreign exchange reserves stood at US$ 747.6 billion which is equivalent to 10.9 months of goods imports and 96.1 per cent of the external debt outstanding as at end-June 2026.</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C3_2"></a>III.2 Aggregate Supply</p>    <p>Aggregate supply &ndash; measured by real Gross Value Added (GVA) at basic prices &ndash; expanded by 8.2 per cent in Q1:2026-27 (8.7 per cent in the preceding quarter) despite headwinds after the onset of the West Asia conflict. Proactive measures undertaken by the Government to ensure adequate supply of key inputs supported growth in both manufacturing and services sectors (<a href="#CH3_16" class="links">Chart III.16a</a> and <a href="#T3_9" class="links">Table III.9</a>). Supply-side measures included prioritisation of domestic supply and inventories, diversification of import sources of key inputs and targeted interventions to support the most vulnerable sectors. Some of these measures were subsequently withdrawn as supply-side pressures began to ease by end of Q1. The seasonally adjusted momentum of GVA moderated in Q1:2026-27 from the previous quarter (<a href="#CH3_16" class="links">Chart III.16b</a>).</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="11" align="center" class="head"><a id="T3_8"></a>Table III.8: Net Foreign Direct and Portfolio Investment</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="11" align="right">(US$ billion)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="20%" rowspan="2">&nbsp;</td>        <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">2024-25</span></td>        <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26 (Provisional)</span></td>        <td colspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">2026-27 (Provisional)</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">Q1</span></td>        <td width="8%" align="center"><span class="head">