</td>        <td align="right">0.6</td>        <td align="right">0.6</td>        <td align="right">5.2</td>        <td align="right">7.8</td>        <td align="right">7.0</td>        <td align="right">8.7</td>        <td align="right">7.7</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Trade, hotels, transport, communication</td>        <td align="right">7.0</td>        <td align="right">11.3</td>        <td align="right">1.0</td>        <td align="right">1.6</td>        <td align="right">9.8</td>        <td align="right">10.6</td>        <td align="right">11.7</td>        <td align="right">12.9</td>        <td align="right">8.5</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Financial, real estate, ownership of dwelling, IT & professional services</td>        <td align="right">9.0</td>        <td align="right">10.5</td>        <td align="right">2.3</td>        <td align="right">2.7</td>        <td align="right">8.8</td>        <td align="right">9.9</td>        <td align="right">10.9</td>        <td align="right">12.3</td>        <td align="right">12.1</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Public administration, defence and other services</td>        <td align="right">5.1</td>        <td align="right">6.3</td>        <td align="right">0.7</td>        <td align="right">0.8</td>        <td align="right">4.6</td>        <td align="right">5.7</td>        <td align="right">6.6</td>        <td align="right">8.0</td>        <td align="right">7.5</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>GVA at basic prices</td>        <td align="right">7.1</td>        <td align="right">7.9</td>        <td align="right">7.1</td>        <td align="right">7.9</td>        <td align="right">7.0</td>        <td align="right">8.3</td>        <td align="right">7.6</td>        <td align="right">8.7</td>        <td align="right">8.2</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="10">Sources: NSO; and RBI staff estimates.</td>          </tr>      </table>    <br>    <a id="CH3_16"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C316.jpg" alt="Chart III.16: Gross Value Added Growth and its Constituents" title="Chart III.16: Gross Value Added Growth and its Constituents"></div>    <p class="head">III.2.1 Agriculture</p>    <p>Real GVA in agriculture and allied activities grew by 3.6 per cent in Q1:2026-27, lower than a year earlier. Robust growth in allied activities supported the overall growth in this sector. The shortfall and regional unevenness of Southwest monsoon and El Ni&ntilde;o effects may pose some risks to both kharif and rabi output, though non-farm rural activities have shown greater resilience in recent years (<a href="#B3_1" class="links">Box III.1</a>).</p>    <p>The Southwest monsoon, after remaining weak in June, gained significant momentum subsequently, covering the entire country by July 9, 2026. Monsoon remained 1.0 per cent above the long period average (LPA) in July and supported kharif sowing. As of September 30, 2026, the cumulative rainfall was 12.6 per cent below the Long Period Average (<a href="#CH3_17" class="links">Chart III.17a</a>). In terms of spatial distribution, rainfall remained deficient compared to the LPA across all regions. As of September 30, 2026, the production-weighted rainfall index (PRN) stood at 90 per cent, suggesting a skewed rainfall distribution in major crop producing states (<a href="#CH3_17" class="links">Chart III.17b</a>). As of October 1, 2026, reservoir levels at 71.8 per cent remained below normal level<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Normal reservoir level refers to average live capacity of previous 10 years."><a href="#C3FN14" class="links">14</a></sup> (81.8 per cent) and last year&rsquo;s level (90.3 per cent), due to erratic monsoon (<a href="#CH3_17" class="links">Chart III.17c</a>).</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td><p align="center" class="head"><a id="B3_1"></a>Box III.1: Indian Agricultural Sector amidst Weather Shocks</p>            <p>Rainfall shortfalls remain a major driver of year-to-year fluctuation in agricultural output. However, the rural economy is increasingly cushioned from erratic monsoon by resilient allied sector activities. Chand and Singh (2026) observe that for agricultural households possessing land up to one acre, wages contribute more than 55 per cent of household income, while crop production and livestock together contribute 36 per cent. This suggests that rural household incomes are increasingly supported by activities beyond crop production.</p>            <p>Although annual estimates of value added are not available separately for rural and urban areas, historical benchmark estimates support the use of a broad rural-linked proxy. In 1999-2000, agriculture, forestry and fishing, mining and quarrying, and construction together accounted for about 59 per cent of total rural net domestic product (Central Statistical Organisation, 2007). This exercise therefore uses the sum of GVA of these sectors as an indicative proxy for rural-linked activity. Data available for both base years (i.e., 2011-12 and 2022-23) suggest that the non-agricultural component has become more important over time (<a href="#CH3_1_1" class="links">Chart III.1.1</a>). The growth-rate analysis below is calculated within the relevant published GVA base, with 2023-24 onward using the 2022-23-base series.</p>            <p>Rainfall sensitivity differs across the two components of the proxy, viz., agricultural and non-agricultural (<a href="#CH3_1_2" class="links">Chart III.1.2a</a>). Agricultural growth suffers more in years of higher monsoon deficit, while growth in the non-agricultural component stays close to 6 per cent at every cut-off. In years of surplus rainfall, by contrast, agricultural growth averages 5.1 per cent, above the 4.3 per cent recorded in years close to the LPA. It is estimated that non-agricultural activity was the predominant contributor to growth in rural economic activity during years of rainfall deficiency exceeding 7 per cent, as against in other years.</p>            <a id="CH3_1_1"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C31_1.jpg" alt="Chart III.1.1: Non-agricultural Share of the Rural Activity" title="Chart III.1.1: Non-agricultural Share of the Rural Activity"></div>            <p>The same contrast is evident in the regression estimates (<a href="#CH3_1_2" class="links">Chart III.1.2b</a>). Over 1994-95 to 2025-26, a regression of annual growth of agriculture and allied activities on the rainfall shortfall from the long-period average gives a coefficient of (&ndash;) 0.40, statistically significant at 1 per cent. The shortfall measure explains about 39 per cent of the variation in agricultural growth. For the non-agricultural component, the coefficient is 0.01 but not statistically significant (<a href="#CH3_1_2" class="links">Chart III.1.2b</a>). The pattern is also stable across two equal sub-periods. Including a separate term for rainfall above the LPA leaves the shortfall coefficient for agriculture largely unchanged at (&ndash;)0.44, while the excess-rainfall term is small and not statistically significant at (&ndash;)0.12. A formal test rejects a symmetric response to deficient and surplus rainfall for agriculture (p value = 0.04) but not for the non-agricultural component. The asymmetry is not an artefact of the censoring implicit in the shortfall measure. The evidence on the declining role of rainfall in crop production is in line with Gupta et al. 2023. The factors that seem to have reduced the sensitivity of agricultural activity to rainfall shortfall include increased irrigation coverage, new weather resilient crop varieties, and diversification towards less water-intensive crops. At the same time, the composition of agricultural output has shifted towards allied activities. Over 1999-2000 to 2024-25, the coefficient for agriculture and allied GVA is (&ndash;)0.44 and significant at 1 per cent while the coefficients for milk, eggs, meat and fish as well as non-agricultural component are small and statistically insignificant (<a href="#CH3_1_3" class="links">Chart III.1.3a</a>). These estimates suggest that growth in these activities is less closely related to aggregate rainfall shortfalls than agricultural GVA. Rolling correlations of the absolute deviation of rainfall from the LPA with growth in agricultural GVA and the rural-linked activity proxy have generally become less negative since the mid-2000s (irrespective of the window-length), while remaining below zero throughout (<a href="#CH3_1_3" class="links">Charts III.1.3b</a>).</p>            <a id="CH3_1_2"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C31_2.jpg" alt="Chart III.1.2 A and Chart III.1.2 B" title="Chart III.1.2 A and Chart III.1.2 B"></div>            <br>            <a id="CH3_1_3"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C31_3.jpg" alt="Chart III.1.3 A and Chart III.1.3 B (i) and Chart III.1.3 B (ii)" title="Chart III.1.3 A and Chart III.1.3 B (i) and Chart III.1.3 B (ii)"></div>            <p>Summing up, empirical findings suggest that while rainfall shortfalls remain important for agricultural output, their association with allied activities and other rural-linked activity has moderated over time. Diversification across rural-linked activity and within agriculture has made the rural economy more resilient to rainfall shortfall.</p>            <p class="head">References:</p>            <p>Chand, R., and J. Singh (2026). &ldquo;Agriculture for Viksit Bharat: Strategies for Sustainable Growth and Transformation.&rdquo; ICRIER Working Paper 434, July.</p>            <p>Gupta, Kashyap; Sunil Kumar and Sarthak Gulati (2023), &ldquo;Agriculture&rsquo;s Dependency on Monsoon Rainfall in India&rdquo;, RBI Bulletin, August.</p></td>          </tr>      </table>    <p>As of October 2, 2026, kharif sowing was down by 1.0 per cent as compared to last year, while it remained above normal level by 1.0 per cent. The decline in kharif acreage was on account of lower sowing in rice, sugarcane and cotton, while sowing increased in pulses, coarse cereals and oilseeds (<a href="#CH3_17" class="links">Chart III.17d</a>). Rabi production may also face headwinds from low reservoir level due to deficient monsoon and above-normal temperatures associated with El Ni&ntilde;o.</p>    <a id="CH3_17"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C317.jpg" alt="Chart III.17: Progress of Rainfall and Kharif Sowing" title="Chart III.17: Progress of Rainfall and Kharif Sowing"></div>    <p>According to the third advance estimates of crops production for 2025-26, all major crops, except cotton, recorded an increase in production. Foodgrain output grew by 5.3 per cent to a record 3,766 lakh tonnes in 2025-26 (<a href="#T3_10" class="links">Table III.10</a>). As per the second advance estimates, the production of horticultural crops reached 3,778 lakh tonnes in 2025-26, i.e., 1.9 per cent higher than the final estimates for 2024-25, driven primarily by higher output of banana; mangoes; potato; tomato; and non-TOP vegetables. The Government announced Minimum Support Prices (MSP) for kharif crops for the 2026-27 marketing season and rabi crops for 2027-28 marketing season. The increase in MSP ranged between 0.1-8.8 per cent across various kharif crops and 1.0-10.3 per cent across various rabi crops, with an intent to promote crop diversification and address demand-supply imbalances.</p>    <p class="head">III.2.2 Industry</p>    <p>Industrial sector recorded a growth of 7.7 per cent in Q1:2026-27, which is higher than the previous quarter as well as a year ago. Real GVA growth in industry mainly reflected robust pace of activity in manufacturing and electricity segments, while the mining sector contracted in Q1 (<a href="#CH3_18" class="links">Chart III.18a</a>). A broad-based recovery was observed in the manufacturing sector with corporate manufacturing recording healthy revenue growth in Q1 amid rising cost pressures (<a href="#CH3_18" class="links">Chart III.18b</a>).</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="6" align="center" class="head"><a id="T3_10"></a>Table III.10: Agricultural Production in 2025-26</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="6" align="right">(Lakh tonnes)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="30%" rowspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Crop</span></td>        <td width="14%" rowspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">2024-25 Final</span></td>        <td colspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26</span></td>        <td colspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Variation in 2025-26 (Per cent)</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="14%" align="center"><span class="head">SAE</span></td>        <td width="14%" align="center"><span class="head">TAE</span></td>        <td width="14%" align="center"><span class="head">Over SAE 2025-26*</span></td>        <td width="14%" align="center"><span class="head">Over Final 2024-25</span></td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Foodgrains</td>        <td align="right">3,577.3</td>        <td align="right">3,486.6</td>        <td align="right">3,765.6</td>        <td align="right">2.3</td>        <td align="right">5.3</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Kharif</td>        <td align="right">1,694.6</td>        <td align="right">1,741.4</td>        <td align="right">1,760.4</td>        <td align="right">1.1</td>        <td align="right">3.9</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td>Rabi</td>        <td align="right">1,691.7</td>        <td align="right">1,745.1</td>        <td align="right">1,805.1</td>        <td align="right">3.4</td>        <