.0</td>        <td align="right">12.7</td>        <td align="right">6.8</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="6">Notes: *: SAE covers production of Kharif and Rabi crops only. Growth is calculated accordingly.<br>          #: Lakh bales of 170 kgs each; ##: Lakh bales of 180 kgs each.<br>          SAE: Second Advance Estimates. TAE: Third Advance Estimates.<br>          Sources: Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare, GoI.</td>          </tr>      </table>    <br><a id="CH3_18"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C318.jpg" alt="Chart III.18: Industrial GVA Growth" title="Chart III.18: Industrial GVA Growth"></div>    <p>The index of industrial production (IIP) registered a healthy expansion of 6.7 per cent in April-August 2026 vis-&agrave;-vis 4.2 per cent in April-August 2025 (<a href="#T3_11" class="links">Table III.11</a>). Manufacturing and electricity segments grew at a robust pace. Sectors having two-thirds of total weight within manufacturing reported higher production. Electrical, other transport equipment; motor vehicle; rubber & plastic products; and computer, electronics & optical products contributed to manufacturing growth. Mining sector slipped into contraction due to decline in fuel minerals; non-metallic minerals; and minor minerals.</p>    <p>Activity in electricity, gas, water supply, and other utility services grew robustly in Q1:2026-27, after a prolonged phase of moderate growth. As per IIP data, electricity generation grew by 10.2 per cent during April-August 2026, while gas supply contracted marginally, probably reflecting input disruptions due to the West Asia Crisis. Electricity power generation from thermal; nuclear; and renewable sources reported higher production in April-July 2026, while hydro power generation contracted due to erratic weather conditions that reduced reservoir levels. In terms of regional demand, Southern region recorded a double-digit increase in electri