            <td align="right">-0.7</td>            <td align="right">2.6</td>            <td align="right">3.2</td>            <td align="right">-1.5</td>            <td align="right">3.3</td>            <td align="right">-0.3</td>            <td align="right">1.9</td>            <td align="right">8.2</td>            <td align="right">9.8</td>            <td align="right">12.7</td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td colspan="12">Sources: Central Electricity Authority (CEA); and Power System Operation Corporation Limited (POSOCO).</td>          </tr>        </table>        <p>Manufacturing purchasing managers&rsquo; index (PMI) remained in expansionary zone in H1:2026-27, although it moderated from H2:2025-26. The optimism in the new export orders inched up in September, whereas the outlook on the new orders remained steady. Stocks of finished goods increased, suggesting inventory build-up by manufacturing companies. The cost pressure on manufacturing firms remained elevated (<a href="#CH3_19" class="links">Chart III.19a</a>).</p>        <p class="head">III.2.3 Services</p>        <p>Services sector expanded by 9.7 per cent and contributed nearly three-fourth to overall GVA growth in Q1:2026-27. The robust activity in services was also evident in PMI services, which remained in expansionary zone in Q2 (<a href="#CH3_19" class="links">Chart III.19b</a>). The momentum was driven by an upsurge in financial, real estate and professional services and trade, hotels, transport, communication, and services related to broadcasting (<a href="#CH3_20" class="links">Chart III.20a</a>). Construction activity also recorded a healthy growth of 7.7 per cent in Q1. Among the high frequency indicators of construction, cement production expanded at a robust pace during April-August 2026, whereas steel consumption growth moderated sequentially in July and August 2026 from its level in Q1 (<a href="#CH3_20" class="links">Chart III.20b</a>).</p>        <a id="CH3_19"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C319.jpg" alt="Chart III.19: PMI Manufacturing and Services" title="Chart III.19: PMI Manufacturing and Services"></div>        <br>    <a id="CH3_20"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C320.jpg" alt="Chart III.20: Services Sector" title="Chart III.20: Services Sector"></div>    <p>The growth in the real GVA of trade, hotels, transport, communication, and broadcasting services remained resilient at 8.5 per cent in Q1:2026-27 (12.9 per cent in Q4:2025-26). Among the transport indicators, domestic air traffic contracted mainly due to airlines scaling back their operations in response to surge in aviation turbine fuel price and pass-through of fuel prices impacting domestic travel. On the other hand, commercial vehicle sales and toll collection volume, indicators of road transport services, continued to grow at a robust pace in H1:2026-27 (April-July). After muted growth in Q1, gross GST collections surged by 15.4 per cent in July and 14.8 per cent in August 2026 indicating resilient business activity.</p>    <p>Financial, real estate and professional services grew by 12.1 per cent in Q1:2026-27, contributing a major part to services sector growth (51.6 per cent) as well as to aggregate growth (37.0 per cent). Bank credit and deposit growth remained buoyant in Q2. Life insurance premium growth remained in double-digit in April-July 2026 (<a href="#T3_13" class="links">Table III.13</a>). All these indicators suggest a continued resilient growth in financial services during Q2.</p>    <p>Corporate sector performance in services sector improved in Q1:2026-27. Both GVA and operating profits of information technology (IT) firms improved, partly supported by favourable exchange rate movements. Non-IT services firms also recorded strong growth in both GVA and operating profits for the second successive quarter in Q1:2026-27 (<a href="#CH3_21" class="links">Chart III.21</a>).</p>    <p>Growth in public administration, defence, and other services (PADO) &ndash; which also include education, health, recreation, and other personal services &ndash; grew at a stronger pace in Q1:2026-27 than a year ago. The centre&rsquo;s revenue expenditure, excluding interest payments and subsidies, expanded by 11.4 per cent during April-July 2026.</p>    <p>Services PMI dropped to 54.2 (average) in Q2:2026-27 from 58.7 in Q1:2026-27, albeit it remained in expansionary zone. Although new export business maintained similar optimism, outlook in new business worsened (<a href="#CH3_19" class="links">Chart III.19b</a>). After declining for two months from May 2026, the composite PMI rose to 55.9 in September 2026, compared to 54.3 in the previous month. Improvement in composite PMI was on the back of stronger increase in new order volumes reported by the private sector, though the pace of growth remained below the trend recorded in recent years.</p>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="9" align="center" class="head"><a id="T3_13"></a>Table III.13: Services Sector Indicators</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="9" align="right">(Y-o-y growth, per cent)</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td width="44%">&nbsp;</td>        <td colspan="4" align="center" class="head">2025-26</td>        <td colspan="4" align="center" class="head">2026-27</td>          </tr>      <tr>        <td align="center" class="head">Indicators</td>        <td width="7%" align="center" class="head">Q1</td>        <td width="7%" align="center" class="head">Q2</td>        <td width="7%" align="center" class="head">Q3</td>        <td width="7%" align="center" class="head">Q4</td>        <td width="7%" align="center" class="head">Q1</td>        <td width="7%" align="center" class="head">Jul</td>        <td width="7%" align="center" class="head">Aug</td>        <td width="