ntroduced a US Dollar-Rupee Forex Swap Facility at a fixed rate of 1.5 per cent per annum (compounded semi-annually) till December 31, 2026 for (i) ECB mobilisation by PSUs (other than banks) with an average maturity of three years and a maximum period of five years and (ii) overseas foreign currency borrowings (OFCBs) raised by Authorised Dealer Category I banks with a minimum maturity of three years and a maximum period of five years. Consequently, under this swap facility, gross inflows under OFCBs stood at US$ 5.3 billion till September 18, 2026.</p>    <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C3FN12"></a>12</sup> Adjusted for intercompany borrowings and repayments.</p>    <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C3FN13"></a>13</sup> On August 14, 2026, RBI announced that the swap facility for the FCNR(B) deposits will be available for deposits mobilisation till August 31, 2026.</p>    <p class="footnote"><sup><a id="C3FN14"></a>14</sup> Normal reservoir level refers to average live capacity of previous 10 years.</p><hr><p class="head" align="center"><a id="C4"></a>IV. Prices and Costs</p>    <p><em>Headline CPI inflation increased sequentially, rising above the target during June-August driven by a sustained positive price momentum. Higher food and fuel inflation led to this increase, reflecting adverse weather conditions and the sharp rise in energy prices following the West Asia conflict. Core inflation registered a pick-up with signs of generalisation in recent months. Going ahead, persistent supply-side pressures in food and volatile global energy prices pose risks to the domestic inflation outlook, particularly through second-round effects amid strong growth impulses.</em></p>    <p class="head"><a id="C4_1"></a>IV.1 Introduction</p>    <p>The headline consumer price index (CPI) inflation<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Headline inflation is measured by year-on-year (y-o-y) changes in the all-India consumer price index (CPI) published by the National Statistics Office (NSO), Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI), Government of India."><a href="#F1" class="links">1</a></sup> has been increasing steadily in the period since the release of the previous MPR in April 2026. From 3.4 per cent in March 2026, headline inflation increased over successive months to 4.4 per cent in June (the first print above the inflation target in seventeen months) and further to 4.8 per cent in August (<a href="#C41" class="links">Chart IV.1a</a>).</p>    <p>The sequential pick-up in inflation witnessed since March was driven by a strong price momentum, which more than offset the favourable base effects in each month (<a href="#C41" class="links">Chart IV.1b</a>).<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="A change in CPI year-on-year (y-o-y) inflation between any two months is approximately the difference between the current month-on-month (m-o-m) change in the price index (momentum) and the m-o-m change in the price index 12 months earlier (base effect). For more details, see Box I.1 of the MPR of September 2014."><a href="#F2" class="links">2</a></sup> The momentum has been particularly strong since May.</p>    <p>Among the major sub-components, food and fuel recorded sharp increases in y-o-y inflation over March levels (<a href="#C42" class="links">Chart IV.2a</a>). Reflecting this, the contribution of food and fuel sub-components to headline inflation increased significantly (<a href="#C42" class="links">Chart IV.2b</a>). The pick-up in food inflation was driven by unfavourable weather conditions and increasing fuel prices brought about by the sharp upturn in global energy prices following the West Asia conflict. Core inflation (CPI excluding food and fuel) also registered a pick-up, albeit by a lower magnitude upto July, reflecting limited pass-through of supply side pressures. In August core inflation accelerated to 4.2 per cent.</p>    <a id="C41"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C41.jpg" alt="Chart IV.1: CPI Headline Inflation" title="Chart IV.1: CPI Headline Inflation"></div><br>    <a id="C42"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C42.jpg" alt="Chart IV.2: Major Sub-components within the CPI" title="Chart IV.2: Major Sub-components within the CPI"></div>    <p>The April MPR projections had factored in risks from the West Asia conflict and adverse weather conditions as part of the baseline forecast. Reflecting this, the actual inflation outcomes remained broadly in line with the projections (<a href="#C43" class="links">Chart IV.3</a>).<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) Act, 1934 (amended in 2016) enjoins the RBI to set out deviations of actual inflation outcomes from projections, if any, and explain the underlying reasons thereof."><a href="#F3" class="links">3</a></sup> Inflation for Q2:2026-27 so far (July-August) has turned out to be about 20 basis points (bps) higher than the quarterly average projection, as food price pressures have accentuated in recent months.</p>    <p>The remainder of the chapter delves deeper into component-wise developments in inflation, statistical properties of observed inflation trends as well as determinants of inflation.</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C4_2"></a>IV.2 Developments across Major Components of the CPI</p>    <p class="head">CPI Food and Beverages</p>    <p>Food and beverages inflation increased from 3.7 per cent in March to 5.7 per cent in August 2026, driven by items other than vegetables (<a href="#C44" class="links">Chart IV.4</a>).</p>    <a id="C43"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C43.jpg" alt="Chart IV.3: CPI Inflation: Projection versus Actuals" title="Chart IV.3: CPI Inflation: Projection versus Actuals"></div><br>    <a id="C44"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C44.jpg" alt="Chart IV.4: Drivers of CPI Food andBeverages Inflation" title="Chart IV.4: Drivers of CPI Food and"></div>    <p>Of the 200-bps increase in food inflation in August over March, nearly 48 per cent was contributed by two sub-components, viz., animal protein<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Animal protein in the CPI basket includes meat, fish, milk and dairy products and eggs. Together, they have a weight of 10.8 per cent in the CPI(2024=100) and 29.4 per cent in the ‘Food and beverages’ division."><a href="#F4" class="links">4</a></sup> and spices<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="‘Spices, culinary herbs and seeds’ subclass has weight of 2.3 per cent in CPI (2024=100) and 6.2 per cent weight in the ‘Food and beverages’ division."><a href="#F5" class="links">5</a></sup>, which together have a weight of about 36 per cent within the food and beverages division. The increase in inflation in animal protein primarily reflected rising input costs (<a href="#C45" class="links">Chart IV.5a</a>). Higher feed and fodder costs, high transportation and packaging outlays, elevated energy prices, and heatwave-induced bird mortality contributed to the firming up of prices (<a href="#C45" class="links">Chart IV.5b</a>). Inflation in spices rose steadily since January 2026 and touched 13.0 per cent in August, after being in deflation for a prolonged period.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Spices category recorded deflation for eighteen months till December 2025."><a href="#F6" class="links">6</a></sup> Ginger, dry chillies and coriander contributed the most to the increase.</p>    <p>The emergence of El Ni&ntilde;o conditions and deficient southwest monsoon have raised risks to overall food price outlook. However, robust domestic production in the previous year, comfortable foodgrain stocks<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="As on September 16, 2026, the rice and wheat stocks stood at 584.1 lakh tonnes (4.3 times the buffer norm) and 474.5 lakh tonnes (1.7 times the buffernorm), respectively."><a href="#F7" class="links">7</a></sup>, conducive trade policy measures and proactive supply management provided some cushion, keeping the cumulative price build-up so far broadly in line with that of non-El Ni&ntilde;o years (<a href="#C46" class="links">Chart IV.6</a>).</p>    <a id="C45"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C45.jpg" alt="Chart IV.5: Animal Protein Inflation" title="Chart IV.5: Animal Protein Inflation"></div><br>    <a id="C46"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C46.jpg" alt="Chart IV.6: Price Build-up in CPI Food andBeverages" title="Chart IV.6: Price Build-up in CPI Food and"></div>    <p>Among the major products, inflation in cereals and products<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="‘Cereals and cereal products’ class in the CPI (2024=100) series has a weight of 5.9 per cent in the CPI and 16.2 per cent in the ‘Food and beverages’division."><a href="#F8" class="links">8</a></sup> increased to 2.8 per cent in August 2026 from -0.2 per cent in April amidst a pickup in rice prices on account of lower kharif sowing (<a href="#C47" class="links">Chart IV.7a</a>). The build-up in cereals prices during 2026-27 (up to August) has also been above the historical average (<a href="#C47" class="links">Chart IV.7b</a>). Pulses<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="In the CPI (2024=100) series, the sub-class ‘Pulses’ belongs to the broader class of ‘Vegetables, tubers, plantains, cooking bananas and pulses’ and has a weight of 1.5 per cent in the CPI and 4.0 per cent in the ‘Food and beverages’ division. Together with the items ‘besan and gram products’ and ‘other pulse products’, these weights constitute 1.6 per cent in the CPI and 4.5 per cent in the food and beverages division. In August 2026, the ‘pulses’ subclassregistered 3.6 per cent inflation, and together with other pulse products, it registered 3.2 per cent inflation."><a href="#F9" class="links">9</a></sup> recorded a modest increase in y-o-y inflation, largely reflecting unfavourable base effects after remaining in deflation through most of 2025-26. Imports of key pulses from Myanmar, Brazil and Australia, along with government interventions in retail markets, helped keep prices at moderate levels.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="According to the Directorate General of Commercial Intelligence and Statistics (DGCI&S), pulses imports increased by nearly 63 per cent to 1.7 million metric tonnes during April-July 2026, from 1.0 million metric tonnes in the corresponding period of the previous year."><a href="#F10" class="links">10</a></sup></p>    <p>Inflation in oils and fats was lower at 7.8 per cent by August 2026 as compared to over 20 per cent a year ago (<a href="#C48" class="links">Chart IV.8a</a>). Despite the moderation, elevated global edible oil prices amid increased diversion towards biofuel production in major producing economies continue to pose pressure on domestic prices. Domestically, tighter mustard seed arrivals amid steady export demand also kept mustard oil prices elevated (<a href="#C48" class="links">Chart IV.8b</a>). However, reduction in basic import duty on crude palm, soybean and sunflower oils is likely to soften domestic prices.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="‘Oils and fats’ class in the CPI (2024=100) series has a weight of 2.7 per cent in overall CPI and 7.5 per cent in the ‘Food and beverages’ division. Government reduced the basic import duty on crude palm and crude soybean oils from 10 per cent to 5 per cent and on crude sunflower oil from 10 per cent to zero per cent, effective from September 24, 2026."><a href="#F11" class="links">11</a></sup></p>    <a id="C47"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C47.jpg" alt="Chart IV.7: Cereals and Products" title="Chart IV.7: Cereals and Products"></div><br>    <a id="C48"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C48.jpg" alt="Chart IV.8: Oils and Fats" title="Chart IV.8: Oils and Fats"></div>    <p>Vegetables<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="‘Vegetables’ in the CPI (2024=100) series has a weight of 5.2 per cent in the CPI and is constructed using the class ‘vegetables, tubers, plantains, cooking bananas and pulses’ by excluding the sub-class pulses and related items, i.e., ‘Besan and gram products’, and ‘other pulse products’. It has a weight of 14.1 per cent in the ‘Food and beverages’ division."><a href="#F12" class="links">12</a></sup> inflation remained moderate, averaging 3.5 per cent (y-o-y) in H1:2026-27 so far (April-August 2026), supported by improved production and robust market arrivals.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="As per AGMARKNET, mandi arrivals of major vegetables increased by 26.6 per cent y-o-y during April–September 2026, with arrivals of TOP (tomato, onion and potato) increasing by 7.3 per cent. The second advance estimates released by Department of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare indicated that the vegetable production increased by 1.5 per cent in 2025-26, supported by increases in both area and yield, with higher output of potato, tomato, peas and okra."><a href="#F13" class="links">13</a></sup> The price build-up in the Tomato, Onion and Potato (TOP) category was broadly aligned with its historical seasonal pattern while the increase in non-TOP vegetable prices remained below that observed in both El Ni&ntilde;o and non-El Ni&ntilde;o years (<a href="#C49" class="links">Chart IV.9</a>). </p>    <a id="C49"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C49.jpg" alt="Chart IV.9: Price Build-up in Vegetables" title="Chart IV.9: Price Build-up in Vegetables"></div>    <p>The impact of the deficient<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="As on September 30, 2026, cumulative Southwest Monsoon rainfall was about 13 per cent below long period average (LPA)."><a href="#F14" class="links">14</a></sup> and uneven spatial rainfall distribution and lower kharif sowing<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="As on October 2, 2026, kharif sowing was 1.0 per cent lower than a year ago, with rice sowing declining by 3.5 per cent over the corresponding period of last year."><a href="#F15" class="links">15</a></sup> on food prices is reflected in the recent high frequency retail price data in certain food items such as sugar and onion. Sugar<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="‘Sugar, confectionery and desserts’ class has a weight of 1.4 per cent in CPI (2024=100) and 3.9 per cent weight in ‘Food and beverages’ division."><a href="#F16" class="links">16</a></sup> prices increased by about 34 per cent since the first week of July to reach &#8377;65/ kg by late August, due to lower than anticipated domestic production, lower carry-over stocks, and precautionary stocking by market intermediaries<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Lower production was due to crop diseases and waterlogging, and precautionary stocking that took place amid concerns over the upcoming season’s yield on the back of uneven rainfall (Source:https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseDetail.aspx?PRID=2302018&reg=3&lang=1)."><a href="#F17" class="links">17</a></sup> (<a href="#C410" class="links">Chart IV.10</a>). The government undertook targeted measures such as export restrictions, stock limits on dealers and bulk consumers, duty-free imports of up to 10 lakh tonnes of raw sugar, physical verification of stocks, and advancing the sugarcane crushing season to augment domestic availability and contain speculative pressures. Prices have since retreated in September.</p>    <a id="C410"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C410.jpg" alt="Chart IV.10: Retail Sugar Prices" title="Chart IV.10: Retail Sugar Prices"></div>    <p>Onion prices have increased by about 84 per cent since end-June (up to September 30) due to depletion/ some spoilage of stored rabi stocks and concerns over the timing and quality of kharif arrivals amid uneven rainfall (<a href="#C411" class="links">Chart IV.11</a>). Although onion prices exhibit a seasonal uptick during August-September before the arrival of the kharif crops, the magnitude of increase has been much higher this year than the historical average.</p>    <a id="C411"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C411.jpg" alt="Chart IV.11: Retail Onion Prices" title="Chart IV.11: Retail Onion Prices"></div><br>    <a id="C412"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C412.jpg" alt="Chart IV.12: Global Energy Markets" title="Chart IV.12: Global Energy Markets"></div>    <p class="head">CPI Fuel</p>    <p>Global crude oil prices ratcheted up since March 2026 along with an increase in the premium for refined products on account of extreme market tightness (<a href="#C412" class="links">Chart IV.12</a>). Reflecting the partial pass-through of this increase, CPI fuel<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Fuel represents household fuel, i.e., the group ‘Electricity, gas and other fuels’; and transport fuel, i.e., the class ‘Fuels and lubricants for personal transport equipment’ which includes ‘petrol’, ‘diesel’, and ‘other natural gas (CNG)’."><a href="#F18" class="links">18</a></sup> inflation went up from 0.4 per cent in April to 5.2 per cent in August.</p>    <p>Among household fuel items included in CPI, domestic liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) prices were revised upwards by &#8377;60 per cylinder in early March 2026 and by a further &#8377;29 per cylinder in June, resulting in a cumulative increase of around 10.3 per cent. Kerosene and other fuel items also witnessed an increase in prices. Electricity prices, however, recorded a decline on a y-o-y basis on account of reduction in tariffs and provision of free or subsidised electricity in certain states (C<a href="#C413" class="links">hart IV.13a</a>).</p>    <p>Within transport fuel, retail prices of petrol and diesel were increased in four successive rounds during the month of May leading to a 7.5 per cent increase for petrol and 8.4 per cent for diesel (<a href="#C413" class="links">Chart IV.13b</a>). The magnitude of domestic price increases was however moderate as compared to the increase in global prices (<a href="#C414" class="links">Chart IV.14</a>). Part of the shock was absorbed by the Government by reducing the Special Additional Excise Duty on petrol and diesel by &#8377;10 per litre each in March to ease the burden on domestic consumers.</p>    <p>After the initial surge, global oil prices have remained highly volatile with sharp two-way movements triggered by geopolitical developments. Amidst renewed uncertainty about the US-Iran negotiations, Indian basket crude oil prices increased from US$82 per barrel in July to around US$116 per barrel in September.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The Indian basket of Crude Oil (ICB) represents a derived basket comprising of Sweet grade (Brent Dated) and Sour grade (Oman and Dubai average) of crude oil imported by Indian refineries during each month. ICB Ratio for July was 79.40:20.60, August 74.94:25.06 and September 77.81:22.19."><a href="#F19" class="links">19</a></sup> Tightness in refined product markets also continues with low diesel inventories, higher stock drawdowns and the crude futures curve in backwardation.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Market conditions where the current spot price of a commodity is higher than futures prices, indicating higher current demand or shortages of supply for a commodity vis-a-vis the future."><a href="#F20" class="links">20</a></sup> These conditions, along with elevated refined product margins, pose upside risks to domestic fuel prices if geopolitical disruptions persist.</p>    <a id="C413"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C413.jpg" alt="Chart IV.13: CPI Fuel Inflation" title="Chart IV.13: CPI Fuel Inflation"></div><br>    <a id="C414"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C414.jpg" alt="Chart IV.14: Domestic and International Prices" title="Chart IV.14: Domestic and International Prices"></div>    <p class="head">Core CPI (CPI excluding Food and Fuel)</p>    <p>Core inflation (CPI excluding food and fuel)<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="In the current CPI (2024=100) series, CPI excluding food and fuel is estimated by excluding the division ‘food and beverages’, the group ‘electricity, gas, other fuels’, and the class ‘Fuels and lubricants for personal transport equipment’ from overall CPI."><a href="#F21" class="links">21</a></sup> increased from 3.7 per cent in April to 4.2 per cent in August 2026. Core inflation excluding precious metals<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Precious metals in the current CPI (2024=100) basket include the items ‘Gold/diamond/platinum jewellery’ and ‘Silver jewellery’."><a href="#F22" class="links">22</a></sup> registered a sharper increase from 2.2 per cent to 2.9 per cent during the same period (<a href="#T41" class="links">Table IV.1</a>). Precious metals, which are part of the &lsquo;personal care and effects&rsquo; sub-group, were a major driver of core inflation over the last year. During April-December 2025, gold and silver prices rose by 41.6 per cent and 66.3 per cent, respectively, accounting for about one-third of the overall core inflation during this period. After peaking in January 2026, precious metal prices have remained range-bound.</p>    <p>In terms of sub-components, housing, restaurants and accommodation services, and clothing and footwear were the other major contributors to core inflation (<a href="#C415" class="links">Chart IV.15</a>).</p>    <table width="85%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head"><a id="T41"></a>Table IV.1: Exclusion-based Measures of Inflation<br>          (Y-o-y, per cent)</span></td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td width="40%" align="center"><span class="head">Period</span></td>        <td width="30%" align="center"><span class="head">CPI excluding food and fuel</span></td>        <td width="30%" align="center"><span class="head">CPI excluding food, fuel and precious metals</span></td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Jul-25</td>        <td align="right">4.2</td>        <td align="right">3.2</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Aug-25</td>        <td align="right">4.3</td>        <td align="right">3.1</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Sep-25</td>        <td align="right">4.5</td>        <td align="right">3.1</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Oct-25</td>        <td align="right">4.5</td>        <td align="right">2.6</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Nov-25</td>        <td align="right">4.4</td>        <td align="right">2.4</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Dec-25</td>        <td align="right">4.8</td>        <td align="right">2.4</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Jan-26</td>        <td align="right">3.7</td>        <td align="right">2.1</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Feb-26</td>        <td align="right">3.7</td>        <td align="right">2.1</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Mar-26</td>        <td align="right">3.7</td>        <td align="right">2.1</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Apr-26</td>        <td align="right">3.7</td>        <td align="right">2.2</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>May-26</td>        <td align="right">3.9</td>        <td align="right">2.3</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Jun-26</td>        <td align="right">3.9</td>        <td align="right">2.5</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Jul-26</td>        <td align="right">3.9</td>        <td align="right">2.7</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Aug-26</td>        <td align="right">4.2</td>        <td align="right">2.9</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="3" align="justify">Note: Inflation numbers from July 2025 to December 2025 are based on the old CPI (2012=100) series, while those from January 2026 to August 2026 are from the current CPI (2024=100) series.<br>          Sources: NSO; and RBI staff estimates.</td>      </tr>    </table><br>    <a id="C415"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C415.jpg" alt="Chart IV.15: Contribution to CPI Core Inflation" title="Chart IV.15: Contribution to CPI Core Inflation"></div>    <p>The pick-up in inflation up to July was primarily on account of higher inflation within goods category whereas services inflation remained moderate (<a href="#C416" class="links">Chart IV.16a</a>). In August, however, services inflation also increased, indicating broadening of price pressures (<a href="#C416" class="links">Chart IV.16b</a>).</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C4_3"></a>IV.3 Decoding the Inflation Dynamics</p>    <p>Statistical properties</p>    <p>Statistical properties provide useful insights into the nature of the inflation process, in terms of how generalised inflation is, and whether the underlying inflation differs from headline numbers. The distribution of CPI inflation on an average basis during 2026-27 so far (April-August) vis-&agrave;-vis 2025-26 indicates a positive skew, reflecting the disproportionately large contribution from precious metals for most months (<a href="#C417" class="links">Chart IV.17</a>).</p>    <p>In terms of the monthly trajectory, the increase in inflation during April-August 2026 was accompanied by a widening of inflation divergence across quantiles (<a href="#C418" class="links">Chart IV.18</a>). The widening of inflation divergence across CPI groups is on account of an uptick in inflation in food, pan and tobacco, and personal transport groups.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Out of 43 groups, 6 registered y-o-y inflation in excess of 6 per cent in August as compared to 3 groups in April 2026."><a href="#F23" class="links">23</a></sup></p>    <a id="C416"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C416.jpg" alt="Chart IV.16: Goods and Services Inflation" title="Chart IV.16: Goods and Services Inflation"></div><br>    <a id="C417"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C417.jpg" alt="Chart IV.17: Average CPI Inflation (y-o-y)" title="Chart IV.17: Average CPI Inflation (y-o-y)"></div>    <p>The recent uptick in inflation is also reflected in the CPI headline diffusion indices<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Methodology document and data is available at the following path: DBIE Home (data.rbi.org.in) > Statistics > Real Sector > Prices & Wages > Diffusion Indices based on CPI data."><a href="#F24" class="links">24</a></sup> in August 2026, the weighted distribution of y-o-y headline inflation components shows a shift towards higher inflation categories (<a href="#B4_1" class="links">Box IV.1</a>). </p>    <a id="C418"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C418.jpg" alt="Chart IV.18: CPI Sub-Group/Group Inflation Range" title="Chart IV.18: CPI Sub-Group/Group Inflation Range"></div><br>    <table width="95%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td><p class="head" align="center"><a id="B4_1"></a>Box IV.1: Measuring Inflation Generalisation through the Diffusion Index</p>          <p>When confronted with supply shocks, monetary policy responses are calibrated based on the assessment of second-round effects. If supply shocks are temporary, monetary policy often looks through them, whereas when there are signs of generalisation, policy acts to prevent inflation from becoming embedded in expectations. One critical challenge in this context is to gauge the extent to which inflation has become generalised. This can be assessed by examining whether the initial price pressures remain confined to the directly affected components or have spread to a wider set of items in the CPI basket.</p>          <p>A useful measure of this generalisation is the inflation diffusion index, which captures the weighted distribution of item-level price changes in the CPI basket (de Soyres et al., 2026). An increase in the diffusion index in the high inflation categories indicates that price pressures are becoming more widespread across the consumption basket. This can be used along with the headline or core inflation numbers to understand the nature of inflation process. An increase in headline inflation accompanied by a low diffusion index for the high inflation categories is indicative of inflation driven by idiosyncratic supply shocks. On the other hand, surge in diffusion index in the higher inflation category along with the headline inflation number, indicates that inflationary pressures have become more broad-based and generalised.</p>          <p>Many countries use diffusion indices alongside other measures of underlying inflation to understand the spread of inflationary pressures.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Examples include The Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco’s Personal Consumption Expenditure (PCE)-based inflation dispersion and diffusionmeasures, the Bank of Japan’s CPI diffusion index based on the share of items recording price increases relative to those recording price declines and theCentral Bank of Brazil’s diffusion index, which measures the proportion of components recording price increases in each month."><a href="#F25" class="links">25</a></sup> Over the years, the Reserve Bank of India has also examined the distribution of item-level inflation using diffusion indices, inflation distributions and kernel density plots (RBI, Monetary Policy Report, various issues; Nadhanael and Pattanaik, 2010; Hasan et al, 2026).</p>          <p>Against this backdrop, the box attempts to compile diffusion indices for inflation in India based on the revised CPI (2024=100) series. The diffusion indices are compiled for i) Headline CPI comprising all items; and ii) Core or CPI excluding food and fuel.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="‘Fuel’ here represents two categories, viz., ‘electricity, gas and other fuels’; and ‘Fuels and lubricants for personal transport equipment’ which includes‘petrol’, ‘diesel’, and ‘other natural gas (CNG)’. Same definition is applied elsewhere in this box."><a href="#F26" class="links">26</a></sup> The indices are constructed using weighted item-level year-on-year (y-o-y) inflation rates.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="A more nuanced measure of the distribution of item level price change distribution is to use the seasonally adjusted m-o-m annualised rates. However, the seasonal adjustment of the commodity level price indices in the current CPI series (with base year 2024) is challenging due to the non-availability of longer time series at the item-level."><a href="#F27" class="links">27</a></sup></p>          <p>Conceptually, diffusion indices represent the weighted share of items in the respective CPI basket, categorised into the following four buckets (intervals), aligning it with the inflation target and its tolerance band:</p>          <p>i. Below 2 per cent;</p>          <p>ii. Between 2 per cent and 4 per cent;</p>          <p>iii. Between 4 per cent and 6 per cent; and</p>          <p>iv. Above 6 per cent.</p>          <p>The item-level y-o-y inflation rates are calculated using the following formulae:</p>          <div><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_E41.jpg"></div><br>          <a id="B41"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_B41.jpg" alt="Chart IV.1.1: Diffusion Indices for y-o-y Inflation" title="Chart IV.1.1: Diffusion Indices for y-o-y Inflation"></div>          <p>The expenditure (CPI)-weighted diffusion index corresponding to inflation interval k is then calculated as:</p>          <div><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_E42.jpg"></div>          <p>where &omega;i is the weight of the item i in the CPI basket and N is the number of the items in CPI basket (number of items = 358 for headline and 198 for CPI excluding food and fuel).</p>          <p>The distribution of CPI inflation points to a progressive broadening of inflationary pressures. In August 2026, around 37 per cent of the CPI basket registered inflation above 4 per cent, as compared to 23 per cent in April 2026 (<a href="#B41" class="links">Chart IV.1.1a</a>). The weighted share of the items in the CPI basket experiencing inflation below 2 per cent declined substantially with a notable rise in the weighted share of items experiencing inflation above 6 per cent. Distribution of core, i.e., CPI excluding food and fuel inflation also indicates a similar shift in distribution as in the case of headline inflation, albeit of a lower magnitude (<a href="#B41" class="links">Chart IV.1.1b</a>).</p>          <p class="head">References:</p>          <p>de Soyres, F., Pradhan, A., and Saijid, Z. (2026). Is the Inflation Process in Advanced Economies Different After the Pandemic? FEDS Notes. Washington, DC: Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System. <a href="https://doi.org/10.17016/2380-7172.4024" target="_blank" class="links">https://doi.org/10.17016/2380-7172.4024</a></p>          <p>Hasan, S., Sarkar, S., Anjanakrishna K and John, J. (2026). Diffusion Indices for Inflation in India: A Measure of Inflation Generalisation. Mimeo.</p>          <p>Nadhanael, G. V., and Pattanaik, S. (2010). Measurement of Inflation in India: Issues and Associated Challenges for the Conduct of Monetary Policy. RBI Staff Studies. Reserve Bank of India.</p>          <p>Reserve Bank of India. (n.d.). CPI Diffusion Indices: Various issues. Monetary Policy Reports. <a href="https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/PublicationsView.aspx?id=23426" target="_blank" class="links">https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/PublicationsView.aspx?id=23426</a></p></td>      </tr>    </table>    <p>Statistical measures of underlying inflation such as the trimmed mean and the weighted median<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="While exclusion-based measures drop a fixed set of volatile items (for example, food and fuel) in each period, trimmed measures exclude items located in the tails of the inflation distribution - items displaying changes more than the specified threshold in prices each month are excluded, and the items dropped differ from month to month. The weighted median inflation rate is defined as the inflation rate corresponding to the item that lies at the 50th percentile in the distribution of price changes within the CPI basket, with weights based on the CPI basket."><a href="#F28" class="links">28</a></sup>, although increasing steadily during March to August 2026, remained lower than CPI excluding food and fuel inflation (<a href="#T42" class="links">Table IV.2</a>). In terms of trajectory, they remain largely aligned with the conventional core inflation.</p><table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">  <tr>    <td colspan="5" align="center"><span class="head"><a id="T42"></a>Table IV.2: Measures of Underlying Inflation: Trimmed Mean Measures and Weighted Median<br>      </span>(Y-o-y, per cent)</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td width="28%" align="center"><span class="head">Month</span></td>    <td width="18%" align="center"><span class="head">5% trimmed</span></td>    <td width="18%" align="center"><span class="head">10% trimmed</span></td>    <td width="18%" align="center"><span class="head">25% trimmed</span></td>    <td width="18%" align="center"><span class="head">Weighted Median</span></td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Jul-25</td>    <td align="right">2.7</td>    <td align="right">3.0</td>    <td align="right">3.1</td>    <td align="right">3.0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Aug-25</td>    <td align="right">2.9</td>    <td align="right">2.9</td>    <td align="right">2.9</td>    <td align="right">3.0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Sep-25</td>    <td align="right">2.5</td>    <td align="right">2.6</td>    <td align="right">2.7</td>    <td align="right">2.7</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Oct-25</td>    <td align="right">1.9</td>    <td align="right">2.2</td>    <td align="right">2.4</td>    <td align="right">2.4</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Nov-25</td>    <td align="right">2.0</td>    <td align="right">2.2</td>    <td align="right">2.4</td>    <td align="right">2.5</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Dec-25</td>    <td align="right">1.8</td>    <td align="right">1.9</td>    <td align="right">2.1</td>    <td align="right">2.1</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Jan-26</td>    <td align="right">2.3</td>    <td align="right">2.2</td>    <td align="right">2.0</td>    <td align="right">2.0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Feb-26</td>    <td align="right">2.4</td>    <td align="right">2.5</td>    <td align="right">2.1</td>    <td align="right">2.0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Mar-26</td>    <td align="right">2.7</td>    <td align="right">2.4</td>    <td align="right">2.2</td>    <td align="right">2.0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Apr-26</td>    <td align="right">2.7</td>    <td align="right">2.6</td>    <td align="right">2.3</td>    <td align="right">2.0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>May-26</td>    <td align="right">3.1</td>    <td align="right">3.0</td>    <td align="right">2.6</td>    <td align="right">2.3</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Jun-26</td>    <td align="right">3.7</td>    <td align="right">3.6</td>    <td align="right">3.3</td>    <td align="right">2.7</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Jul-26</td>    <td align="right">3.8</td>    <td align="right">3.5</td>    <td align="right">3.4</td>    <td align="right">3.1</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Aug-26</td>    <td align="right">4.2</td>    <td align="right">4.0</td>    <td align="right">3.7</td>    <td align="right">3.5</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="5" align="justify">Note: Inflation numbers till December 2025 are based on the old CPI (2012=100) series, while from January 2026 are from the CPI (2024=100) series.<br>      Sources: NSO; and RBI staff estimates.</td>  </tr></table><p>Regional Trends in Inflation</p>    <p>Turning to inflation trajectory across regions, both urban and rural areas have been experiencing an uptick in inflation since January 2026. Rural inflation consistently exceeded urban inflation during January-August 2026, which is largely attributable to the greater weight of &lsquo;food and beverages&rsquo; that has witnessed a consistently higher inflation vis-&agrave;-vis other components during the same period (<a href="#C419" class="links">Chart IV.19</a>).</p>    <p>There was a marked divergence in inflation across states during April-August 2026 as compared to the corresponding period of last year. During 2025-26, average headline inflation was concentrated between 0-4 per cent. However, while 21 states in the current year registered average inflation in the range of 2-4 per cent, 13 states registered inflation in the 4-6 per cent range (<a href="#T43" class="links">Table IV.3</a>). Only one state<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="In Telangana, ‘Gold/diamond/platinum jewellery’ and ‘Silver jewellery’ contributed, on average, about 67 per cent of Telangana's CPI Headline inflation during April-August 2026. This is primarily due to high weight and high inflation of these items in Telangana."><a href="#F29" class="links">29</a></sup> reported average inflation greater than 6 per cent during this period. </p>    <a id="C419"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C419.jpg" alt="Chart IV.19: CPI Headline Inflation: Urban and Rural" title="Chart IV.19: CPI Headline Inflation: Urban and Rural"></div><br>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head"><a id="T43"></a>Table IV.3: Distribution of Headline Inflation across States/UTs: Number of states<sup>1</sup></span></td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td width="50%" align="center"><span class="head">Headline Inflation Range<br>          (Per cent)</span></td>        <td width="25%" align="center"><span class="head">2025-26<br>          (Apr-Aug)<sup>2</sup></span></td>        <td width="25%" align="center"><span class="head">2026-27<br>          (Apr-Aug)<sup>3</sup></span></td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>In Deflation</td>        <td align="right">0</td>        <td align="right">0</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Between 0 and 2.0</td>        <td align="right">14</td>        <td align="right">1</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Between 2.0 and 4.0</td>        <td align="right">16</td>        <td align="right">21</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Between 4.0 and 6.0</td>        <td align="right">5</td>        <td align="right">13</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Greater than 6.0</td>        <td align="right">1</td>        <td align="right">1</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="3" align="justify">Notes: 1. Accounted for the unification of Daman and Diu with Dadra & Nagar Haveli and the formation of Ladakh as a Union Territory (UT) in the old CPI (2012=100) series.<br>          2. Average headline inflation figures for April-August 2025-26 are based on old CPI (2012=100) series.<br>          3. Average headline inflation figures for 2026-27 (April-August 2026-27) are from the CPI (2024=100) series.<br>          Sources: NSO; and RBI staff estimates.</td>      </tr>    </table><p class="head"><a id="C4_4"></a>IV.4 Drivers of Inflation Trajectory</p><p>This section analyses the evolution of inflation conditions based on the assessment of underlying factors.</p><p>Imported Inflation</p><p>The contribution of imported components to domestic inflation picked up in 2026 following the onset of the West Asia conflict. Imported inflation as per the Wholesale Price Index (WPI) (2022-23=100) registered a significant increase up to May 2026, with some pullback thereafter, driven by petroleum products (<a href="#C420" class="links">Chart IV.20</a>). CPI imported inflation also remained elevated during this period, mainly due to higher precious metals prices, given the larger relative weight of precious metals in CPI.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The passthrough-adjusted weights for petroleum products in the retail imported basket (based on CPI (2024=100)) increased vis-à-vis the retail imported basket (based on CPI (2012=100) whereas, it reduced for vegetable oils, clothing, and precious metals. Since the inflation of vegetable oils was high andthat of petroleum product was low during 2025, this resulted in the drop in retail imported inflation in January 2026."><a href="#F31" class="links">30</a></sup> The extent of sequential increase in imported inflation in CPI since the onset of the West Asia conflict was lower than that in WPI, partly on account of delayed and partial price increases in petroleum products at the retail level.</p>    <a id="C420"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C420.jpg" alt="Chart IV.20: Imported Inflation" title="Chart IV.20: Imported Inflation"></div>    <p>Cost Conditions</p>    <p>Input cost pressures, as measured by WPI industrial raw materials and farm inputs inflation, rose sharply during March-May and remained elevated thereafter. The acceleration in industrial input costs was led by petroleum products, particularly aviation turbine fuel (ATF), high-speed diesel (HSD), naphtha, bitumen, furnace oil, and petroleum coke. The increase in farm input cost inflation was driven mainly by HSD, fertilisers, and nitrogen compounds (<a href="#C421" class="links">Chart IV.21</a>).</p>    <p>In the organised corporate sector, staff cost growth (y-o-y) in manufacturing accelerated in Q1:2026-27, following a moderation in Q4:2025-26 driven by strong momentum. In the services sector, y-o-y staff cost growth moderated during both Q4:2025-26 and Q1:2026-27, despite a positive momentum, on account of unfavourable base effects (<a href="#C422" class="links">Chart IV.22</a>). </p>    <a id="C421"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C421.jpg" alt="Chart IV.21: Farm and Non-farm InputCost Inflation" title="Chart IV.21: Farm and Non-farm Input"></div><br>    <a id="C422"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C422.jpg" alt="Chart IV.22: Staff Cost in Manufacturing and Services" title="Chart IV.22: Staff Cost in Manufacturing and Services"></div>    <p>On the outlook for cost and price conditions, infrastructure and services firms expect both input cost and selling price pressures to abate during Q3:2026-27, as per the firms polled in the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s enterprise surveys. Manufacturing firms expect a moderation in input cost pressures while also expecting an increase in selling prices. Salary outgo pressures are expected to increase for manufacturing firms during Q3:2026-27. In contrast, it is expected to ease for services and infrastructure firms (<a href="#C423" class="links">Chart IV.23</a>).</p>    <p>The Purchasing Managers&rsquo; Index (PMI) surveys indicate some sequential moderation in manufacturing firms&rsquo; cost pressures after the sharp increase following the onset of the West Asia conflict. Input price pressures reported by manufacturing firms eased gradually up to August 2026 after peaking in April. Output price pressures followed a broadly similar trajectory. Both input and output prices inched up in September. The manufacturing PMI input-output price gap turned positive in March 2026, indicating weak pass-through of input costs to selling prices. Although the gap narrowed from its March peak, it remained positive in August, suggesting some pent-up pass-through (<a href="#C424" class="links">Chart IV.24a</a>).</p>    <a id="C423"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C423.jpg" alt="Chart IV.23: Expectations of Cost and Price Conditions" title="Chart IV.23: Expectations of Cost and Price Conditions"></div>    <p>In the services sector, operating cost pressures eased gradually up to July before a mild pick-up in August 2026. Selling price pressures also moderated up to June but increased in July-August. Consequently, the services PMI input-output price gap narrowed significantly in August (<a href="#C424" class="links">Chart IV.24b</a>).</p>    <p>Similar trends were observed for Output and Input Producer Price Index (PPI)<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="On June 15, 2026, the Office of Economic Adviser, Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade, Ministry of Commerce & Industry released the new series of Output Producer Price Index (OPPI) along with a Trial Input Producer Price Index (IPPI). Apart from this, a Service Producer Price Index (Service PPI) of seven services with the same base year was also released."><a href="#F31" class="links">31</a></sup> for manufactured products during April-August 2026. For most industry groups, pace of increase in input prices was faster than that of output prices, suggesting that the full extent of input price rise may not have been passed on to output prices (<a href="#C425" class="links">Chart IV.25</a>). At the aggregate level, however, output prices rose faster than input prices driven by coke and refined petroleum products.</p>    <p>Margins</p>    <p>Food price margins<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The absolute retail margin is defined as the difference between retail and wholesale prices. Item-level retail and wholesale prices are aggregated at respective class using item level CPI (2024=100) weights. Data for January-March 2021 have been excluded due to changes in price collection mechanism and item varieties by DCA."><a href="#F32" class="links">32</a></sup> have recorded a mixed trend in the recent period. Retail margins for cereals remained stable during April-September 2026. Margins for pulses remained stable until June before moderating during July-September. For TOP vegetables, margins picked up since April. In case of edible oils, margins peaked in May 2026 but remained range-bound thereafter (<a href="#C426" class="links">Chart IV.26</a>).</p>    <a id="C424"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C424.jpg" alt="Chart IV.24: PMI Input-Output Price Gap" title="Chart IV.24: PMI Input-Output Price Gap"></div><br>    <a id="C425"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C425.jpg" alt="Chart IV.25: Cumulative Change in Input and Output Prices" title="Chart IV.25: Cumulative Change in Input and Output Prices"></div><br>    <a id="C426"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C426.jpg" alt="Chart IV.26: Retail, Wholesale Prices and Margin" title="Chart IV.26: Retail, Wholesale Prices and Margin"></div>    <p>In terms of the relative contribution of supply and demand factors in conditioning inflation dynamics, the evidence points towards the dissipation of both favourable supply and benign demand conditions, which led to increase in inflation in 2026-27 so far (<a href="#B4_2" class="links">Box IV.2</a>).</p>    <table width="95%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td><p class="head" align="center"><a id="B4_2"></a>Box IV.2: What Drives Inflation in India? Disentangling Demand and Supply Factors</p>          <p>Disentangling the relative roles of demand and supply factors in driving inflation is critical for an inflation-targeting central bank to calibrate appropriate policy response. Demand-driven inflation typically calls for monetary tightening, while supply-driven inflation creates difficult policy trade-offs as it is often accompanied by a slowdown in growth. In case of India, the recent experience of inflation significantly undershooting the target before a sharp reversal merits a thorough examination of the role of demand and supply factors in shaping the inflation dynamics.</p>          <p>Empirical literature has adopted different approaches to disentangle the contributions of aggregate demand and supply conditions such as identifying the cyclical component of inflation by examining its relationship with economic activity as proposed by Stock and Watson (2020). Another approach is to explicitly decompose inflation into demand and supply-driven components using price and quantity information at the commodity or consumption-category level (Shapiro, 2024<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="This framework estimates separate price and quantity regressions for individual consumption categories and classifies inflation movements based on the direction of unexpected price and quantity changes. Under this approach, simultaneous increases or decreases in prices and quantities are associated with demand-driven movements, while movements in opposite directions are associated with supply-driven changes."><a href="#F33" class="links">33</a></sup> and Shekhar, Kishore and Bhoi, 2023).</p>          <p>More recently, Eickmeier and Hofmann (2025) have developed a structural factor model to identify aggregate demand and supply conditions using a broad set of inflation and real activity indicators. Their methodology exploits the common variation across a large number of macroeconomic series and identifies demand and supply factors through theoretically motivated sign restrictions on factor loadings.</p>          <p>The demand factor is characterised by positive co-movement between inflation and economic activity, whereas the supply factor is associated with opposite movements in inflation and economic activity. This framework enables the construction of aggregate indicators of demand and supply conditions and facilitates an assessment of their contribution to inflation dynamics over time.</p>          <p>This factor-based approach is applied to the Indian economy to identify indicators of aggregate demand and supply conditions and examine their role in the evolution of inflation in India. Compared with approaches that focus primarily on cyclical inflation or commodity-level price and quantity responses, the proposed framework incorporates information from a broader set of macroeconomic variables.</p>          <p class="head">Data</p>          <p>The dataset includes a broad set of inflation measures including headline CPI, house price index, and other group/sub-group-wise inflation. The real-activity block includes GDP and expenditure components, gross and sectoral GVA indices, and other demand proxies such as diesel consumption and capacity utilisation. Other global price indicators like gold, copper, Brent crude oil and fertiliser prices are also included.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="List of variables includes Real GDP, Real Agriculture GVA, Real Industry GVA, Real Services GVA, Real Private Final Consumption Expenditure, Real Government Final Consumption Expenditure, Real Gross Capital Formation, Real Gross Fixed Capital Formation, Real Exports, Real Imports, Headline CPI, Urban CPI, Rural CPI, CPI Food and Beverages, CPI Urban Food and Beverages, CPI Rural Food and Beverages, CPI Pan and Tobacco, CPI Clothing and Footwear, CPI Housing, CPI Fuel and Light, CPI Miscellaneous, Housing Price Index, Capacity Utilisation, Brent Crude Prices, Gold Prices, DAP Fertiliser Prices, Urea Prices, and Copper Prices."><a href="#F34" class="links">34</a></sup></p>          <p class="head">Methodology</p>          <p>The empirical strategy uses a factor model estimated by employing principal component analysis on quarterly macroeconomic time series data for India. Factor identification follows the standard supply-demand analysis based on randomly generated orthogonal rotations. The demand factor is identified as one that loads positively on inflation measures and real-activity measures. The supply factor is identified as one that loads negatively on inflation measures and positively on real-activity measures. Sign restrictions are imposed across key variables while allowing some variables to remain unrestricted.</p>          <p>Once the demand and supply factors are identified<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="A positive demand factor contributes to an increase in inflation whereas a positive supply factor contributes to a decline."><a href="#F35" class="links">35</a></sup>, Fry-Pagan median target approach is used to select accepted rotation closest to the median target.</p>          <p class="head">Results</p>          <p>The findings indicate that the recent episode of inflation declining from H2:2024-25 to H2:2025-26 was driven by a weakening of demand as well as easy supply conditions (<a href="#BC421" class="links">Chart IV.2.1</a> and <a href="#BC422" class="links">Chart IV.2.2</a>).</p>          <p>Latest data for Q1:2026-27 show closing of negative demand conditions as well as waning of easy supply conditions. Demand factor converged to almost zero levels during Q1 as economic activity strengthened despite global shocks and emerging supply side pressures. As growth has surprised on the upside and inflation is showing early signs of generalisation, this indicates waning of favourable factors that kept inflation benign during 2025-26 and potential pressures going forward.</p>          <p class="head">References</p>          <p>Eickmeier, S., and Hofmann, B. (2025). What Drives Inflation? Disentangling Demand and Supply Factors, International Journal of Central Banking, 21(3).</p>          <a id="BC421"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_B421.jpg" alt="Chart IV.2.1: Median Demand Factor" title="Chart IV.2.1: Median Demand Factor"></div><br>          <a id="BC422"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_B422.jpg" alt="Chart IV.2.2: Median Supply Factor" title="Chart IV.2.2: Median Supply Factor"></div>          <p>Shapiro (2004), A. (2024). Decomposing Supply and Demand Driven Inflation, Working Paper Series, Federal Bank of San Francisco.</p>          <p>Shekhar, H., Kishore, B. and Bhoi B. B. (2023). Recent Inflation Dynamics in India: Role of Supply vis-&agrave;-vis Demand, RBI Bulletin, December.</p>          <p>Stock, J. H., and Watson, M. W. (2020). Slack and Cyclically Sensitive Inflation. Journal of Money, Credit and Banking, 52(S2), 393-428.</p></td>      </tr>    </table><p>The historical decomposition of inflation using a vector autoregression (VAR)<sup><a href="#F36" class="links">36</a></sup> model indicates that inflation firmed up since Q4:2025-26 as the impact of favourable supply shocks progressively waned while the contribution of fuel price shocks and exchange rate shocks increased (<a href="#C427" class="links">Chart IV.27</a>). </p><a id="C427"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C427.jpg" alt="Chart IV.27: Decomposition of CPI Inflation" title="Chart IV.27: Decomposition of CPI Inflation"></div><p class="head"><a id="C4_5"></a>IV.5 Conclusion</p><p>Headline inflation has increased steadily throughout 2026-27 (up to August), initially driven by higher food and fuel inflation but showing signs of broad-based price pressures in recent months. The impact of El Ni&ntilde;o conditions and deficient southwest monsoon is increasingly reflected in food prices. Core inflation recorded a pick-up along with incipient signs of generalisation. Going forward, there is a material risk that supply-side pressures in food may remain unabated as weather uncertainties persist and global oil prices continue to remain volatile. In this context, generalisation of inflation through second-round effects remains a major risk from the firming up of demand conditions, given strong growth impulses. This is particularly pertinent as growth in credit and monetary aggregates remains robust (see Chapter II); therefore, stricter vigilance is required on the inflation front.</p><p>_________________________________________________________________________________</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F1"></a><sup>1</sup> Headline inflation is measured by year-on-year (y-o-y) changes in the all-India consumer price index (CPI) published by the National Statistics Office (NSO), Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI), Government of India.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F2"></a><sup>2</sup> A change in CPI year-on-year (y-o-y) inflation between any two months is approximately the difference between the current month-on-month (m-o-m) change in the price index (momentum) and the m-o-m change in the price index 12 months earlier (base effect). For more details, see Box I.1 of the MPR of September 2014.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F3"></a><sup>3</sup> The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) Act, 1934 (amended in 2016) enjoins the RBI to set out deviations of actual inflation outcomes from projections, if any, and explain the underlying reasons thereof.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F4"></a><sup>4</sup> Animal protein in the CPI basket includes meat, fish, milk and dairy products and eggs. Together, they have a weight of 10.8 per cent in the CPI (2024=100) and 29.4 per cent in the &lsquo;Food and beverages&rsquo; division.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F5"></a><sup>5</sup> &lsquo;Spices, culinary herbs and seeds&rsquo; subclass has weight of 2.3 per cent in CPI (2024=100) and 6.2 per cent weight in the &lsquo;Food and beverages&rsquo; division.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F6"></a><sup>6</sup> Spices category recorded deflation for eighteen months till December 2025.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F7"></a><sup>7</sup> As on September 16, 2026, the rice and wheat stocks stood at 584.1 lakh tonnes (4.3 times the buffer norm) and 474.5 lakh tonnes (1.7 times the buffer norm), respectively.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F8"></a><sup>8</sup>