he proportion of components recording price increases in each month.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F26"></a><sup>26</sup> &lsquo;Fuel&rsquo; here represents two categories, viz., &lsquo;electricity, gas and other fuels&rsquo;; and &lsquo;Fuels and lubricants for personal transport equipment&rsquo; which includes &lsquo;petrol&rsquo;, &lsquo;diesel&rsquo;, and &lsquo;other natural gas (CNG)&rsquo;. Same definition is applied elsewhere in this box.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F27"></a><sup>27</sup> A more nuanced measure of the distribution of item level price change distribution is to use the seasonally adjusted m-o-m annualised rates. However, the seasonal adjustment of the commodity level price indices in the current CPI series (with base year 2024) is challenging due to the non-availability of longer time series at the item-level.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F28"></a><sup>28</sup> While exclusion-based measures drop a fixed set of volatile items (for example, food and fuel) in each period, trimmed measures exclude items located in the tails of the inflation distribution - items displaying changes more than the specified threshold in prices each month are excluded, and the items dropped differ from month to month. The weighted median inflation rate is defined as the inflation rate corresponding to the item that lies at the 50th percentile in the distribution of price changes within the CPI basket, with weights based on the CPI basket.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F29"></a><sup>29</sup> In Telangana, &lsquo;Gold/diamond/platinum jewellery&rsquo; and &lsquo;Silver jewellery&rsquo; contributed, on average, about 67 per cent of Telangana's CPI Headline inflation during April-August 2026. This is primarily due to high weight and high inflation of these items in Telangana.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F30"></a><sup>30</sup> The passthrough-adjusted weights for petroleum products in the retail imported basket (based on CPI (2024=100)) increased vis-&agrave;-vis the retail imported basket (based on CPI (2012=100) whereas, it reduced for vegetable oils, clothing, and precious metals. Since the inflation of vegetable oils was high and that of petroleum product was low during 2025, this resulted in the drop in retail imported inflation in January 2026.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F31"></a><sup>31</sup> On June 15, 2026, the Office of Economic Adviser, Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade, Ministry of Commerce & Industry released the new series of Output Producer Price Index (OPPI) along with a Trial Input Producer Price Index (IPPI). Apart from this, a Service Producer Price Index (Service PPI) of seven services with the same base year was also released.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F32"></a><sup>32</sup> The absolute retail margin is defined as the difference between retail and wholesale prices. Item-level retail and wholesale prices are aggregated at respective class using item level CPI (2024=100) weights. Data for January-March 2021 have been excluded due to changes in price collection mechanism and item varieties by DCA.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F33"></a><sup>33</sup> This framework estimates separate price and quantity regressions for individual consumption categories and classifies inflation movements based on the direction of unexpected price and quantity changes. Under this approach, simultaneous increases or decreases in prices and quantities are associated with demand-driven movements, while movements in opposite directions are associated with supply-driven changes.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F34"></a><sup>34</sup> List of variables includes Real GDP, Real Agriculture GVA, Real Industry GVA, Real Services GVA, Real Private Final Consumption Expenditure, Real Government Final Consumption Expenditure, Real Gross Capital Formation, Real Gross Fixed Capital Formation, Real Exports, Real Imports, Headline CPI, Urban CPI, Rural CPI, CPI Food and Beverages, CPI Urban Food and Beverages, CPI Rural Food and Beverages, CPI Pan and Tobacco, CPI Clothing and Footwear, CPI Housing, CPI Fuel and Light, CPI Miscellaneous, Housing Price Index, Capacity Utilisation, Brent Crude Prices, Gold Prices, DAP Fertiliser Prices, Urea Prices, and Copper Prices.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F35"></a><sup>35</sup> A positive demand factor contributes to an increase in inflation whereas a positive supply factor contributes to a decline.</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F36"></a><sup>36</sup> Historical decomposition estimates the contribution of each shock to the movements in inflation over the sample period (Q4:2010-11 to Q2:2026-27) based on a VAR with the following variables (represented as the vector Y<sub>t</sub> ) &ndash; crude oil prices (US$ per barrel); exchange rate (INR per US$), asset price (BSE Sensex), CPI; the output gap; rural wages; the policy repo rate; and money supply (M3). All variables other than policy repo rate are y-o-y growth rates. The VAR can be written in reduced form as: Y<sub>t</sub> = c + A Y<sub>t&ndash;1</sub> + e<sub>t</sub>; where e<sub>t</sub> represents a vector of shocks. Using Wold decomposition, Y<sub>t</sub> can be represented as a function of its deterministic trend and sum of all the shocks et.</p><hr><p class="head" align="center"><a id="C5"></a>V. Macroeconomic Outlook</p><p><em>Amidst persistent geopolitical tensions, volatility in global commodity and financial markets and persisting trade uncertainty, India&rsquo;s economic activity remains robust, supported by resilient domestic demand, strengthening investment and sound macroeconomic fundamentals. Headline inflation has firmed up in recent months, largely reflecting food and fuel price pressures, but with increasing evidence of generalisation. The outlook for growth and inflation is subject to uncertainties arising from external economic environment, commodity prices, weather events and global financial conditions. Against this backdrop, monetary policy remains focussed on maintaining price stability while supporting sustained economic growth.</em></p><p class="head"><a id="C5_1"></a>V.1 Key Developments since the April 2026 MPR</p><p>Since the release of the Monetary Policy Report (MPR) in April 2026, the global economy has continued to navigate an uncertain environment precipitated by geopolitical tensions, evolving trade and tariff stipulations, volatile financial conditions, and rapid technological change. Despite these headwinds, global economic activity has remained resilient, with the International Monetary Fund (IMF) projecting global growth at 3.0 per cent in 2026 and 3.4 per cent in 2027.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="World Economic Outlook (WEO) Update, International Monetary Fund (IMF), July 2026."><a href="#F51" class="links">1</a></sup> The inflation outlook, however, is of material concern with global headline inflation projected to rise from 4.1 per cent in 2025 to 4.7 per cent in 2026 by the IMF, suggesting that the disinflation process that was underway since early 2024 has reversed its course. Macroeconomic dynamics have been multi-paced across countries, reflecting the varying level of exposure to geopolitical developments, state of domestic demand conditions and extent of involvement in the ongoing technology-related investment cycle.</p><p>Against this backdrop, monetary policy has been cautious in major economies with central banks balancing the risk of renewed inflationary pressures against concerns on slowing economic activity.</p><p>Financial markets, meanwhile, have remained resilient, albeit with intermittent bouts of volatility in the wake of the prevailing uncertainty. Global equity markets have generally been supported by strong corporate earnings and optimism around technology-related investment, even as geopolitical uncertainties and macroeconomic data releases led to periodic corrections. Sovereign bond yields, particularly at longer maturities, have hardened across major economies amidst concerns over inflation, elevated public debt and large financing requirements.</p><p>Global commodity prices have been volatile since April 2026, with the West Asia conflict induced initial surge moderating across some segments before renewed supply concerns triggered price pressures again since August. Energy markets were particularly sensitive to supply disruptions. Similarly, metal prices remained elevated amidst supply disruptions, supported by strong demand from infrastructure and technology-related investment, before moderating subsequently and rising again since August. The emergence of El Ni&ntilde;o conditions has also added uncertainty to the outlook for agricultural production and food prices. Agricultural commodity prices, which were relatively stable after April, faced upward pressures from higher energy and fertiliser costs, adverse weather conditions and disruptions to trade and supplies. These developments were also reflected in currency markets with varying level of exchange rate adjustments across major economies. Fluctuations in the US dollar and evolving monetary policy expectations contributed to changing currency dynamics, while higher energy prices continued to exert pressure on some energy-importing economies. Amidst this volatile backdrop, the global economy has so far demonstrated resilience, although the interplay of geopolitical developments, inflationary pressures and financial market conditions will remain the key in shaping the outlook ahead.</p><p>On the domestic front, economic activity remained robust in Q1:2026&ndash;27, with real GDP expanding by 7.8 per cent and real GVA by 8.2 per cent. Growth remains broad-based, with strong momentum in industry and services, while agriculture and allied activities also contributed positively. On the expenditure side, private consumption remained resilient and investment activity strengthened, with gross fixed capital formation registering a notable expansion.</p><p>Headline CPI inflation increased from 3.5 per cent in April 2026 to 4.8 per cent in August, driven by sustained positive price momentum. Food inflation firmed up during this period and contributed significantly to the increase in headline inflation. Fuel inflation, which had remained subdued earlier, started increasing from May due to elevated international energy prices and their pass-through to domestic prices, albeit partially. Core inflation recorded only a modest increase till July before accelerating in August amidst signs of generalisation.</p><p>Against this growth-inflation dynamics, the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) considered it prudent to wait for the implications of prevailing uncertainties on growth and greater clarity on the inflation outlook to emerge before undertaking any policy action. Therefore, following the cumulative easing of 125 basis points during February to December 2025, the MPC left the policy repo rate unchanged at 5.25 per cent in its April, June and August 2026 meetings while retaining the neutral stance.</p><p>Monetary Policy Committee Meetings: April 2026-September 2026</p><p>The MPC met for its first policy of the financial year in April 2026, amidst heightened geopolitical tensions arising from the West Asia conflict, which severely disrupted global supply chains, posing headwinds for India. The Indian economy, however, remained resilient, though elevated energy and commodity prices and disruptions in the Strait of Hormuz were expected to weigh adversely on domestic economic activity and inflation in 2026-27. Considering all these factors, real GDP growth for 2026-27 was projected at 6.9 per cent with downside risks while CPI headline inflation was projected at 4.6 per cent with upside risks. Core inflation was projected at 4.4 per cent for 2026-27. Recognising the supply-side nature of the shock and strong fundamentals of the domestic economy, the MPC considered it prudent to wait and unanimously decided to keep the repo rate unchanged at 5.25 per cent while retaining the neutral stance.</p><p>At the June 2026 meeting, the continuing West Asia conflict posed risks to inflation and growth; nonetheless, domestic economic activity remained broadly resilient. Elevated energy and commodity prices and persistent supply disruptions were expected to weigh on growth. Consequently, real GDP growth projection for 2026-27 was revised downwards by 30 basis points (bps) to 6.6 per cent. Meanwhile, marginal pick up in headline CPI inflation in March and April, primarily due to higher food inflation and subsequent increases in retail fuel prices, raised inflationary pressures, leading to an upward revision of the CPI inflation projection for 2026-27 to 5.1 per cent. Core inflation was projected at 4.7 per cent for 2026-27. The MPC noted that while the risks to inflation have increased, it would be prudent to await for greater clarity on the evolving outlook. Accordingly, the MPC unanimously decided to retain the policy repo rate at 5.25 per cent along with the neutral stance.</p><p>In the run up to the August 2026 meeting, the global economic outlook remained volatile as the relief from temporary ceasefire dissipated quickly. Domestically, risks to agriculture emanated from an erratic monsoon and El Ni&ntilde;o conditions and supply-side measures were undertaken to mitigate the impact. Overall, the Indian economy demonstrated resilience, driven by better-than-expected consumption and investment demand, despite persistent global headwinds. Accordingly, real GDP growth projection for 2026-27 was revised slightly upwards to 6.7 per cent. On the inflation front, CPI inflation rose to 4.4 per cent in June 2026 after remaining below the target of 4.0 per cent for 16 consecutive months, but was 30 bps lower than projections, prompting a downward revision of the 2026-27 inflation projection to 5.0 per cent. The core inflation was also projected lower at 4.3 per cent for 2026-27. The MPC observed that the rise in headline inflation was mainly driven by supply-side pressures and considered it appropriate for greater clarity to emerge on the inflation outlook before taking policy action. Accordingly, the MPC unanimously voted to keep the policy repo rate unchanged at 5.25 per cent and retained the neutral stance. Nevertheless, the MPC pointed out that any policy action would also consider the recalibration of policy rates in line with the evolving growth-inflation dynamics, especially normalisation of the underlying inflation from its earlier benign levels.</p><p>The Indian MPC&rsquo;s unanimous voting pattern stands in contrast to the diversity in voting witnessed in many other central banks (<a href="#T51" class="links">Table V.1</a>). Monetary policy decisions remained divergent across economies based on domestic conditions, although a tilt towards hawkish pivot is visible. Among the Advanced Economies (AEs), while policy rate remained broadly unchanged, dissent among the members was more pronounced. Japan and the UK witnessed voting disagreements in all their meetings, while the US saw dissent in two out of four meetings with a rate hike in its latest meeting. Among the Emerging Market Economies (EMEs), pivot towards hiking is visible for some although Brazil and Hungary have reduced their policy rates by 100 bps and 75 bps, respectively, in view of high real policy rates.</p><table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">  <tr>    <td colspan="5" align="center"><span class="head"><a id="T51"></a>Table V.1: Monetary Policy Committees and Policy Rate Voting Patterns</span></td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td width="28%" rowspan="2" align="center"><span class="head">Country</span></td>    <td colspan="4" align="center"><span class="head">Policy Meetings: April 2026 - September 2026</span></td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td width="18%" align="center"><span class="head">Total meetings</span></td>    <td width="18%" align="center"><span class="head">Meetings with full consensus</span></td>    <td width="18%" align="center"><span class="head">Meetings without full consensus</span></td>    <td width="18%" align="center"><span class="head">Variation in policy rate (basis points)</span></td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Brazil</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">0</td>    <td align="right">-100</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Chile</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">0</td>    <td align="right">0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Colombia</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">1</td>    <td align="right">3</td>    <td align="right">100</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Czech Republic</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">3</td>    <td align="right">1</td>    <td align="right">25</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Hungary*</td>    <td align="right">6</td>    <td align="right">3</td>    <td align="right">2</td>    <td align="right">-75</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>India</td>    <td align="right">3</td>    <td align="right">3</td>    <td align="right">0</td>    <td align="right">0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Japan</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">0</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">50</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>South Africa</td>    <td align="right">3</td>    <td align="right">1</td>    <td align="right">2</td>    <td align="right">50</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Sweden</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">0</td>    <td align="right">0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>Thailand</td>    <td align="right">3</td>    <td align="right">3</td>    <td align="right">0</td>    <td align="right">0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>UK</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">0</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td>US</td>    <td align="right">4</td>    <td align="right">2</td>    <td align="right">2</td>    <td align="right">25</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="5">Note: 1. Minus sign indicates a reduction in policy rate.<br>      2. *: Total number of meetings happened is six. However, the minutes of last meeting (September 22, 2026) are not published to date.<br>      Sources: Central bank websites.</td>  </tr></table><p>Factors conditioning the Macroeconomic Outlook</p><p>Macroeconomic developments pertaining to economic activity and inflation during H1:2026-27 are analysed in chapters III and IV. Going forward, the outlook is based on a set of baseline assumptions. First, assumption on crude oil price has been revised up to US$95 per barrel for the second half of 2026-27 from US$85 per barrel for 2026-27 in the last MPR (<a href="#T52" class="links">Table V.2</a>). This makes the alternate scenario presented in MPR April 2026 as our baseline with a $10 per barrel rise in oil price assumption over 2026- 27 and 2027-28. International crude oil prices peaked in April 2026 primarily because of severe supply shock caused by the West Asia conflict. Following the signing of Memorandum of Understanding between the US and Iran in June, oil prices eased and reached around US$83 per barrel in July 2026. After the ceasefire violations and the consequent collapse of the US-Iran agreement, prices began to firm up in August and further escalated in September. The Brent futures curve, however, remained in backwardation, with expectations of prices easing in future (<a href="#C51" class="links">Chart V.1a</a>). The upward pressure on prices was mitigated to some extent due to destocking of crude oil inventory by several countries, which has witnessed sharp depletion since Q4:2025-26, with minimal support to prices expected in the months ahead. With steady increase in production, crude oil inventory is expected to be replenished in the second half of 2026-27 that might keep demand and price pressures high (<a href="#C51" class="links">Chart V.1b</a>).</p><table width="95%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">  <tr>    <td colspan="3" align="center"><span class="head"><a id="T52"></a>Table V.2: Baseline Assumptions for Projections</span></td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td width="34%" align="center"><span class="head">Indicator</span></td>    <td width="33%" align="center"><span class="head">MPR April 2026</span></td>    <td width="33%" align="center"><span class="head">MPR October 2026</span></td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td valign="top">Crude Oil</td>    <td valign="top">US$85 per barrel during 2026-27 and US$75 per barrel during 2027-28</td>    <td valign="top">US$95 per barrel during H2: 2026-27 and $85 per barrel during 2027-28</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td valign="top">Exchange rate</td>    <td valign="top">&#8377; 94/US$ during 2026-27</td>    <td valign="top">&#8377; 95/US$ during 2026-27</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td valign="top">Monsoon</td>    <td valign="top">Normal for 2026-27 and 2027-28</td>    <td valign="top">Below normal for 2026-27 and normal for 2027-28</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td valign="top">Global growth</td>    <td valign="top">2.9 per cent in 2026<br>      3.0 per cent in 2027</td>    <td valign="top">3.0 per cent in 2026<br>      3.4 per cent in 2027</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td valign="top">Fiscal deficit<br>      (per cent of GDP)</td>    <td valign="top">To remain within BE 2026-27<br>      Centre: 4.3<br>      Combined:6.8</td>    <td valign="top">To remain within BE 2026-27<br>      Centre: 4.3<br>      Combined: 7.0</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td valign="top">Domestic macroeconomic/ structural policies during the forecast period</td>    <td valign="top">No major change</td>    <td valign="top">No major change</td>  </tr>  <tr>    <td colspan="3">Notes: 1. The assumption on oil is based on Brent Crude.<br>      2. The exchange rate path assumed here is for the purpose of generating the baseline projections and does not indicate any &lsquo;view&rsquo; on the level of the exchange rate. The Reserve Bank is guided by the objective of containing excess volatility in the foreign exchange market and not by any specific level of and/or band around the exchange rate.<br>      3. BE: Budget Estimates.<br>      4. Combined fiscal deficit refers to that of the Centre and States taken together.<br>      Sources: RBI estimates; Budget documents; and International Monetary Fund (IMF).</td>  </tr></table><p>Second, the nominal exchange rate of the Indian rupee (&#8377;) remained in the range of &#8377;92&ndash;97 per US dollar during April&ndash;September 2026 (<a href="#C51" class="links">Chart V.1c</a>). The rupee depreciated towards the end of April driven by portfolio outflows that reflected a risk off sentiment towards EMEs under heightened uncertainty. Subsequently, the rupee reversed its trend, supported by the easing of geopolitical tensions and the announcement of measures by the Reserve Bank of India to attract foreign capital flows including USD-INR swap window for FCNR(B) deposits and foreign investment in government securities. The Indian rupee subsequently weakened in early July in tandem with rising crude oil prices, but has since remained range-bound. Considering the volatility of the US dollar and fluctuations in global capital flows, the baseline assumption for the exchange rate has been revised to &#8377;95 per US dollar as against &#8377;94 in the April 2026 MPR.</p><a id="C51"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C51.jpg" alt="Chart V.1: Crude Oil Prices and Exchange Rate Movements" title="Chart V.1: Crude Oil Prices and Exchange Rate Movements"></div>    <p>Third, according to the IMF, global economic growth is projected at 3.0 per cent in 2026 and 3.4 per cent in 2027, lower than 3.5 per cent registered during 2025 (<a href="#C52" class="links">Chart V.2</a>). The prolonged conflict in West Asia continues to pose significant downside risk, with the potential to exacerbate supply-chain disruptions, and tighten financial conditions. AEs are projected to grow at 1.7 per cent in 2026, marginally lower than 1.9 per cent in 2025, while growth in EMEs is expected to decline to 3.8 per cent in 2026 from 4.5 per cent in 2025. In its Economic Outlook (September 2026), the Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) projected global GDP at 2.9 per cent in 2026 and 3.0 per cent in 2027. According to the IMF, world trade volume is expected to moderate sharply from 5.0 per cent in 2025 to 3.5 per cent in 2026 before recovering to 4.3 per cent in 2027. This moderation reflects the easing of earlier front-loading, tariff-related headwinds and the gradual reconfiguration of trade and production networks, partly offset by robust growth in technology-related trade flows. Global inflation is projected to reverse its declining trajectory temporarily, rising from 4.1 per cent in 2025 to 4.7 per cent in 2026 before easing to 3.9 per cent in 2027, primarily reflecting higher energy and food prices in 2026.</p>    <a id="C52"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C52.jpg" alt="Chart V.2: IMF and OECD projections forGrowth and Inflation" title="Chart V.2: IMF and OECD projections for"></div>    <p class="head"><a id="C5_2"></a>V.2 Inflation Outlook</p>    <p>Domestic headline inflation edged up to 4.8 per cent in August, driven primarily by rising food and fuel prices but with increasing evidence of broad-basing of price pressures as evident from diffusion indices (as discussed in Chapter IV). Inflation in food and beverages rose to 5.7 per cent, while core inflation inched up to 4.2 per cent. Going forward, inflation trajectory remains subject to considerable upside risks from overlapping climate and geopolitical shocks. Domestically, outlook is clouded by recent projections from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA), USA, warning that the prevailing El Ni&ntilde;o phenomenon is set to intensify till the end of 2026 and remain strong in the first half of 2027, posing downside risks to agriculture output, and reservoir replenishment. On the external front, developments in West Asia pose key upside risks, particularly through their impact on global energy prices, commodity markets and supply chains. Renewed geopolitical tensions have kept energy markets volatile and could raise freight, insurance and other transportation costs. Any sustained increase in these costs could feed into domestic retail prices and raise input costs across a range of products, triggering the risk of second-round and broader cost-push pressures.</p>    <p>Urban households&rsquo; inflation expectations for the next three months as well as one year ahead rose by 70 bps and 60 bps to 9.9 per cent and 10.0 per cent, respectively, in the latest survey round (September 2026) of the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s bi-monthly households survey.<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The Reserve Bank’s inflation expectations survey of households is being conducted in 19 cities since March 2021 (18 cities in the previous rounds) and the results of the September 2026 round are based on responses from 5,987 households."><a href="#F52" class="links">2</a></sup> The proportion of respondents anticipating a rise in general inflation has inched up for both the time horizons (<a href="#C53" class="links">Chart V.3a</a>). Additionally, according to the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s recent bi-monthly Rural Consumer Confidence Survey (RCCS)<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The Reserve Bank’s rural consumer confidence survey is being conducted across all Indian states and three major UTs since September 2023 and the results of the September 2026 round are based on responses from 8,799 respondents."><a href="#F53" class="links">3</a></sup>, the current perception (vis-&agrave;-vis a year ago) of inflation in rural and semi-urban households increased by 70 bps to 6.8 per cent in September 2026 as compared with the previous round. Moreover, their one-year ahead expectations also rose by 50 bps to 7.9 per cent. A sequential rise in the inflation expectations has been observed since January 2026 survey round as against the declining trend during the calendar year 2025 (Chart V.3b).</p>    <a id="C53"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C53.jpg" alt="Chart V.3: Inflation Expectations of Households" title="Chart V.3: Inflation Expectations of Households"></div>    <p>Manufacturing firms surveyed in the July-September 2026 round of the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s quarterly Industrial Outlook Survey expect pressures from cost of raw materials to persist in Q3:2026-27 with increased pass-through to selling prices (<a href="#C54" class="links">Chart V.4a</a>).<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="The results of the July-September 2026 round of the industrial outlook survey are based on responses from 1,224 companies."><a href="#F54" class="links">4</a></sup> Services and infrastructure sectors expect some easing of input cost pressures in Q3:2026-27, resulting in moderation in the pace of increase of selling prices (<a href="#C54" class="links">Chart V.4b</a> and <a href="#C54" class="links">V.4c</a>).<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Based on 666 services companies and 84 infrastructure firms polled in the July-September 2026 round of the services and infrastructure outlook survey."><a href="#F55" class="links">5</a></sup></p>    <p>Professional forecasters surveyed by the Reserve Bank in September 2026 projected CPI inflation to increase from 3.9 per cent in Q1:2026-27 to 4.9 per cent in Q2. It is expected to increase further to 5.9 per cent in Q3:2026-27, then moderate to 5.5 per cent in Q4 and further to 4.7 per cent in Q2:2027-28 (<a href="#C55" class="links">Chart V.5a</a> and <a href="#T53" class="links">Table V.3</a>).<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Forty-seven panellists participated in the September 2026 round of the Reserve Bank’s Survey of Professional Forecasters."><a href="#F56" class="links">6</a></sup> CPI inflation, excluding food and fuel<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="‘Fuel’ represents two categories, viz., ‘electricity, gas and other fuels’; and ‘Fuels and lubricants for personal transport equipment’ which includes‘petrol’, ‘diesel’, and ‘other natural gas (CNG)’."><a href="#F57" class="links">7</a></sup>, is expected at 4.1 per cent during Q2:2026-27, and thereafter remain between 4.3 to 4.7 per cent till Q2:2027-28.</p>    <p>Long-run inflation expectations of professional forecasters &ndash; measured by their five- and ten-year horizons &ndash; is at 4.5 per cent and 4.2 per cent, respectively, in the current round (<a href="#C55" class="links">Chart V.5 b</a>).</p>    <p>Considering the initial conditions, signals from forward-looking surveys and estimates from time-series and structural models<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Joice John, Deepak Kumar, Asish Thomas George, Pratik Mitra, Muneesh Kapur and Michael Debabrata Patra (2023), “A Recalibrated Quarterly Projection Model (QPM 2.0) for India”, Reserve Bank of India Bulletin, February, Volume LXXVII (2), pp.59-77."><a href="#F58" class="links">8</a></sup>, CPI inflation is projected to average 5.2 per cent in 2026-27 with Q2 at 4.9 per cent; Q3 at 6.0 per cent; and Q4 at 5.7 per cent with risks evenly balanced (<a href="#C56" class="links">Chart V.6</a> and <a href="#T53" class="links">Table V.3</a>). Assuming a normal monsoon, and no further exogenous or policy shocks, structural model estimates indicate that inflation will average 5.0 per cent in 2027-28. The 50 per cent and the 70 per cent confidence intervals for headline inflation in Q4:2027-28 are 3.1-6.1 per cent and 2.3-6.9 per cent, respectively.</p>    <a id="C54"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C54.jpg" alt="Chart V.4: Expectations about Cost of Raw Materials/Inputs and Selling Prices" title="Chart V.4: Expectations about Cost of Raw Materials/Inputs and Selling Prices"></div><br>    <a id="C55"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C55.jpg" alt="Chart V.5: CPI Inflation Expectations of Professional Forecasters" title="Chart V.5: CPI Inflation Expectations of Professional Forecasters"></div><br>    <a id="C56"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C56.jpg" alt="Chart V.6: Projection of CPI Inflation (y-o-y)" title="Chart V.6: Projection of CPI Inflation (y-o-y)"></div><br>    <table width="90%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td colspan="3" align="center" class="head"><a id="T53"></a>Table V.3: Projections - Reserve Bank and Professional Forecasters</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="3" align="right">(Per cent)</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td width="70%">&nbsp;</td>        <td width="15%" align="center" class="head">2026-27</td>        <td width="15%" align="center" class="head">2027-28</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Reserve Bank&rsquo;s Baseline Projections</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Inflation</td>        <td align="right">5.2</td>        <td align="right">5.0</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Real GDP growth</td>        <td align="right">7.1</td>        <td align="right">7.0</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Median Projections of Professional Forecasters</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>        <td align="right">&nbsp;</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Inflation, Q4 (y-o-y)</td>        <td align="right">5.5</td>        <td align="right">-</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Real GDP growth</td>        <td align="right">7.1</td>        <td align="right">7</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Gross domestic saving (per cent of GNDI)</td>        <td align="right">33.2</td>        <td align="right">33.5</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Gross capital formation (per cent of GDP)</td>        <td align="right">35</td>        <td align="right">35</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Credit growth of scheduled commercial banks</td>        <td align="right">16</td>        <td align="right">13.7</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Combined gross fiscal deficit (per cent of GDP)</td>        <td align="right">7.5</td>        <td align="right">7.3</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Central government gross fiscal deficit (per cent of GDP)</td>        <td align="right">4.5</td>        <td align="right">4.3</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Repo rate (end-period)</td>        <td align="right">5.75</td>        <td align="right">-</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Yield on 91-days treasury bills (end-period)</td>        <td align="right">5.9</td>        <td align="right">6.0</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Yield on 10-year central government securities (end-period)</td>        <td align="right">7.2</td>        <td align="right">7.0</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Overall balance of payments (US$ billion)</td>        <td align="right">90</td>        <td align="right">10</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Merchandise exports growth</td>        <td align="right">11.6</td>        <td align="right">4.9</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Merchandise imports growth</td>        <td align="right">11.7</td>        <td align="right">5.4</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>Current account balance (per cent of GDP)</td>        <td align="right">-1.2</td>        <td align="right">-1.1</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td colspan="3" align="justify">Note: GNDI: Gross National Disposable Income.<br>          Source: RBI staff estimates; and Survey of Professional Forecasters (September 2026).</td>      </tr>    </table>    <p>The baseline forecasts are subject to both upside and downside risks. Upside risks to inflation are from weather-related shocks as well as a prolonged or renewed escalation of geopolitical tensions that could disrupt energy supplies and global supply chains and keep commodity, freight and insurance costs elevated. Downside risks could emanate from de-escalation of geopolitical tensions and easing of global trade-policy uncertainty, a moderation in global growth and commodity prices, and an improvement in domestic supply conditions, particularly food supplies.</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C5_3"></a>V.3 Growth Outlook</p>    <p>Domestic economic activity remains resilient supported by private consumption and fixed investment. High-capacity utilisation, robust bank credit growth, conducive financial conditions, and the government&rsquo;s continued emphasis on infrastructure are expected to stimulate investment activity. Moreover, several measures announced in the Union Budget 2026-27 are also growth supportive. However, spillovers from heightened geopolitical tensions, along with volatility in international financial markets and trade dynamics, pose downside risks to the growth outlook.</p>    <p>Forward-looking surveys offer additional insights. The bi-monthly consumer confidence (current situation index) for urban households remained almost at a similar level in September 2026 vis-&agrave;-vis the previous round. Consumer confidence of rural households registered a decline in the latest survey round, dipping further into pessimistic zone (<a href="#C57" class="links">Chart V.7</a>).</p>    <p>Consumers&rsquo; optimism for the year ahead, measured by the future expectations index, remained in positive territory; however, it continued to decline for both urban and rural households in the recent survey round, driven mainly by lower expectations across most survey parameters (<a href="#C57" class="links">Chart V.7</a>).</p>    <a id="C57"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C57.jpg" alt="Chart V.7: Consumer Confidence" title="Chart V.7: Consumer Confidence"></div><br>    <a id="C58"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C58.jpg" alt="Chart V.8: Business Assessment and Expectations" title="Chart V.8: Business Assessment and Expectations"></div>    <p>In the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s quarterly industrial outlook survey of July-September 2026, manufacturing firms continued to stay optimistic and recorded improved sentiments of the firms from the previous quarter about the business conditions (BAI/BEI)<sup data-toggle="tooltip" title="Business Assessment Index (BAI)/Business Expectations Index (BEI) (over a range of 0 to 200) gives a snapshot of demand conditions in the manufacturing sector by combining nine parameters – (i) overall business situation, (ii) production, (iii) order books, (iv) inventory of raw material, (v) inventory of finished goods, (vi) profit margin, (vii) employment, (viii) exports and (ix) capacity utilisation. A value above 100 indicates an expansion of the overall business activity and value below 100 indicates contraction."><a href="#F59" class="links">9</a></sup> (<a href="#C58" class="links">Chart V.8a</a>). The services firms experienced some moderation in sentiments in Q3:2026-27 as compared to previous quarter, while optimism increased for infrastructure firms (<a href="#C58" class="links">Charts V.8b</a> and <a href="#C58" class="links">V.8c</a>).</p>    <p>Professional forecasters polled in the September 2026 round of the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s survey expect real GDP growth to be at 7.2 per cent during Q2:2026-27. Growth is expected at about 6.8-7.1 per cent during the subsequent four quarters (<a href="#C59" class="links">Chart V.9</a>).</p>    <p>Taking into account the baseline assumptions, survey indicators and model forecasts, real GDP growth is expected at 7.1 per cent in 2026-27 with 7.2 per cent in Q2; 6.9 per cent in Q3 and 6.8 per cent in Q4 &ndash; with risks evenly balanced around this baseline path (<a href="#C510" class="links">Chart V.10</a> and <a href="#T53" class="links">Table V.3</a>). Assuming a normal monsoon and no major exogenous or policy shocks, structural model estimates for 2027-28 place real GDP growth at 7.0 per cent.</p>    <a id="C59"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C59.jpg" alt="Chart V.9: Professional Forecasters' Projection ofReal GDP Growth" title="Chart V.9: Professional Forecasters' Projection of"></div><br>    <a id="C510"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C510.jpg" alt="Chart V.10: Projection of Growth in Real GDP (y-o-y)" title="Chart V.10: Projection of Growth in Real GDP (y-o-y)"></div>    <p>There are upside and downside risks to the baseline growth estimate. Upside risks emanate from easing of geopolitical tensions, abating of trade-related uncertainty and a broad-based revival in private investment. On the downside, spillovers from prolonged geopolitical tensions, volatility in global financial markets, shifting trade patterns, and climate-related shocks pose risks to the baseline growth path.</p>    <p class="head"><a id="C5_4"></a>V.4 Balance of Risks</p>    <p>The baseline projections of domestic growth and inflation remain conditional on key domestic and global macroeconomic assumptions that are given in <a href="#T52" class="links">Table V.2</a>. These assumptions are subject to evolving uncertainties from ongoing geopolitical tensions in West Asia, commodity price volatility, global trade fragmentation, and domestic weather-related risks. Against this backdrop, this section outlines the balance of risks surrounding the baseline projections under plausible alternative scenarios.</p>    <p class="head">(i) Global Growth Uncertainties</p>    <p>Global economic activity is navigating crosscurrents from ongoing war-induced supply shocks and a positive technology-led investment cycle. Growth momentum remains highly uneven, with advanced and emerging economies that are integrated into the artificial intelligence (AI) and semiconductor value chains experiencing technology-driven growth. In contrast, activity across energy-importing and non-tech economies are constrained by elevated energy prices, structural headwinds, and trade disruptions.</p>    <p>Downside risks to global growth dominate. Broadening geopolitical conflicts in the West Asia may continue to disrupt global supply chains and critical maritime transit routes, particularly the Strait of Hormuz. This continued escalation of geopolitical hostilities may further disrupt the commodity markets. Moreover, elevated policy uncertainty and a potential surge in trade protectionism may restrain international trade flows and investment.</p>    <p>High public debt levels in major economies leave sovereign markets exposed to financial tightening, restricting the fiscal space for productive investment. Downside risk also originate from potential sharp downside corrections in AI-related asset valuations which pose contagion risks to global financial markets.</p>    <p>On the upside, a durable resolution of geopolitical tensions, swifter normalisation in energy markets, and faster AI-driven productivity gains could lift global growth above the baseline. </p>    <a id="C511"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C511.jpg" alt="Chart V.11: Impact of Risk Scenarios on the Baseline Growth Path" title="Chart V.11: Impact of Risk Scenarios on the Baseline Growth Path"></div>    <p>If global growth turns out to be 100 bps below the baseline, domestic growth and inflation could be lower by around 30 bps and 15 bps, respectively. Conversely, if global growth is higher by 50 bps relative to the baseline, domestic growth and inflation could turn out to be higher by around 15 bps and 7 bps, respectively (<a href="#C511" class="links">Charts V.11a</a> and <a href="#C512" class="links">V.12a</a>).</p>    <p class="head">(ii) International Crude Oil Prices</p>    <p>Global crude oil markets remain volatile due to supply disruptions in the Gulf region. After prices surged following the onset of the West Asia conflict, brent crude oil scaled a peak of US$105 per barrel in late July 2026 following renewed hostilities and maritime disruptions, before easing to around US$90 per barrel by end August 2026. Prices rose to an average of US$114 during September 2026.</p>    <a id="C512"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_C512.jpg" alt="Chart V.12: Impact of Risk Scenarios on the Baseline Inflation Path" title="Chart V.12: Impact of Risk Scenarios on the Baseline Inflation Path"></div>    <p>According to the Oil Market Report by International Energy Agency (IEA), approximately 10.0 million barrels per day (mb/d) of Gulf output remained unavailable as of August 2026. The IEA projects global oil supply to contract by 5.7 mb/d, while global oil demand is expected to decline by 2.5 mb/d in 2026.</p>    <p>The primary upside risks originate from significant supply disruption due to prolonged West Asia conflict, which could threaten major energy infrastructure or further restrict critical maritime transit routes. Additionally, deeper, or prolonged production constraints by major oil-exporting nations, combined with depleted global inventory buffers, could severely tighten supply. If these shocks materialise, a sustained spike in crude oil prices would rapidly transmit to domestic inflation through higher imported fuel costs and second round effects (<a href="#B5_1" class="links">Box V.1</a>).</p>    <p>Assuming full pass-through to domestic product prices, if crude oil prices are higher by 10 per cent than the baseline, inflation could turn out to be higher by around 50 bps and growth may be lower by around 15 bps. Conversely, sharper-than-expected deceleration in global growth, an accelerated resolution to ongoing geopolitical hostilities or robust production increases from non-traditional suppliers could ease prices. If crude oil prices are lower by 10 per cent relative to the baseline, inflation could be lower by around 50 bps and GDP growth could be higher by 15 bps (<a href="#C511" class="links">Charts V.11a</a> and <a href="#C512" class="links">V.12a</a>).</p>    <table width="95%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td><p class="head" align="center"><a id="B5_1"></a>Box V.1: Crude Oil Price Shocks: Quantifying the Macroeconomic Impact on the Indian Economy</p>          <p>Being an energy importer, crude oil price dynamics remain a critical determinant of macroeconomic stability in India, exerting a profound influence on domestic price stability, external sector balances, and overall growth outlook. Global crude oil prices have experienced extreme volatility in 2026 so far, driven by the West Asian conflict and supply chain disruptions. The Indian crude oil basket spiked to around US$125 per barrel in March 2026. Prices subsequently eased to an average of US$82 per barrel in July, before rising to US$90 per barrel by August 2026. The renewed West Asia conflict has pushed up crude oil prices to $116 per barrel in September 2026.</p>          <p>Given India&rsquo;s heavy reliance on external energy sources, adverse international crude price shocks pose significant pass-through risks to headline inflation &ndash; both directly through fuel components and indirectly via generalised cost-push pressures, creating headwinds to medium-term macroeconomic stability. Furthermore, the broader global energy outlook remains clouded by several compounding factors including widespread uncertainty regarding the timeline for supply resumption and the restoration of critical shipping routes. Additionally, global strategic petroleum reserves remain low, leaving markets vulnerable to further shocks.</p>          <p>To evaluate how these price fluctuations ripple through the economy, the Quarterly Projection Model (QPM) is utilised to delineate several interconnected channels through which the increase in crude oil prices impacts the economy (John et.al., 2022, 2023).</p>          <p>An escalation in crude oil prices adversely impacts key global macroeconomic aggregates, aggravating global inflationary pressures, dampening output growth, possibly triggering higher monetary policy rates. Heightened global energy prices cause import bill to rise, putting downward pressure on the domestic currency, leading to rise in imported inflation. An immediate pass-through of international price movements to domestic energy prices exert an instantaneous upward pressure on headline inflation, capturing the direct effect. The increase in petroleum product prices triggers indirect cost push effects emanating from firms passing on higher input costs, pushing up prices of final goods and services. Concurrently, elevated energy prices compress household real disposable incomes and narrow corporate profit margins, precipitating a contraction in discretionary consumer spending and private investment. This resultant moderation in aggregate demand has a dampening effect on inflationary pressures.</p>          <a id="BC511"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_B511.jpg" alt="Chart V.1.1. Decomposition of HeadlineInflation Impact" title="Chart V.1.1. Decomposition of Headline"></div>          <p>To prevent inflation expectations from getting unhinged, the central bank could raise policy rates. This monetary tightening widens the output gap and compresses demand to bring inflation back to target.</p>          <p><a href="#BC511" class="links">Chart V.1.1</a> depicts the decomposition of impact on headline inflation due to a persistent 10 per cent shock in crude oil prices highlighting the relative strength of the channels of transmission. Higher inflation in the first year is primarily driven by the direct effects, which dissipates over the next year. In the medium term, indirect cost push effects play the prominent role. While the demand channel exerts a moderating impact on price pressures, its offsetting impact is considerably smaller than that of the cost-push channel. Furthermore, monetary policy reaction also imparts a consequential dampening effect on inflation in the medium-term.</p>          <a id="BC512"></a><div align="center"><img src="https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/images/01MPR07102026_B512.jpg" alt="Chart V.1.2. Decomposition of OutputGrowth Impact" title="Chart V.1.2. Decomposition of Output"></div>          <p>In the case of output growth, the favourable impact of exchange rate depreciation will only be evident in the first year but this impact will be more than offset by moderation in domestic demand as well as weaker global growth. The pass-through of a depreciated currency turns contractionary in the subsequent year, as higher imported costs exert pressure on macroeconomic output (<a href="#BC512" class="links">Chart V.1.2</a>).</p>          <p class="head">References:</p>          <p>John, J., Kumar, D., George, A.T., Mitra, P., Kapur, M. and Patra. M.D. (2023), &ldquo;A Recalibrated Quarterly Projection Model (QPM 2.0) for India&rdquo;, Reserve Bank of India Bulletin, February, pp.59-77.</p>          <p>John, J., Kumar, D., and Patra, M.D. (2022). Monetary Policy: Confronting Supply-driven Inflation, Reserve Bank of India Bulletin, July, pp. 97-109.</p></td>      </tr>    </table><p class="head">(iii) Exchange Rate</p><p>The Indian Rupee (INR) was supported by robust FCNR(B) deposit inflows in early September 2026, subsequently, these gains were reversed due to higher crude oil prices.</p><p>Looking ahead, the INR may face headwinds, including a robust US dollar, elevated import cost, volatile capital inflows and rise in trade policy uncertainties. Any escalation in geopolitical tensions or unexpectedly aggressive monetary tightening by major central banks may put pressure on INR. Conversely, the INR could find support from the resilience of India&rsquo;s services exports, the stability in bilateral trade frameworks and sustained foreign capital inflows.</p><p>In a scenario where the INR depreciates by 5 per cent over the baseline, inflation could be higher by around 40 bps, while GDP growth may benefit by around 25 bps through the exports channel in the short term, under normal macroeconomic conditions. In contrast, an appreciation of the INR by 5 per cent relative to the baseline would moderate both inflation and GDP growth by around 40 bps and 25 bps, respectively (<a href="#C511" class="links">Charts V.11b</a> and <a href="#C512" class="links">V.12b</a>).</p><p class="head">(iv) Food Inflation</p><p>Domestic headline CPI inflation experienced an uptick in mid-2026, driven primarily by food price pressures. Price pressures across all the classes within the food and beverage basket firmed up. Risk to agricultural crops remain in the wake of deficient and erratic monsoon experienced in 2026-27.</p><p>Food price risks remain sensitive to weather variations. Adverse impact of El Ni&ntilde;o, unseasonal rain, lower reservoir levels, localised heatwaves, higher input costs pose upside risks to food inflation, which may result in headline inflation being higher by 40 bps vis-&agrave;-vis the baseline. On the other hand, large buffer stocks, and government's various supply side measures could moderate headline inflation by around 40 bps relative to the baseline (<a href="#C511" class="links">Charts V.11b</a> and <a href="#C512" class="links">V.12b</a>).</p><p class="head"><a id="C5_5"></a>V.5 Conclusion</p><p>India&rsquo;s macroeconomic outlook continues to be supported by resilient domestic economic activity on the back of private consumption and investment. The external environment, however, remains uncertain. At the same time, improving investment outlook, bilateral trade agreements and conducive financial conditions could provide further support to economic activity. On the inflation front, the recent increase in headline inflation has been driven largely by food and fuel prices, but core inflation has also registered a pickup from its benign levels, indicating signs of greater generalisation of price pressures as evident from diffusion indices. Going forward, upside risks to the inflation outlook persist from renewed geopolitical tensions, disruptions to energy supplies and adverse weather conditions. Against this backdrop, potential second-round effects warrant stricter vigilance particularly as demand conditions remain strong. Overall, the outlook for growth and inflation will depend on how domestic demand and supply conditions evolve alongside developments in the global economy, with the balance of risks remaining contingent on these factors.</p><p>_________________________________________________________________________________</p><p class="footnote"><a id="F51"></a><sup>1</sup> World Economic Outlook (WEO) Update, International Monetary Fund (IMF), July 2026.</p>    <p class="footnote"><a id="F52"></a><sup>2</sup> The Reserve Bank&rsquo;s inflation expectations survey of households is being conducted in 19 cities since March 2021 (18 cities in the previous rounds) and the results of the September 2026 round are based on responses from 5,987 households.</p>    <p class="footnote"><a id="F53"></a><sup>3</sup> The Reserve Bank&rsquo;s rural consumer confidence survey is being conducted across all Indian states and three major UTs since September 2023 and the results of the September 2026 round are based on responses from 8,799 respondents.</p>    <p class="footnote"><a id="F54"></a><sup>4</sup> The results of the July-September 2026 round of the industrial outlook survey are based on responses from 1,224 companies.</p>    <p class="footnote"><a id="F55"></a><sup>5</sup> Based on 666 services companies and 84 infrastructure firms polled in the July-September 2026 round of the services and infrastructure outlook survey.</p>    <p class="footnote"><a id="F56"></a><sup>6</sup> Forty-seven panellists participated in the September 2026 round of the Reserve Bank&rsquo;s Survey of Professional Forecasters.</p>    <p class="footnote"><a id="F57"></a><sup>7</sup> &lsquo;Fuel&rsquo; represents two categories, viz., &lsquo;electricity, gas and other fuels&rsquo;; and &lsquo;Fuels and lubricants for personal transport equipment&rsquo; which includes &lsquo;petrol&rsquo;, &lsquo;diesel&rsquo;, and &lsquo;other natural gas (CNG)&rsquo;.</p>    <p class="footnote"><a id="F58"></a><sup>8</sup> Joice John, Deepak Kumar, Asish Thomas George, Pratik Mitra, Muneesh Kapur and Michael Debabrata Patra (2023), &ldquo;A Recalibrated Quarterly Projection Model (QPM 2.0) for India&rdquo;, Reserve Bank of India Bulletin, February, Volume LXXVII (2), pp.59-77.</p>    <p class="footnote"><a id="F59"></a><sup>9</sup> Business Assessment Index (BAI)/Business Expectations Index (BEI) (over a range of 0 to 200) gives a snapshot of demand conditions in the manufacturing sector by combining nine parameters &ndash; (i) overall business situation, (ii) production, (iii) order books, (iv) inventory of raw material, (v) inventory of finished goods, (vi) profit margin, (vii) employment, (viii) exports and (ix) capacity utilisation. A value above 100 indicates an expansion of the overall business activity and value below 100 indicates contraction.</p>    <hr>    <p align="center" class="head"><a id="A"></a>ABBREVIATIONS</p>    <table width="85%" border="0" align="center" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="1" class="tablebg">      <tr>        <td width="25%">A</td>        <td width="75%">Actuals</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>AEs</td>        <td>Advanced Economies</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>AI</td>        <td>Artificial Intelligence</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>bbl</td>        <td>Barrel</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>BE</td>        <td>Budget Estimates</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>BoP</td>        <td>Balance of Payment</td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td>bps</td>        <td>Basis Points<